60-a875-4a30-95eb-a2396f4218d3'}, 'action': 'update', 'status': 'review', 'reviewer': None, 'timestamp': '2022-02-23T08:43:14.410225Z', 'reviewtimestamp': None}}}, {'tileid': '7f386dd0-c174-4f0a-b983-2dbc37599bcc', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('4c1b42c5-09c8-451b-8fcc-cb641f420024'), 'parenttile_id': UUID('98ff691c-bec3-4751-be17-d103acd16aaa'), 'data': {'60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f79-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'ST2888985326'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': {'16': {'value': {'60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f79-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'ST2877085200'}, 'action': 'update', 'status': 'review', 'reviewer': None, 'timestamp': '2022-02-23T08:45:25.086968Z', 'reviewtimestamp': None}}}, {'tileid': 'cc00333a-aea4-4250-888f-442b16a267d4', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('4c1b42c5-09c8-451b-8fcc-cb641f420024'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f6e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': "1. Parklands & Gardens of Wales No:PGW.GT48      <br>\nAssociated with: Tredegar House (Nprn 20907).<br>\nIncludes: Gates/gate piers (Nprn 20908), Gatehouses (Nprn 301696) and Warren (Nprn 24533). <br>\nRCAHMW AP94-CS 1442-6, 1450-1<br>\nRCAHMW AP945158/65-70; 965104/69-70; 965105/42      <br>\n<br>\n2. Tredegar Park makes up the original grounds to Tredegar House, one of the most magnificent 17th century buildings in South Wales.  Important parts of its grand late l7th-century layout survive, including garden walls enclosing both `restored' and modern gardens, with a gatescreen, and avenue.  The basis for restoration of the spectacular restored `inorganic parterre' is questionable. Much of the l8th-century landscape park and later planting survives, but there are also modern gardens, some of them in the modern public park.c. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s <br>\n<br>\n3. Redevelopment of part of the formal gardens documented during RCAHMW aerial reconnaissance on 24th June 2003.<br>\nT. Driver<br>\n<br>\n4. This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Monmouthshire XXXIII, sheet  7 (1901). Its main elements on that map include parterress, smithy, walled garden, walk, deer shelter, deer park, boathouse, kennels, kitchen garden, woodland, formal garden, avenues, sundials, orangery, relict hedgelines, parkland, lodges, lawns, greenhouses, fountain, a possible second formal garden, isolated geometric copses, carriage drive, river, sunken Italianate garden, bowling green, maze and a pump.<br>\nC.S.Briggs 19.05.06<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n", '60120f70-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '7a82d020-e0f5-4459-b07f-d1e634547fa1'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': {'16': {'value': {'60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f6e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '<p>Tredegar House and its&nbsp;gardens are located in fragmented parkland on the west edge of Newport (nprn 700046). The gardens lie around the house and are important for the survival of much of their original&nbsp;layout.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>The gardens developed mainly during the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. They lie on level ground to the south-west, north-west and north-east of the house. They consist mainly of three large walled compartments and a smaller one at the north-west end, aligned on a north-west/south-east axis to the south-west of the house. The high walls are of brick and on the central axis are ornamented openings. Each compartment is laid out differently.</p>\n\n<p>The smallest, on the north-west, is now a yard devoid of garden features. The next, the Orangery Garden, has been restored (following excavation in 1991) to its early eighteenth-century formal layout of &#39;inorganic parterre&#39; with central and perimeter paths plus a small mount in the west corner. Along most of its north-east side is a brick orangery attached to the stable block (25152). The square &#39;Cedar Garden&#39; compartment lies next to the house with a wrought iron gate opening&nbsp;into the forecourt. It is largely laid out to lawn with a wide perimeter gravel path, wall borders and a large cedar tree. In the centre is the grave of the horse Sir Briggs surrounded by yew hedging. The southernmost compartment is the largest, with central axis path, grassed and planted informally with specimen trees and shrubs, and with glass houses and with workshops which include&nbsp;the potting shed&nbsp;(407444).</p>\n\n<p>To the north-west of the house is a forecourt, laid out with grass squares and wide gravel paths, enclosed on the north-west by brick walls with a pair of decorative wrought-iron gates (the Edney Gates). Beyond is an outer court enclosed by modern brick walls and the stable block (on the south-west side). In front of the house is a wide turning circle with a grass centre, with a central circular stone basin, the gravel drive enters through the Grade II Listed wrought-iron &lsquo;Ruperra&rsquo; gates. To the north-east is a small restored Italianate sunken rose garden dating from the 1920s.</p>\n\n<p>A large walled kitchen garden once existed east of the house but has now gone.</p>\n\n<p>Sources:<br />\nCadw 1994: Register of Parks and Gardens of Special Historic Interest in Wales, Gwent, 146-9 (ref: PGW(Gt)48(NPT)).<br />\nOrdnance Survey Third Edition 25-inch map, sheet: Monmouthshire XXXIII.7 (1916).&nbsp;<br />\nRCAHMW AP94-CS 1442-6, 1450-1<br />\nRCAHMW AP945158/65-70; 965104/69-70; 965105/42 &nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>1. Parklands &amp; Gardens of Wales No:PGW.GT48<br />\nAssociated with: Tredegar House (Nprn 20907).<br />\nIncludes: Gates/gate piers (Nprn 20908), Gatehouses (Nprn 301696) and Warren (Nprn 24533).<br />\n<br />\n<br />\n2. Tredegar Park makes up the original grounds to Tredegar House, one of the most magnificent 17th century buildings in South Wales. Important parts of its grand late l7th-century layout survive, including garden walls enclosing both `restored&#39; and modern gardens, with a gatescreen, and avenue. The basis for restoration of the spectacular restored `inorganic parterre&#39; is questionable. Much of the l8th-century landscape park and later planting survives, but there are also modern gardens, some of them in the modern public park.c. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s<br />\n<br />\n3. Redevelopment of part of the formal gardens documented during RCAHMW aerial reconnaissance on 24th June 2003.<br />\nT. Driver<br />\n<br />\n4. This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Monmouthshire XXXIII, sheet 7 (1901). Its main elements on that map include parterress, smithy, walled garden, walk, deer shelter, deer park, boathouse, kennels, kitchen garden, woodland, formal garden, avenues, sundials, orangery, relict hedgelines, parkland, lodges, lawns, greenhouses, fountain, a possible second formal garden, isolated geometric copses, carriage drive, river, sunken Italianate garden, bowling green, maze and a pump.<br />\nC.S.Briggs 19.05.06<br />\n<br />\n&nbsp;</p>\n', '60120f70-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '7a82d020-e0f5-4459-b07f-d1e634547fa1'}, 'action': 'update', 'status': 'review', 'reviewer': None, 'timestamp': '2022-02-23T09:19:21.827349Z', 'reviewtimestamp': None}}}, {'tileid': 'f6cea54d-e2e5-4e61-89b8-f645785c3411', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('4c1b42c5-09c8-451b-8fcc-cb641f420024'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': "WELSH COUNTRY HOMES  1 TREDEGAR HOUSE South Wales Echo 30th October 1909<br>..Tredegar Oaks are famous<br>and the grounds near the house contain many fine examples of choice trees. such as the Cedar of lebanon, the American Pine, and the Tupil Tree, while the groves of Sweet Chestnut and evergreen oak are especially worthy of remark. The river Ebbw, black with its burden of coal washings, flanks the part at its eastern end. A short walk from the house through a leafy copse takes the visitor to the edge of a beautiful lake from which the waters of the river Ebbw are rigidly excluded, for it is clear as crystaland the ? rise briskly on every hand. The shrubbery was planted about 1795, at the same time taht the lake was made.<br>---------<br>Two aerial photographs taken in 1937 over the site. Where are the originals?<br>------------<br>From Briggs 1991a<br>A century after Dineley, plans were made for Tredegar Park which would 'sweep away courtyards and stables, break..avenues and divert the turnpike road..forming a substantial lake'(Jacques 1983, 116). Although the stables were left intact, its formal garden disappeared. Now, under local authority  ownership,  a praiseworthy effort is being made to recover and restore for display features of the seventeenth century garden, by carefully excavating the parterres and walled areas (Freeman 1989). In summer 1990 excavations directed by John Phibbs re-located some of these features, turning up buried geometric parterres originally coloured by coaldust, broken brick and shells. Trawscoed, now leased to the Property Services Agency, was completely remodelled during the nineteenth century, as was Picton, still in private ownership, which, in common with Ruperra, has apparently lost its Renaissance features. Nevertheless, that it has been possible to uncover intact some parts of Tredegar's formal garden should be a salutary warning of the need to be ever watchful when later landscaping is restored or ground disturbance undertaken around any early gentry or manor houses. <br>-------------------------------------------<br>Tredegar House<br>The formal walled gardens at Tredegar House (Newport, Gwent), have long been considered 'one of the most important early eighteenth century garden landscapes in Wales'(Freeman 1992 which is the main source of the interpretative account which follows). Probably originally constituting a Tudor five-enclosure garden layout to the SW of the medieval house, in the early 1700s its garden walls were realigned and reconstructed in brick, rather than stone, to make three enclosures linked by one central gravel path running parallel to the house.<br>The largest, most southerly enclosure contained the orchard and gardener's cottage with ancillary buildings. Next came the Cedar Garden, on its present pattern  no more than  a century old; dominated by a great Cedrus Libani, framed by herbaceous borders, and carrying an obelisk horse memorial and three headstones to pet dogs. At one time, this enclosure may have been laid out in the Dutch style, but in the later eighteenth century it appears to have become an American garden (i.e.it carried some elements of American shrub flora). <br>The third enclosure, the Orangery Garden, was geophysically survey, then excavated, between 1989 and 1991 by the Debois Partnership. Apparently  the pattern of parterre uncovered was surfaced 'with different coloured materials including sea shells, crushed lime mortar, brickdust, coal dust, white sand, yellow sand, orange sand and grass, all arranged in a formal pattern and sometimes bordered with low clipped box hedging'. It should be added that until this discovery, as Freeman candidly remarks 'materials used for late seventeenth-early eighteenth century parterres have only been known from contemporary garden writers'..[but at Tredegar it was felt]..they had miraculously survived together with evidence of the garden layout'(Freeman 1992). Another source apparently indicates  that spoil excavated from a lake created during landscaping at Tredegar in 1790 was actually spread over this garden at that time, 'burying the baroque layout'(Whittle 1993,66).<br>As a direct result of the Debois Partnership investigation, commissioned to inform a process  restoring the enclosure to c 1680-1800 (but with the idea of Dutch parterres uppermost), the Authorities subsequently adopted an imaginative layout in which coloured minerals form its component pattern. As an experiment in re-creation, this was a most interesting departure; visually the result is attractive and exciting (though extremely difficult to maintain).<br>Unfortunately, critical examination of the evidence underlying the restorers' guidelines is currently hampered by absence of any published excavation report. But whilst it may seem ungracious to venture appraisal of unpublished findings, the finished restoration is in itself a convincing, if not rather  opulent statement, which has already attracted considerable acclaim from visitors and garden historians alike. This enclosure and Tredegar House's other gardens are in fact widely venerated for their historic importance and archaeological potential (Phibbs 1991). Critical comment is therefore  justified in view of the probability that future work of a similar type  may be intended there. Accordingly, whilst  access to a complete excavation archive has not been possible, the following observations are offered from an examination of the investigation accounts so far presented to Newport Borough Council (Ewart 1990;Ewart and Phibbs 1990):<br>\tFirst, the Orangery Garden, some 200 ft long by 150 ft wide, said to be one of the most important of its type and period, was investigated and stripped mainly by machine. Apparently less than three weeks' work went into this exercise, and an important part of the excavators' commission was to make only a 'watching brief' of the machine digging. At the outset,it appears that inadequate human resourcing was afforded for effective observation, recording, sampling and research on such a massive and complex site (whatever its acclaimed importance). <br>\tSecondly, without doubt a broad spectrum of earthen, clay and mineral deposits was found and described. However, these deposits occurred in contexts which hardly  recommend their unequivocal interpretation in the manner now re-created. In fact, vital contributions absent from the excavators' statement include reports from a pedologist, petrographer and sedimentologist defining or identifying key elements of the crucial stratified deposits on which the garden's history was interpreted. Equally strange is an absence of any apparent sampling strategy for pollen or plant macro-remains. <br>\tThirdly, available notes on the excavated deposits seem to offer few real clues which demonstrate convincing complete patterns of garden beds at any particular period. Indeed, two large rectangular features interpreted as having been 'beds' might equally have been lost buildings with their brick or stone  footings robbed out. In fact, overall, the site's extremely complex history was demonstrated by stratigraphic exposures in each of a dozen or so trenches (most of them 'keyhole' excavations). Although trenched to assist an understanding of the site, these appeared to raise far more questions than they answered. Fortunately, it seems that only a limited amount of topsoil was machined from the area, and much earlier stratigraphy was deliberately covered over by the garden re-creation to be left for future investigation. <br>From the documentation so far presented, it is difficult to escape the view that a dating of c 1700 afforded the eventual reconstruction (of a 'Dutch parterre'), 'the only one of its kind in Britain'(Whittle 1993,66), is in fact almost entirely conjectural. Although some of these  misgivings will no doubt be allayed by full publication of this important investigation, it is difficult to escape the view that alternative interpretations to the present re-creation may eventually present themselves when the garden's deposits are thoroughly re-examined at some future time.<br>  <br>csb 1996<br>------------------------------------------------<br>CADW/ICOMOS REGISTER OF PARKS AND GARDENS<br> OF SPECIAL HISTORIC INTEREST IN WALES<br>SITE DOSSIER<br>SITE NAME  Tredegar Park                                     <br>REF. NO.  PGW (Gt) 48     <br>OS MAP  l7l   GRID REF.  ST 290853<br>FORMER COUNTY  Gwent   UNITARY AUTHORITY  Newport B.C.   COMMUNITY COUNCIL  Duffryn<br>OWNER/S  Single/multiple         Public/private<br>Tredegar House:  Newport Borough Council<br>OCCUPIER/S<br>As above;  multiple<br>DESIGNATIONS   Listed building: Tredegar House:  grade I; Riding School and stables: grade II;  Gate piers and gates:  grade II<br>SAM (Mn 84: Tredegar Fort)   CA   TPO no. l04<br>SITE EVALUATION        Grade  II*<br>Primary reasons for grading<br>Survival of parts of grand late l7th-century layout, including garden walls, gatescreen, inorganic parterres, and avenue.<br>TYPE OF SITE<br>Restoration period formal walled gardens and part of park; late l8th-century landscape park with later planting<br>MAIN PHASES OF CONSTRUCTION<br>c. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s <br>VISITED BY/DATE  Elisabeth Whittle/August l99l; November l99l<br> HOUSE<br>Name  Tredegar House<br>Grid ref  ST 288852<br>Date/style late l5th century; c. l664 - l672/medieval manor house; Baroque<br>Brief description:  <br>Tredegar House stands on low-lying level ground west of the river Ebbw, on the western fringe of Newport. Until l95l the house was a private home, the principal residence of the Morgan family, who lived there from the early l5th century onwards. A substantial stone manor house was built on the site in the late l5th or early l6th century, of which only a small part, the south-west wing of the present house, survives.<br>\tBetween about l664 and l672 the present grand mansion was built by Sir William Morgan. It is a large two-storey square house built around a small courtyard. It is constructed of brick, with a pitched slate roof (originally higher, and surmounted by a cupola and balustrading) with attic storey. The main entrance is in the middle of the north-west side, with a fine doorway flanked by spiral twist columns supporting a broken split pediment. The elaborate stone porch on the north-east side is a l9th-century addition. The architect of the house is unknown. <br>\tApart from some internal rearrangements, the lowering of the roof, and the addition of the north-east porch, there were few subsequent alterations to the house. In l859 Sir Charles Morgan became the first Baron Tredegar. In l95l the last of the Morgans, John Morgan, sold the house to the Sisters of St Joseph for use as a Catholic girls' boarding school. In l974 house and grounds were purchased by Newport Borough Council.<br>OUTBUILDINGS<br>Name  Stable block<br>Grid ref  ST 287853<br>Date/style, and brief description<br>The stable block is a long low free-standing range lying to the north-west of the house, at right-angles to the main entrance. It is a fine building, constructed of brick, with a monumental central archway over which is a large pediment with a clock (l766). There are  Ionic brick pilasters  reaching half way up the wall, between the lower mullioned windows, above which are small oval windows. Inside, to the south of the archway are the hunting stables, and to the north is the riding school. Attached to the rear is an orangery (see Garden section). The stables probably date, on architectural grounds, to some time between c. l690 and c. l725, and were probably built by John Morgan (d. l7l9).<br>\tOther outbuildings lie to the south-east of the house, and are arranged loosely around a cobbled courtyard. They date mainly from the late l7th and l8th centuries. The Brewhouse (now restaurant) may have been part of the medieval stables, but was subsequently used as a brewhouse (until the early 20th century). On the east side of the yard are two large stone barns, the Greater Barn, and the Lesser Barn (originally one). Behind the latter is the Cattle byre (now Visitor Centre), and the Flour Mill. At right-angles to the Greater Barn was the Laundry and Dairy Block (now the school chapel for St Joseph's School).<br> THE PARK<br>Central grid ref  ST 284858<br>Date/style  Late l7th century;  late l8th century/formal; landscape<br> <br>GENERAL DESCRIPTION, HISTORY AND LAYOUT <br>Tredegar Park lies on the western edge of Newport, on low-lying ground in the Ebbw valley, and also on higher, rolling ground to the east and west. The original park is now severely truncated, built on, and transected by roads, including the M4 motorway. The main areas that remain open are to the north and north-west of the house.<br>\tThe park was first created after c. l664 by Sir William Morgan (who built the present house), and was an irregularly shaped area  to the N, S, E and W of the house, with the main ornamented area to the N and NE. Soon after it was created great axial avenues were planted, the main ones being the oak avenue running NW from the NW front of the house, and the double avenues focused on the Iron Age hillfort to the NE (Tredegar Fort), which ran SW-NE from the grounds up to the fort (a walnut avenue), and ESE-WNW from the fort towards Bassaleg church. A sweet chestnut avenue ran SE from the S gates of the walled gardens. Further avenues branched off the main ones or lined routes through the park. This layout is shown on the earliest map of the park, the survey by Robert Snell (c. l770), which also shows clumps of trees in the area to the N and NE of the house. By this time the park was about l000 acres in extent. The southern part, which is flat and low-lying, was the Home Farm, and was never ornamented, except for the chestnut avenue, and was managed mainly for sporting purposes. The part of the park to the NW of the house is referred to in the records as the 'New Park', and may have been added later than the other parts (the area to the N of the house is called the 'Old Park'). Part originally formed an earlier, medieval park, Cleppa Park, and within the Gwern-y-Cleppa wood are the ruins of a medieval house, reputedly once the home of Ivor-hael (early l4th century). Some ruined park walling remains in this part of the park.<br>\tThe next phase of development came in the l790s when the landscape designer Adam Mickle landscaped the grounds and park, removing most of the formal elements. Mickle made three main changes (although his initial plans involved even more radical change, including the demolition of the stables and removal of all but one of the walled gardens). He removed most of the avenues, leaving only the oak avenue on the N front, the chestnut avenue to the S, parts of the avenues to the hillfort, and some minor ones in the park. He built the new turnpike road through the park (now the A48), moving the old one further away from the house (to the N). This work included the screening of the road from the house and grounds by a wall and grass bank (Mickle signed the contract for the walling in l790). In conjunction with the new road Mickle realigned the main drive to its present position, and made a long sinuous lake, the 'Great Pool', to the N of the house, between the house and the road. It is dammed at its S end, and Mickle landscaped the south end  with shrubberies of holly, laurel and box (partly to screen the kitchen gardens). The water level is higher than the ground floor of the house.  <br>\tIn the late l9th century the gates and lodges were built at the entrance to the grounds, N of the house. When the M4 motorway was built, cutting the park in half, the A48 in front of the NW front of the house was realigned and the grass bank screening it was removed.<br>\tWhat remains of the park lies to the N and NW of the house, and is transected by the A48, the M4 and the A4072 roads. Mickle's lake remains, surrounded by later planting, including wellingtonias and rhododendrons planted in the l9th century, and a l930s plantation to the E of the lake. Only fragments of park planting remain to the E of the lake, most of which is taken up by office development. To the N, between the A48 and the M4, is an area of Recreation Ground, bounded on the N by the canalized river Ebbw. A few clumps of trees remain in this area. Above, to the N, is the steep-sided hill topped by the Tredegar Fort. This remains open, although modern housing estates have been built right up to the park boundary. The hill is rough grass and bracken covered, and no avenues remain. Between the M4 and the A4072 Bassaleg road the park has been converted into a golf course. A little planting remains in this area.<br>\tTo the NW of the house, NW of the M4, is the area of the 'New Park'. This is rolling agricultural land, with two areas of woodland. The only landscaping remaining in this area is the great oak avenue to the NW of the house. This begins on level ground to the S of the M4, which cuts through it, and continues NW up the hillside past Graig-y-Saeson Farm. Only a few of the trees in it are thought to be original.<br>\tThe park to the W of the house has been developed for office accommodation, and the only remnants of the park in this area are the 'Red Pond', a small  pond W of the stable block, which dates from before l770, and a stretch of avenue flanking the track leading to the 'New Park' area. To the S of the house most of the park is now covered with housing development.\t  <br>STRUCTURAL COMPONENTS<br>The present drive is that created by Adam Mickle in the l790s, when he move the  turnpike road  further away from the house. (At the same time a new approach was made to the rear of the house across the Home Farm.) The drive to the house enters the park off the A48 at ST 285858. It is gravelled, and runs parallel with the W side of the lake, curving round towards the house at the S end, where it divides, one branch to the outer court to the NW of the house, the other to the circle in front of the NE side of the house.<br>WATER FEATURES <br>The Ebbw river runs through the eastern side of the park. It is now canalized, but in the late l8th-early l9th centuries it provided a picturesque feature, with rocky banks. <br>The Red Pond (ST 286853) lies to the E of the stable block. This dates from before l775, and was formed when an embankment was built to carry the road from the house to the 'New Park'. The pond is roughly rectangular and  is naturalistic in outline.<br>Pond in the 'New Park' (ST 274851). A small artificial pond in a valley bottom, dammed along its S side. This is shown on the l770 survey map.<br>The lake, or Great Pool (ST 288855). This is a long, sinuous,  narrow artificial lake, dammed at its S end, which lies between the house and the A48 road to the N. It was created by excavation by Adam Mickle in the l790s (some of the spoil covered the Orangery Garden, see Garden Section). At the NW end, where the lake narrows, there are two islands. At the dam end there is a recently rebuilt bridge (replacing a l9th-century one) over the overflow outlet, with a rebuilt small cascade below it (possibly originally a Mickle feature). The SW edge, in front of the NE front of the house, is edged with a low brick wall topped with dressed stone, with stone plinths (with central iron pins to take ? urns) at intervals. <br>BUILT FEATURES <br>Park walls, lodges and gates<br>The park entrance (ST 285858) consists of a late l9th-century grouping of double wrought-iron gates between square stone gate piers topped by decorative stone urns. These are flanked by side pedestrian gates, railings on low brick walls, and then twin pavilion lodges. These are small square buildings of brick with stone dressings, with hipped slate roofs and central chimneys. (This entrance is now disused.) There is a short stretch of contemporary brick wall with stone capping  W of the lodges, which is continued by a modern stone wall, where the alignment of the A48 has been altered. To the E there is a similar short stretch of wall, ending in a pier topped by a ball finial. The boundary between the park and the A48 then continues as a low wall topped by iron railings.<br>\tIn the NW part of the park (the 'New Park') there are remains of the park boundary wall. It is of rubble construction, standing to an uneven height, but c. 2 m. high where complete, and runs along the S edge of Gwern-y-Cleppa wood (demolished recently at its E end, by the former lodge), westwards across a small valley, and then SSW across pasture land.<br>Boathouse<br>On the S side of the lake, near the SE end. This wooden boathouse is an early 20th-century construction.<br>Other remaining buildings in the park are of a utilitarian nature:  there is a stone barn in the 'Old Park' (ST 284865), a ruined stone barn, Cleppa Park (ST 275851), in the 'New Park', built between l770 and l827. On the S edge of Gwern-y-Cleppa wood is a former lodge (inhabited) (ST 273856).<br>\tJust N of the S end of the main drive is a stone Gorsedd Circle (20th-century).<br>PLANTED COMPONENTS <br>What remains of the park divides into five main areas:  between the house and the A48, the recreation ground, the Tredegar Fort hill, the golf course, and the western part of the park.<br>l. Between the house and the A48<br>This is a flat area to the NW and N of the house. To the NW it is grassed, with the southern end of the oak avenue between the outer court and the A48. The avenue will be taken as a whole here, including the part to the NW of the M4, the trees of which are protected under TPO no. l04 (T8 - T4l). The oaks in the avenue are of mixed age. There are three different species (Quercus robur (English oak), Q. cerris (Turkey oak), and Q. rubra (Red oak). The avenue is broken by the A48, roundabout, and M4, and continues up the slope in the NW part of the park. It will be considered here as a whole. It is a single avenue, with 49 remaining trees of varying ages and sizes. It is thought that the avenue was originally planted with Q. robur, and that there may be four of the original trees still alive. Replantings have been mainly with Q. cerris and Q. rubra, and to the S of the A48 the planting distances have been halved. <br>\tTo the NE of the southern end of the avenue the drive is flanked on its W side by a row of cedars (20th-century), with a group of wellingtonias (20th-century) at the southern end.\t<br>\tThe lake is fringed by trees all around. On the S side, opposite the NE front of the house it is lined with six large alders (Alnus cordata), which predate the building of the lake retaining wall here in the second half of the l9th century. Huge wellingtonias and pines (l9th-century) grow at regular intervals around the lake (wellingtonias at the S end, pines at the N end). The S end of the lake is well wooded, and some large hollies may be all that is left of Mickle's shrubbery. Along the E side of the lake are the remnants of a l930s plantation of conifers (mainly Sitka spruce,  Corsican pine and Scots pine), with some deciduous trees mixed in. The islands in the lake are well wooded (mainly deciduous), and there is mixed semi-natural woodland around the Red Pond.<br>2. The Recreation Ground.<br>This is a level grass area, with a childrens' playground, miniature golf course and hard tennis courts in the eastern corner. The rest of the area is laid out with playing fields. Scattered throughout are isolated deciduous trees (mostly 20th-century). There are four large clumps of oak trees, each with 5-7 trees. A clumped avenue is shown in this area (extending N into the golf-course area) on the l770 and (partly) on the l827 maps, and these clumps may be replantings of some of these clumps.<br>3. Tredegar Fort hill.<br>This hill is bracken and rough grass covered. There are no traces of the former avenues.<br>4. The golf course.<br>This is a level grass area through which winds the river Ebbw, flanked by trees. On the western edge of the area the ground begins to rise quite steeply above the river's flood plain, and this steep slope is planted with conifers, including pines, at its southern end. There is a large clump of mature trees (mainly oak, with one very large oak) near the southern end, to the E of a small watercourse. There are a few small clumps of oaks scattered throughout the area, and along the northern half of the western boundary with the A4072 road is a row of large oaks, which is all that is left of an avenue (a branch off the oak avenue NW of the house), which is shown on the l770 map.<br>5. The NW part of the park.<br>The remaining part of the park in this area, which was known as the 'New Park' is agricultural, mainly permanent pasture. There are two areas of deciduous woodland, Gwern-y-Cleppa wood (ST 273856), and the wood W of Cleppa Park (ST 273850). Some of the woodland (narrow strip E of Gwern-y-Cleppa, ref. Wl) and trees (to the S, ref. T47 - T53, and A4) are protected by TPO no. l04. One small stretch of avenue (horse chestnut) survives W of the A48, flanking the track that led from the house to the park (ST 282852, TPO no. l04, ref. A3). A few trees of it remain to the E of the A48. The oak avenue in the northern part of the park has been described in l. above. <br>BOUNDARIES <br>NE of house:  wooden fence on E boundary of wood next to lake; wall and railings on boundary with A48 road;  housing estates E of Tredegar Fort hill.<br>N of house:   roads and housing<br>NW of house:  field boundaries, including ruined walling<br>SW of house:  stone wall (recently built)<br>SE of house:  garden boundary<br>ESSENTIAL SETTING, VIEWPOINTS AND CONTINUATIONS OF FEATURES BEYOND THE PARK, EYECATCHERS ETC. <br>Essential setting: farmland to the NW of the park<br>Views: NW from the house up the oak avenue (A)<br>LAND-USE<br>Public park; recreation ground; golf course; public open space (Tredegar Fort hill); agricultural land (permanent pasture and woodland)<br>ELEMENTS OF BOTANICAL OR OTHER NATURE CONSERVATION INTEREST<br>Area around the Red Pond is being developed as an area of nature conservation interest<br>SURVIVAL OF INDIVIDUAL COMPONENTS<br>Structural components:  most<br>Water features:  all<br>Built components:  most<br>Planted components:  little<br>---------------------<br>THE PLEASURE GARDEN <br>Grid ref  ST 287852<br>Date/style  c. l670s - l920s/walled; sunken 'Italian'<br>GENERAL DESCRIPTION, HISTORY  AND LAYOUT <br>The gardens of Tredegar House lie on level ground to the SW, NW and NE of the house. They consist mainly of three large walled compartments (and a further smaller one at the NW end)  aligned on a NW-SE axis to the SW of the house. The high walls are of brick, and on the central axis are openings flanked by square piers topped by stone urn finials. Each compartment is laid out differently.  The northernmost, smaller one, is a yard devoid of garden features. The next, the Orangery Garden, is now bare, having been excavated in l99l. Along most of its NE side is a brick orangery, attached to the stable block. The next compartment, the 'Cedar Garden', lies next to the house, with a wrought iron gate through into the forecourt. It is square, and is largely laid out to lawn, with a wide perimeter gravel path, wall borders, and a large cedar tree in the lawn. In the centre is the grave of the horse Sir Briggs, surrounded by yew hedging. The southernmost compartment is the largest. The central axis is continued with a path, now grassed over. The rest of the compartment is grassed, and planted informally with  specimen trees and shrubs. At the southern end of the central axis is a gateway flanked by tall square brick piers topped by stone urn finials.<br>\t  To the NW of the house is a forecourt, laid out recently with grass squares and wide gravel paths, which is enclosed by brick walls and  on the NW side by a pair of wrought-iron gates (the Edney Gates) flanked by low walls topped by iron railings. Beyond is an outer court enclosed by modern brick walls and the stable block (on the SW side). The court is divided into quadrants by wide cobbled paths with a central oval area (recently restored).  <br>\tThe ground between the NE front of the house and the lake is largely lawn, with a screening plantation of trees and shrubs, and a low wall  along its NW side. The gravel drive enters the area through wrought iron gates flanked by square piers on the NW side. In front of the house is a wide turning circle, with a grass centre in the middle of which is a circular stone basin. To the NE of this is a small sunken rectangular garden, restored as a rose garden,  with a small Italianate 'tempietta' at the southern end.<br>\tThe earliest part of the gardens are the walled compartments to the SW of the house, and the forecourt to the NW. A l9th-century tracing by Octavius Morgan of a plan of the house and gardens as they were in the early l7th century, before William Morgan's rebuilding, shows approximately the same layout of compartments, with a forecourt and 'great bowling greene' on the NW side, a 'wilderness or maze' roughly where the Orangery garden now is, a square garden with 'the great walke about' around it, laid out with a formal parterre, roughly where the 'Cedar Garden' now is, and the southern compartment laid out as orchard. To the SW were two further large orchards. The present brick walls (except for those newly built around the outer court on the NW side) date from the late l7th or early l8th century, as do the Edney Gates, the stable block and the orangery which are incorporated into the layout. It has been discovered by excavation (De Bois Landscape Group, l99l) that the Orangery Garden was laid out, probably c. l720, with an elaborate arrangement of paths, flowerbeds and two inorganic parterres in front of the orangery.<br>\tThe next phase of development was in the late l8th century, when Adam Mickle supervised the landscaping of the park. At this stage, probably in the late l780s, a thick layer of soil from the dredging of the lake was deposited over the Orangery Garden, thus preserving the early l8th-century layout, which has now been excavated. The present layout of the Cedar Garden and the southern compartment dates largely from this period. The gardens are said to have been laid out by a Mr Walker, who was head gardener from l782 to l80l, using newly imported American species. Extensive hot-houses were built in l79l, but these have entirely disappeared, and their exact original location is not known.<br>\tAlterations were made to the NE front of the house in the l870s, with the opening up of the central door, the making of the drive, and the planting of the screen of trees. A fountain was added to the centre of the outer court in front of the stable block some time in the l9th century (now gone). The sunken Italianate garden dates from the l920s, and has been recently restored. <br>STRUCTURAL COMPONENTS <br>The Outer Court<br>This is divided in two (E-W) by the Edney Gates and gatescreen. The inner compartment has been restored with four large grass squares and wide gravel paths (similar to those in the Thomas Dineley sketch of the Outer Court, before the Edney Gates, done in l684). The outer compartment has been restored with its original layout of cobbled cross paths, a central cobbled oval, and gravel.<br>The walled gardens<br>No structural features remain visible in the northernmost compartment.<br>All the following paths and other features are thought to be of late l7th-early l8th century date. <br>\tIn the Orangery Garden, the next compartment to the south, the main structural feature visible is the central gravel path which runs NW-SE down the central axis. In the W corner there is a small mound which may be the remains of a viewing platform. In front of the orangery is a slightly raised terrace.<br>\tThe next compartment, the Cedar Garden, has a wide gravel path continuing the central axis of the walled gardens, and a wide perimeter path around the wall borders (recently restored). The central axis is continued in the southernmost compartment with a raised, grassed over path. <br>The north-east front<br>The main drive, of gravel,  enters the gardens NE of the house on their NW side. It sweeps round in a large turning circle in front of the house. The Italian Garden to the E is sunken, with stepped sides. It is divided into four by stone flagged paths.<br> <br> BUILT COMPONENTS <br>Outer Court<br>The two compartments of the Outer Court are surrounded by brick walls, the house and the stable block. The walls of the outer compartment are modern. Dividing the area in two are the 'Edney' gates and gatescreen. These were installed in l7l3-l8, built for John Morgan by th"}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': {'16': {'value': {'11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '<p>Tredegar Park makes up the original grounds to Tredegar House, one of the most magnificent 17th century buildings in South Wales. Important parts of its grand late l7th-century layout survive, including garden walls enclosing both `restored&#39; and modern gardens, with a gatescreen, and avenue. The basis for restoration of the spectacular restored `inorganic parterre&#39; is questionable. Much of the l8th-century landscape park and later planting survives, but there are also modern gardens, some of them in the modern public park.c. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s<br />\n<br />\nRedevelopment of part of the formal gardens documented during RCAHMW aerial reconnaissance on 24th June 2003.<br />\nT. Driver</p>\n\n<p>WELSH COUNTRY HOMES 1 TREDEGAR HOUSE South Wales Echo 30th October 1909<br />\n..Tredegar Oaks are famous<br />\nand the grounds near the house contain many fine examples of choice trees. such as the Cedar of lebanon, the American Pine, and the Tupil Tree, while the groves of Sweet Chestnut and evergreen oak are especially worthy of remark. The river Ebbw, black with its burden of coal washings, flanks the part at its eastern end. A short walk from the house through a leafy copse takes the visitor to the edge of a beautiful lake from which the waters of the river Ebbw are rigidly excluded, for it is clear as crystaland the ? rise briskly on every hand. The shrubbery was planted about 1795, at the same time taht the lake was made.<br />\n---------<br />\nTwo aerial photographs taken in 1937 over the site. Where are the originals?<br />\n------------<br />\nFrom Briggs 1991a<br />\nA century after Dineley, plans were made for Tredegar Park which would &#39;sweep away courtyards and stables, break..avenues and divert the turnpike road..forming a substantial lake&#39;(Jacques 1983, 116). Although the stables were left intact, its formal garden disappeared. Now, under local authority ownership, a praiseworthy effort is being made to recover and restore for display features of the seventeenth century garden, by carefully excavating the parterres and walled areas (Freeman 1989). In summer 1990 excavations directed by John Phibbs re-located some of these features, turning up buried geometric parterres originally coloured by coaldust, broken brick and shells. Trawscoed, now leased to the Property Services Agency, was completely remodelled during the nineteenth century, as was Picton, still in private ownership, which, in common with Ruperra, has apparently lost its Renaissance features. Nevertheless, that it has been possible to uncover intact some parts of Tredegar&#39;s formal garden should be a salutary warning of the need to be ever watchful when later landscaping is restored or ground disturbance undertaken around any early gentry or manor houses.<br />\n-------------------------------------------<br />\nTredegar House<br />\nThe formal walled gardens at Tredegar House (Newport, Gwent), have long been considered &#39;one of the most important early eighteenth century garden landscapes in Wales&#39;(Freeman 1992 which is the main source of the interpretative account which follows). Probably originally constituting a Tudor five-enclosure garden layout to the SW of the medieval house, in the early 1700s its garden walls were realigned and reconstructed in brick, rather than stone, to make three enclosures linked by one central gravel path running parallel to the house.<br />\nThe largest, most southerly enclosure contained the orchard and gardener&#39;s cottage with ancillary buildings. Next came the Cedar Garden, on its present pattern no more than a century old; dominated by a great Cedrus Libani, framed by herbaceous borders, and carrying an obelisk horse memorial and three headstones to pet dogs. At one time, this enclosure may have been laid out in the Dutch style, but in the later eighteenth century it appears to have become an American garden (i.e.it carried some elements of American shrub flora).<br />\nThe third enclosure, the Orangery Garden, was geophysically survey, then excavated, between 1989 and 1991 by the Debois Partnership. Apparently the pattern of parterre uncovered was surfaced &#39;with different coloured materials including sea shells, crushed lime mortar, brickdust, coal dust, white sand, yellow sand, orange sand and grass, all arranged in a formal pattern and sometimes bordered with low clipped box hedging&#39;. It should be added that until this discovery, as Freeman candidly remarks &#39;materials used for late seventeenth-early eighteenth century parterres have only been known from contemporary garden writers&#39;..[but at Tredegar it was felt]..they had miraculously survived together with evidence of the garden layout&#39;(Freeman 1992). Another source apparently indicates that spoil excavated from a lake created during landscaping at Tredegar in 1790 was actually spread over this garden at that time, &#39;burying the baroque layout&#39;(Whittle 1993,66).<br />\nAs a direct result of the Debois Partnership investigation, commissioned to inform a process restoring the enclosure to c 1680-1800 (but with the idea of Dutch parterres uppermost), the Authorities subsequently adopted an imaginative layout in which coloured minerals form its component pattern. As an experiment in re-creation, this was a most interesting departure; visually the result is attractive and exciting (though extremely difficult to maintain).<br />\nUnfortunately, critical examination of the evidence underlying the restorers&#39; guidelines is currently hampered by absence of any published excavation report. But whilst it may seem ungracious to venture appraisal of unpublished findings, the finished restoration is in itself a convincing, if not rather opulent statement, which has already attracted considerable acclaim from visitors and garden historians alike. This enclosure and Tredegar House&#39;s other gardens are in fact widely venerated for their historic importance and archaeological potential (Phibbs 1991). Critical comment is therefore justified in view of the probability that future work of a similar type may be intended there. Accordingly, whilst access to a complete excavation archive has not been possible, the following observations are offered from an examination of the investigation accounts so far presented to Newport Borough Council (Ewart 1990;Ewart and Phibbs 1990):<br />\nFirst, the Orangery Garden, some 200 ft long by 150 ft wide, said to be one of the most important of its type and period, was investigated and stripped mainly by machine. Apparently less than three weeks&#39; work went into this exercise, and an important part of the excavators&#39; commission was to make only a &#39;watching brief&#39; of the machine digging. At the outset,it appears that inadequate human resourcing was afforded for effective observation, recording, sampling and research on such a massive and complex site (whatever its acclaimed importance).<br />\nSecondly, without doubt a broad spectrum of earthen, clay and mineral deposits was found and described. However, these deposits occurred in contexts which hardly recommend their unequivocal interpretation in the manner now re-created. In fact, vital contributions absent from the excavators&#39; statement include reports from a pedologist, petrographer and sedimentologist defining or identifying key elements of the crucial stratified deposits on which the garden&#39;s history was interpreted. Equally strange is an absence of any apparent sampling strategy for pollen or plant macro-remains.<br />\nThirdly, available notes on the excavated deposits seem to offer few real clues which demonstrate convincing complete patterns of garden beds at any particular period. Indeed, two large rectangular features interpreted as having been &#39;beds&#39; might equally have been lost buildings with their brick or stone footings robbed out. In fact, overall, the site&#39;s extremely complex history was demonstrated by stratigraphic exposures in each of a dozen or so trenches (most of them &#39;keyhole&#39; excavations). Although trenched to assist an understanding of the site, these appeared to raise far more questions than they answered. Fortunately, it seems that only a limited amount of topsoil was machined from the area, and much earlier stratigraphy was deliberately covered over by the garden re-creation to be left for future investigation.<br />\nFrom the documentation so far presented, it is difficult to escape the view that a dating of c 1700 afforded the eventual reconstruction (of a &#39;Dutch parterre&#39;), &#39;the only one of its kind in Britain&#39;(Whittle 1993,66), is in fact almost entirely conjectural. Although some of these misgivings will no doubt be allayed by full publication of this important investigation, it is difficult to escape the view that alternative interpretations to the present re-creation may eventually present themselves when the garden&#39;s deposits are thoroughly re-examined at some future time.<br />\n<br />\ncsb 1996<br />\n------------------------------------------------<br />\nCADW/ICOMOS REGISTER OF PARKS AND GARDENS<br />\nOF SPECIAL HISTORIC INTEREST IN WALES<br />\nSITE DOSSIER<br />\nSITE NAME Tredegar Park<br />\nREF. NO. PGW (Gt) 48<br />\nOS MAP l7l GRID REF. ST 290853<br />\nFORMER COUNTY Gwent UNITARY AUTHORITY Newport B.C. COMMUNITY COUNCIL Duffryn<br />\nOWNER/S Single/multiple Public/private<br />\nTredegar House: Newport Borough Council<br />\nOCCUPIER/S<br />\nAs above; multiple<br />\nDESIGNATIONS Listed building: Tredegar House: grade I; Riding School and stables: grade II; Gate piers and gates: grade II<br />\nSAM (Mn 84: Tredegar Fort) CA TPO no. l04<br />\nSITE EVALUATION Grade II*<br />\nPrimary reasons for grading<br />\nSurvival of parts of grand late l7th-century layout, including garden walls, gatescreen, inorganic parterres, and avenue.<br />\nTYPE OF SITE<br />\nRestoration period formal walled gardens and part of park; late l8th-century landscape park with later planting<br />\nMAIN PHASES OF CONSTRUCTION<br />\nc. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s<br />\nVISITED BY/DATE Elisabeth Whittle/August l99l; November l99l<br />\nHOUSE<br />\nName Tredegar House<br />\nGrid ref ST 288852<br />\nDate/style late l5th century; c. l664 - l672/medieval manor house; Baroque<br />\nBrief description:<br />\nTredegar House stands on low-lying level ground west of the river Ebbw, on the western fringe of Newport. Until l95l the house was a private home, the principal residence of the Morgan family, who lived there from the early l5th century onwards. A substantial stone manor house was built on the site in the late l5th or early l6th century, of which only a small part, the south-west wing of the present house, survives.<br />\nBetween about l664 and l672 the present grand mansion was built by Sir William Morgan. It is a large two-storey square house built around a small courtyard. It is constructed of brick, with a pitched slate roof (originally higher, and surmounted by a cupola and balustrading) with attic storey. The main entrance is in the middle of the north-west side, with a fine doorway flanked by spiral twist columns supporting a broken split pediment. The elaborate stone porch on the north-east side is a l9th-century addition. The architect of the house is unknown.<br />\nApart from some internal rearrangements, the lowering of the roof, and the addition of the north-east porch, there were few subsequent alterations to the house. In l859 Sir Charles Morgan became the first Baron Tredegar. In l95l the last of the Morgans, John Morgan, sold the house to the Sisters of St Joseph for use as a Catholic girls&#39; boarding school. In l974 house and grounds were purchased by Newport Borough Council.<br />\nOUTBUILDINGS<br />\nName Stable block<br />\nGrid ref ST 287853<br />\nDate/style, and brief description<br />\nThe stable block is a long low free-standing range lying to the north-west of the house, at right-angles to the main entrance. It is a fine building, constructed of brick, with a monumental central archway over which is a large pediment with a clock (l766). There are Ionic brick pilasters reaching half way up the wall, between the lower mullioned windows, above which are small oval windows. Inside, to the south of the archway are the hunting stables, and to the north is the riding school. Attached to the rear is an orangery (see Garden section). The stables probably date, on architectural grounds, to some time between c. l690 and c. l725, and were probably built by John Morgan (d. l7l9).<br />\nOther outbuildings lie to the south-east of the house, and are arranged loosely around a cobbled courtyard. They date mainly from the late l7th and l8th centuries. The Brewhouse (now restaurant) may have been part of the medieval stables, but was subsequently used as a brewhouse (until the early 20th century). On the east side of the yard are two large stone barns, the Greater Barn, and the Lesser Barn (originally one). Behind the latter is the Cattle byre (now Visitor Centre), and the Flour Mill. At right-angles to the Greater Barn was the Laundry and Dairy Block (now the school chapel for St Joseph&#39;s School).<br />\nTHE PARK<br />\nCentral grid ref ST 284858<br />\nDate/style Late l7th century; late l8th century/formal; landscape<br />\n<br />\nGENERAL DESCRIPTION, HISTORY AND LAYOUT<br />\nTredegar Park lies on the western edge of Newport, on low-lying ground in the Ebbw valley, and also on higher, rolling ground to the east and west. The original park is now severely truncated, built on, and transected by roads, including the M4 motorway. The main areas that remain open are to the north and north-west of the house.<br />\nThe park was first created after c. l664 by Sir William Morgan (who built the present house), and was an irregularly shaped area to the N, S, E and W of the house, with the main ornamented area to the N and NE. Soon after it was created great axial avenues were planted, the main ones being the oak avenue running NW from the NW front of the house, and the double avenues focused on the Iron Age hillfort to the NE (Tredegar Fort), which ran SW-NE from the grounds up to the fort (a walnut avenue), and ESE-WNW from the fort towards Bassaleg church. A sweet chestnut avenue ran SE from the S gates of the walled gardens. Further avenues branched off the main ones or lined routes through the park. This layout is shown on the earliest map of the park, the survey by Robert Snell (c. l770), which also shows clumps of trees in the area to the N and NE of the house. By this time the park was about l000 acres in extent. The southern part, which is flat and low-lying, was the Home Farm, and was never ornamented, except for the chestnut avenue, and was managed mainly for sporting purposes. The part of the park to the NW of the house is referred to in the records as the &#39;New Park&#39;, and may have been added later than the other parts (the area to the N of the house is called the &#39;Old Park&#39;). Part originally formed an earlier, medieval park, Cleppa Park, and within the Gwern-y-Cleppa wood are the ruins of a medieval house, reputedly once the home of Ivor-hael (early l4th century). Some ruined park walling remains in this part of the park.<br />\nThe next phase of development came in the l790s when the landscape designer Adam Mickle landscaped the grounds and park, removing most of the formal elements. Mickle made three main changes (although his initial plans involved even more radical change, including the demolition of the stables and removal of all but one of the walled gardens). He removed most of the avenues, leaving only the oak avenue on the N front, the chestnut avenue to the S, parts of the avenues to the hillfort, and some minor ones in the park. He built the new turnpike road through the park (now the A48), moving the old one further away from the house (to the N). This work included the screening of the road from the house and grounds by a wall and grass bank (Mickle signed the contract for the walling in l790). In conjunction with the new road Mickle realigned the main drive to its present position, and made a long sinuous lake, the &#39;Great Pool&#39;, to the N of the house, between the house and the road. It is dammed at its S end, and Mickle landscaped the south end with shrubberies of holly, laurel and box (partly to screen the kitchen gardens). The water level is higher than the ground floor of the house.<br />\nIn the late l9th century the gates and lodges were built at the entrance to the grounds, N of the house. When the M4 motorway was built, cutting the park in half, the A48 in front of the NW front of the house was realigned and the grass bank screening it was removed.<br />\nWhat remains of the park lies to the N and NW of the house, and is transected by the A48, the M4 and the A4072 roads. Mickle&#39;s lake remains, surrounded by later planting, including wellingtonias and rhododendrons planted in the l9th century, and a l930s plantation to the E of the lake. Only fragments of park planting remain to the E of the lake, most of which is taken up by office development. To the N, between the A48 and the M4, is an area of Recreation Ground, bounded on the N by the canalized river Ebbw. A few clumps of trees remain in this area. Above, to the N, is the steep-sided hill topped by the Tredegar Fort. This remains open, although modern housing estates have been built right up to the park boundary. The hill is rough grass and bracken covered, and no avenues remain. Between the M4 and the A4072 Bassaleg road the park has been converted into a golf course. A little planting remains in this area.<br />\nTo the NW of the house, NW of the M4, is the area of the &#39;New Park&#39;. This is rolling agricultural land, with two areas of woodland. The only landscaping remaining in this area is the great oak avenue to the NW of the house. This begins on level ground to the S of the M4, which cuts through it, and continues NW up the hillside past Graig-y-Saeson Farm. Only a few of the trees in it are thought to be original.<br />\nThe park to the W of the house has been developed for office accommodation, and the only remnants of the park in this area are the &#39;Red Pond&#39;, a small pond W of the stable block, which dates from before l770, and a stretch of avenue flanking the track leading to the &#39;New Park&#39; area. To the S of the house most of the park is now covered with housing development.<br />\nSTRUCTURAL COMPONENTS<br />\nThe present drive is that created by Adam Mickle in the l790s, when he move the turnpike road further away from the house. (At the same time a new approach was made to the rear of the house across the Home Farm.) The drive to the house enters the park off the A48 at ST 285858. It is gravelled, and runs parallel with the W side of the lake, curving round towards the house at the S end, where it divides, one branch to the outer court to the NW of the house, the other to the circle in front of the NE side of the house.<br />\nWATER FEATURES<br />\nThe Ebbw river runs through the eastern side of the park. It is now canalized, but in the late l8th-early l9th centuries it provided a picturesque feature, with rocky banks.<br />\nThe Red Pond (ST 286853) lies to the E of the stable block. This dates from before l775, and was formed when an embankment was built to carry the road from the house to the &#39;New Park&#39;. The pond is roughly rectangular and is naturalistic in outline.<br />\nPond in the &#39;New Park&#39; (ST 274851). A small artificial pond in a valley bottom, dammed along its S side. This is shown on the l770 survey map.<br />\nThe lake, or Great Pool (ST 288855). This is a long, sinuous, narrow artificial lake, dammed at its S end, which lies between the house and the A48 road to the N. It was created by excavation by Adam Mickle in the l790s (some of the spoil covered the Orangery Garden, see Garden Section). At the NW end, where the lake narrows, there are two islands. At the dam end there is a recently rebuilt bridge (replacing a l9th-century one) over the overflow outlet, with a rebuilt small cascade below it (possibly originally a Mickle feature). The SW edge, in front of the NE front of the house, is edged with a low brick wall topped with dressed stone, with stone plinths (with central iron pins to take ? urns) at intervals.<br />\nBUILT FEATURES<br />\nPark walls, lodges and gates<br />\nThe park entrance (ST 285858) consists of a late l9th-century grouping of double wrought-iron gates between square stone gate piers topped by decorative stone urns. These are flanked by side pedestrian gates, railings on low brick walls, and then twin pavilion lodges. These are small square buildings of brick with stone dressings, with hipped slate roofs and central chimneys. (This entrance is now disused.) There is a short stretch of contemporary brick wall with stone capping W of the lodges, which is continued by a modern stone wall, where the alignment of the A48 has been altered. To the E there is a similar short stretch of wall, ending in a pier topped by a ball finial. The boundary between the park and the A48 then continues as a low wall topped by iron railings.<br />\nIn the NW part of the park (the &#39;New Park&#39;) there are remains of the park boundary wall. It is of rubble construction, standing to an uneven height, but c. 2 m. high where complete, and runs along the S edge of Gwern-y-Cleppa wood (demolished recently at its E end, by the former lodge), westwards across a small valley, and then SSW across pasture land.<br />\nBoathouse<br />\nOn the S side of the lake, near the SE end. This wooden boathouse is an early 20th-century construction.<br />\nOther remaining buildings in the park are of a utilitarian nature: there is a stone barn in the &#39;Old Park&#39; (ST 284865), a ruined stone barn, Cleppa Park (ST 275851), in the &#39;New Park&#39;, built between l770 and l827. On the S edge of Gwern-y-Cleppa wood is a former lodge (inhabited) (ST 273856).<br />\nJust N of the S end of the main drive is a stone Gorsedd Circle (20th-century).<br />\nPLANTED COMPONENTS<br />\nWhat remains of the park divides into five main areas: between the house and the A48, the recreation ground, the Tredegar Fort hill, the golf course, and the western part of the park.<br />\nl. Between the house and the A48<br />\nThis is a flat area to the NW and N of the house. To the NW it is grassed, with the southern end of the oak avenue between the outer court and the A48. The avenue will be taken as a whole here, including the part to the NW of the M4, the trees of which are protected under TPO no. l04 (T8 - T4l). The oaks in the avenue are of mixed age. There are three different species (Quercus robur (English oak), Q. cerris (Turkey oak), and Q. rubra (Red oak). The avenue is broken by the A48, roundabout, and M4, and continues up the slope in the NW part of the park. It will be considered here as a whole. It is a single avenue, with 49 remaining trees of varying ages and sizes. It is thought that the avenue was originally planted with Q. robur, and that there may be four of the original trees still alive. Replantings have been mainly with Q. cerris and Q. rubra, and to the S of the A48 the planting distances have been halved.<br />\nTo the NE of the southern end of the avenue the drive is flanked on its W side by a row of cedars (20th-century), with a group of wellingtonias (20th-century) at the southern end.<br />\nThe lake is fringed by trees all around. On the S side, opposite the NE front of the house it is lined with six large alders (Alnus cordata), which predate the building of the lake retaining wall here in the second half of the l9th century. Huge wellingtonias and pines (l9th-century) grow at regular intervals around the lake (wellingtonias at the S end, pines at the N end). The S end of the lake is well wooded, and some large hollies may be all that is left of Mickle&#39;s shrubbery. Along the E side of the lake are the remnants of a l930s plantation of conifers (mainly Sitka spruce, Corsican pine and Scots pine), with some deciduous trees mixed in. The islands in the lake are well wooded (mainly deciduous), and there is mixed semi-natural woodland around the Red Pond.<br />\n2. The Recreation Ground.<br />\nThis is a level grass area, with a childrens&#39; playground, miniature golf course and hard tennis courts in the eastern corner. The rest of the area is laid out with playing fields. Scattered throughout are isolated deciduous trees (mostly 20th-century). There are four large clumps of oak trees, each with 5-7 trees. A clumped avenue is shown in this area (extending N into the golf-course area) on the l770 and (partly) on the l827 maps, and these clumps may be replantings of some of these clumps.<br />\n3. Tredegar Fort hill.<br />\nThis hill is bracken and rough grass covered. There are no traces of the former avenues.<br />\n4. The golf course.<br />\nThis is a level grass area through which winds the river Ebbw, flanked by trees. On the western edge of the area the ground begins to rise quite steeply above the river&#39;s flood plain, and this steep slope is planted with conifers, including pines, at its southern end. There is a large clump of mature trees (mainly oak, with one very large oak) near the southern end, to the E of a small watercourse. There are a few small clumps of oaks scattered throughout the area, and along the northern half of the western boundary with the A4072 road is a row of large oaks, which is all that is left of an avenue (a branch off the oak avenue NW of the house), which is shown on the l770 map.<br />\n5. The NW part of the park.<br />\nThe remaining part of the park in this area, which was known as the &#39;New Park&#39; is agricultural, mainly permanent pasture. There are two areas of deciduous woodland, Gwern-y-Cleppa wood (ST 273856), and the wood W of Cleppa Park (ST 273850). Some of the woodland (narrow strip E of Gwern-y-Cleppa, ref. Wl) and trees (to the S, ref. T47 - T53, and A4) are protected by TPO no. l04. One small stretch of avenue (horse chestnut) survives W of the A48, flanking the track that led from the house to the park (ST 282852, TPO no. l04, ref. A3). A few trees of it remain to the E of the A48. The oak avenue in the northern part of the park has been described in l. above.<br />\nBOUNDARIES<br />\nNE of house: wooden fence on E boundary of wood next to lake; wall and railings on boundary with A48 road; housing estates E of Tredegar Fort hill.<br />\nN of house: roads and housing<br />\nNW of house: field boundaries, including ruined walling<br />\nSW of house: stone wall (recently built)<br />\nSE of house: garden boundary<br />\nESSENTIAL SETTING, VIEWPOINTS AND CONTINUATIONS OF FEATURES BEYOND THE PARK, EYECATCHERS ETC.<br />\nEssential setting: farmland to the NW of the park<br />\nViews: NW from the house up the oak avenue (A)<br />\nLAND-USE<br />\nPublic park; recreation ground; golf course; public open space (Tredegar Fort hill); agricultural land (permanent pasture and woodland)<br />\nELEMENTS OF BOTANICAL OR OTHER NATURE CONSERVATION INTEREST<br />\nArea around the Red Pond is being developed as an area of nature conservation interest<br />\nSURVIVAL OF INDIVIDUAL COMPONENTS<br />\nStructural components: most<br />\nWater features: all<br />\nBuilt components: most<br />\nPlanted components: little<br />\n---------------------<br />\nTHE PLEASURE GARDEN<br />\nGrid ref ST 287852<br />\nDate/style c. l670s - l920s/walled; sunken &#39;Italian&#39;<br />\nGENERAL DESCRIPTION, HISTORY AND LAYOUT<br />\nThe gardens of Tredegar House lie on level ground to the SW, NW and NE of the house. They consist mainly of three large walled compartments (and a further smaller one at the NW end) aligned on a NW-SE axis to the SW of the house. The high walls are of brick, and on the central axis are openings flanked by square piers topped by stone urn finials. Each compartment is laid out differently. The northernmost, smaller one, is a yard devoid of garden features. The next, the Orangery Garden, is now bare, having been excavated in l99l. Along most of its NE side is a brick orangery, attached to the stable block. The next compartment, the &#39;Cedar Garden&#39;, lies next to the house, with a wrought iron gate through into the forecourt. It is square, and is largely laid out to lawn, with a wide perimeter gravel path, wall borders, and a large cedar tree in the lawn. In the centre is the grave of the horse Sir Briggs, surrounded by yew hedging. The southernmost compartment is the largest. The central axis is continued with a path, now grassed over. The rest of the compartment is grassed, and planted informally with specimen trees and shrubs. At the southern end of the central axis is a gateway flanked by tall square brick piers topped by stone urn finials.<br />\nTo the NW of the house is a forecourt, laid out recently with grass squares and wide gravel paths, which is enclosed by brick walls and on the NW side by a pair of wrought-iron gates (the Edney Gates) flanked by low walls topped by iron railings. Beyond is an outer court enclosed by modern brick walls and the stable block (on the SW side). The court is divided into quadrants by wide cobbled paths with a central oval area (recently restored).<br />\nThe ground between the NE front of the house and the lake is largely lawn, with a screening plantation of trees and shrubs, and a low wall along its NW side. The gravel drive enters the area through wrought iron gates flanked by square piers on the NW side. In front of the house is a wide turning circle, with a grass centre in the middle of which is a circular stone basin. To the NE of this is a small sunken rectangular garden, restored as a rose garden, with a small Italianate &#39;tempietta&#39; at the southern end.<br />\nThe earliest part of the gardens are the walled compartments to the SW of the house, and the forecourt to the NW. A l9th-century tracing by Octavius Morgan of a plan of the house and gardens as they were in the early l7th century, before William Morgan&#39;s rebuilding, shows approximately the same layout of compartments, with a forecourt and &#39;great bowling greene&#39; on the NW side, a &#39;wilderness or maze&#39; roughly where the Orangery garden now is, a square garden with &#39;the great walke about&#39; around it, laid out with a formal parterre, roughly where the &#39;Cedar Garden&#39; now is, and the southern compartment laid out as orchard. To the SW were two further large orchards. The present brick walls (except for those newly built around the outer court on the NW side) date from the late l7th or early l8th century, as do the Edney Gates, the stable block and the orangery which are incorporated into the layout. It has been discovered by excavation (De Bois Landscape Group, l99l) that the Orangery Garden was laid out, probably c. l720, with an elaborate arrangement of paths, flowerbeds and two inorganic parterres in front of the orangery.<br />\nThe next phase of development was in the late l8th century, when Adam Mickle supervised the landscaping of the park. At this stage, probably in the late l780s, a thick layer of soil from the dredging of the lake was deposited over the Orangery Garden, thus preserving the early l8th-century layout, which has now been excavated. The present layout of the Cedar Garden and the southern compartment dates largely from this period. The gardens are said to have been laid out by a Mr Walker, who was head gardener from l782 to l80l, using newly imported American species. Extensive hot-houses were built in l79l, but these have entirely disappeared, and their exact original location is not known.<br />\nAlterations were made to the NE front of the house in the l870s, with the opening up of the central door, the making of the drive, and the planting of the screen of trees. A fountain was added to the centre of the outer court in front of the stable block some time in the l9th century (now gone). The sunken Italianate garden dates from the l920s, and has been recently restored.<br />\nSTRUCTURAL COMPONENTS<br />\nThe Outer Court<br />\nThis is divided in two (E-W) by the Edney Gates and gatescreen. The inner compartment has been restored with four large grass squares and wide gravel paths (similar to those in the Thomas Dineley sketch of the Outer Court, before the Edney Gates, done in l684). The outer compartment has been restored with its original layout of cobbled cross paths, a central cobbled oval, and gravel.<br />\nThe walled gardens<br />\nNo structural features remain visible in the northernmost compartment.<br />\nAll the following paths and other features are thought to be of late l7th-early l8th century date.<br />\nIn the Orangery Garden, the next compartment to the south, the main structural feature visible is the central gravel path which runs NW-SE down the central axis. In the W corner there is a small mound which may be the remains of a viewing platform. In front of the orangery is a slightly raised terrace.<br />\nThe next compartment, the Cedar Garden, has a wide gravel path continuing the central axis of the walled gardens, and a wide perimeter path around the wall borders (recently restored). The central axis is continued in the southernmost compartment with a raised, grassed over path.<br />\nThe north-east front<br />\nThe main drive, of gravel, enters the gardens NE of the house on their NW side. It sweeps round in a large turning circle in front of the house. The Italian Garden to the E is sunken, with stepped sides. It is divided into four by stone flagged paths.<br />\n<br />\nBUILT COMPONENTS<br />\nOuter Court<br />\nThe two compartments of the Outer Court are surrounded by brick walls, the house and the stable block. The walls of the outer compartment are modern. Dividing the area in two are the &#39;Edney&#39; gates and gatescreen. These were installed in l7l3-l8, built for John Morgan by th</p>\n'}, 'action': 'update', 'status': 'review', 'reviewer': None, 'timestamp': '2022-02-23T09:20:53.443160Z', 'reviewtimestamp': None}}}], 'permissions': {'users_without_read_perm': [], 'users_without_edit_perm': [], 'users_without_delete_perm': [], 'users_with_no_access': []}, 'strings': [{'string': 'Coedkernew', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f3d-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Monmouthshire', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f61-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Newport', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f46-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'ST2885', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'ST28NE', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Grid reference taken at a central point within extensive site or complex', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'COUNTRY HOUSE GARDEN', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No Preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '10G', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f55-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f55-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'PGW(Gt)48', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'PGW2*', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Cadw', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Complex', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Subsequent Use', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Part of', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Associated with', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Part of', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Part of', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'NG', 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'CHN', 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'NG', 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'DKL', 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'Post Medieval', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No Preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '17th Century', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'No Preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'Tredegar Park, Garden, Newport', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Tredegar House Garden, Newport', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'Preferred English', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'ST2888985326', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'}, {'string': 'ST2877085200', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'}, {'string': "1. Parklands & Gardens of Wales No:PGW.GT48      <br>\nAssociated with: Tredegar House (Nprn 20907).<br>\nIncludes: Gates/gate piers (Nprn 20908), Gatehouses (Nprn 301696) and Warren (Nprn 24533). <br>\nRCAHMW AP94-CS 1442-6, 1450-1<br>\nRCAHMW AP945158/65-70; 965104/69-70; 965105/42      <br>\n<br>\n2. Tredegar Park makes up the original grounds to Tredegar House, one of the most magnificent 17th century buildings in South Wales.  Important parts of its grand late l7th-century layout survive, including garden walls enclosing both `restored' and modern gardens, with a gatescreen, and avenue.  The basis for restoration of the spectacular restored `inorganic parterre' is questionable. Much of the l8th-century landscape park and later planting survives, but there are also modern gardens, some of them in the modern public park.c. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s <br>\n<br>\n3. Redevelopment of part of the formal gardens documented during RCAHMW aerial reconnaissance on 24th June 2003.<br>\nT. Driver<br>\n<br>\n4. This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Monmouthshire XXXIII, sheet  7 (1901). Its main elements on that map include parterress, smithy, walled garden, walk, deer shelter, deer park, boathouse, kennels, kitchen garden, woodland, formal garden, avenues, sundials, orangery, relict hedgelines, parkland, lodges, lawns, greenhouses, fountain, a possible second formal garden, isolated geometric copses, carriage drive, river, sunken Italianate garden, bowling green, maze and a pump.<br>\nC.S.Briggs 19.05.06<br>\n<br>\n<br>\n", 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'English Description', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '<p>Tredegar House and its&nbsp;gardens are located in fragmented parkland on the west edge of Newport (nprn 700046). The gardens lie around the house and are important for the survival of much of their original&nbsp;layout.&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>The gardens developed mainly during the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. They lie on level ground to the south-west, north-west and north-east of the house. They consist mainly of three large walled compartments and a smaller one at the north-west end, aligned on a north-west/south-east axis to the south-west of the house. The high walls are of brick and on the central axis are ornamented openings. Each compartment is laid out differently.</p>\n\n<p>The smallest, on the north-west, is now a yard devoid of garden features. The next, the Orangery Garden, has been restored (following excavation in 1991) to its early eighteenth-century formal layout of &#39;inorganic parterre&#39; with central and perimeter paths plus a small mount in the west corner. Along most of its north-east side is a brick orangery attached to the stable block (25152). The square &#39;Cedar Garden&#39; compartment lies next to the house with a wrought iron gate opening&nbsp;into the forecourt. It is largely laid out to lawn with a wide perimeter gravel path, wall borders and a large cedar tree. In the centre is the grave of the horse Sir Briggs surrounded by yew hedging. The southernmost compartment is the largest, with central axis path, grassed and planted informally with specimen trees and shrubs, and with glass houses and with workshops which include&nbsp;the potting shed&nbsp;(407444).</p>\n\n<p>To the north-west of the house is a forecourt, laid out with grass squares and wide gravel paths, enclosed on the north-west by brick walls with a pair of decorative wrought-iron gates (the Edney Gates). Beyond is an outer court enclosed by modern brick walls and the stable block (on the south-west side). In front of the house is a wide turning circle with a grass centre, with a central circular stone basin, the gravel drive enters through the Grade II Listed wrought-iron &lsquo;Ruperra&rsquo; gates. To the north-east is a small restored Italianate sunken rose garden dating from the 1920s.</p>\n\n<p>A large walled kitchen garden once existed east of the house but has now gone.</p>\n\n<p>Sources:<br />\nCadw 1994: Register of Parks and Gardens of Special Historic Interest in Wales, Gwent, 146-9 (ref: PGW(Gt)48(NPT)).<br />\nOrdnance Survey Third Edition 25-inch map, sheet: Monmouthshire XXXIII.7 (1916).&nbsp;<br />\nRCAHMW AP94-CS 1442-6, 1450-1<br />\nRCAHMW AP945158/65-70; 965104/69-70; 965105/42 &nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>1. Parklands &amp; Gardens of Wales No:PGW.GT48<br />\nAssociated with: Tredegar House (Nprn 20907).<br />\nIncludes: Gates/gate piers (Nprn 20908), Gatehouses (Nprn 301696) and Warren (Nprn 24533).<br />\n<br />\n<br />\n2. Tredegar Park makes up the original grounds to Tredegar House, one of the most magnificent 17th century buildings in South Wales. Important parts of its grand late l7th-century layout survive, including garden walls enclosing both `restored&#39; and modern gardens, with a gatescreen, and avenue. The basis for restoration of the spectacular restored `inorganic parterre&#39; is questionable. Much of the l8th-century landscape park and later planting survives, but there are also modern gardens, some of them in the modern public park.c. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s<br />\n<br />\n3. Redevelopment of part of the formal gardens documented during RCAHMW aerial reconnaissance on 24th June 2003.<br />\nT. Driver<br />\n<br />\n4. This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Monmouthshire XXXIII, sheet 7 (1901). Its main elements on that map include parterress, smithy, walled garden, walk, deer shelter, deer park, boathouse, kennels, kitchen garden, woodland, formal garden, avenues, sundials, orangery, relict hedgelines, parkland, lodges, lawns, greenhouses, fountain, a possible second formal garden, isolated geometric copses, carriage drive, river, sunken Italianate garden, bowling green, maze and a pump.<br />\nC.S.Briggs 19.05.06<br />\n<br />\n&nbsp;</p>\n', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'English Description', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': "WELSH COUNTRY HOMES  1 TREDEGAR HOUSE South Wales Echo 30th October 1909<br>..Tredegar Oaks are famous<br>and the grounds near the house contain many fine examples of choice trees. such as the Cedar of lebanon, the American Pine, and the Tupil Tree, while the groves of Sweet Chestnut and evergreen oak are especially worthy of remark. The river Ebbw, black with its burden of coal washings, flanks the part at its eastern end. A short walk from the house through a leafy copse takes the visitor to the edge of a beautiful lake from which the waters of the river Ebbw are rigidly excluded, for it is clear as crystaland the ? rise briskly on every hand. The shrubbery was planted about 1795, at the same time taht the lake was made.<br>---------<br>Two aerial photographs taken in 1937 over the site. Where are the originals?<br>------------<br>From Briggs 1991a<br>A century after Dineley, plans were made for Tredegar Park which would 'sweep away courtyards and stables, break..avenues and divert the turnpike road..forming a substantial lake'(Jacques 1983, 116). Although the stables were left intact, its formal garden disappeared. Now, under local authority  ownership,  a praiseworthy effort is being made to recover and restore for display features of the seventeenth century garden, by carefully excavating the parterres and walled areas (Freeman 1989). In summer 1990 excavations directed by John Phibbs re-located some of these features, turning up buried geometric parterres originally coloured by coaldust, broken brick and shells. Trawscoed, now leased to the Property Services Agency, was completely remodelled during the nineteenth century, as was Picton, still in private ownership, which, in common with Ruperra, has apparently lost its Renaissance features. Nevertheless, that it has been possible to uncover intact some parts of Tredegar's formal garden should be a salutary warning of the need to be ever watchful when later landscaping is restored or ground disturbance undertaken around any early gentry or manor houses. <br>-------------------------------------------<br>Tredegar House<br>The formal walled gardens at Tredegar House (Newport, Gwent), have long been considered 'one of the most important early eighteenth century garden landscapes in Wales'(Freeman 1992 which is the main source of the interpretative account which follows). Probably originally constituting a Tudor five-enclosure garden layout to the SW of the medieval house, in the early 1700s its garden walls were realigned and reconstructed in brick, rather than stone, to make three enclosures linked by one central gravel path running parallel to the house.<br>The largest, most southerly enclosure contained the orchard and gardener's cottage with ancillary buildings. Next came the Cedar Garden, on its present pattern  no more than  a century old; dominated by a great Cedrus Libani, framed by herbaceous borders, and carrying an obelisk horse memorial and three headstones to pet dogs. At one time, this enclosure may have been laid out in the Dutch style, but in the later eighteenth century it appears to have become an American garden (i.e.it carried some elements of American shrub flora). <br>The third enclosure, the Orangery Garden, was geophysically survey, then excavated, between 1989 and 1991 by the Debois Partnership. Apparently  the pattern of parterre uncovered was surfaced 'with different coloured materials including sea shells, crushed lime mortar, brickdust, coal dust, white sand, yellow sand, orange sand and grass, all arranged in a formal pattern and sometimes bordered with low clipped box hedging'. It should be added that until this discovery, as Freeman candidly remarks 'materials used for late seventeenth-early eighteenth century parterres have only been known from contemporary garden writers'..[but at Tredegar it was felt]..they had miraculously survived together with evidence of the garden layout'(Freeman 1992). Another source apparently indicates  that spoil excavated from a lake created during landscaping at Tredegar in 1790 was actually spread over this garden at that time, 'burying the baroque layout'(Whittle 1993,66).<br>As a direct result of the Debois Partnership investigation, commissioned to inform a process  restoring the enclosure to c 1680-1800 (but with the idea of Dutch parterres uppermost), the Authorities subsequently adopted an imaginative layout in which coloured minerals form its component pattern. As an experiment in re-creation, this was a most interesting departure; visually the result is attractive and exciting (though extremely difficult to maintain).<br>Unfortunately, critical examination of the evidence underlying the restorers' guidelines is currently hampered by absence of any published excavation report. But whilst it may seem ungracious to venture appraisal of unpublished findings, the finished restoration is in itself a convincing, if not rather  opulent statement, which has already attracted considerable acclaim from visitors and garden historians alike. This enclosure and Tredegar House's other gardens are in fact widely venerated for their historic importance and archaeological potential (Phibbs 1991). Critical comment is therefore  justified in view of the probability that future work of a similar type  may be intended there. Accordingly, whilst  access to a complete excavation archive has not been possible, the following observations are offered from an examination of the investigation accounts so far presented to Newport Borough Council (Ewart 1990;Ewart and Phibbs 1990):<br>\tFirst, the Orangery Garden, some 200 ft long by 150 ft wide, said to be one of the most important of its type and period, was investigated and stripped mainly by machine. Apparently less than three weeks' work went into this exercise, and an important part of the excavators' commission was to make only a 'watching brief' of the machine digging. At the outset,it appears that inadequate human resourcing was afforded for effective observation, recording, sampling and research on such a massive and complex site (whatever its acclaimed importance). <br>\tSecondly, without doubt a broad spectrum of earthen, clay and mineral deposits was found and described. However, these deposits occurred in contexts which hardly  recommend their unequivocal interpretation in the manner now re-created. In fact, vital contributions absent from the excavators' statement include reports from a pedologist, petrographer and sedimentologist defining or identifying key elements of the crucial stratified deposits on which the garden's history was interpreted. Equally strange is an absence of any apparent sampling strategy for pollen or plant macro-remains. <br>\tThirdly, available notes on the excavated deposits seem to offer few real clues which demonstrate convincing complete patterns of garden beds at any particular period. Indeed, two large rectangular features interpreted as having been 'beds' might equally have been lost buildings with their brick or stone  footings robbed out. In fact, overall, the site's extremely complex history was demonstrated by stratigraphic exposures in each of a dozen or so trenches (most of them 'keyhole' excavations). Although trenched to assist an understanding of the site, these appeared to raise far more questions than they answered. Fortunately, it seems that only a limited amount of topsoil was machined from the area, and much earlier stratigraphy was deliberately covered over by the garden re-creation to be left for future investigation. <br>From the documentation so far presented, it is difficult to escape the view that a dating of c 1700 afforded the eventual reconstruction (of a 'Dutch parterre'), 'the only one of its kind in Britain'(Whittle 1993,66), is in fact almost entirely conjectural. Although some of these  misgivings will no doubt be allayed by full publication of this important investigation, it is difficult to escape the view that alternative interpretations to the present re-creation may eventually present themselves when the garden's deposits are thoroughly re-examined at some future time.<br>  <br>csb 1996<br>------------------------------------------------<br>CADW/ICOMOS REGISTER OF PARKS AND GARDENS<br> OF SPECIAL HISTORIC INTEREST IN WALES<br>SITE DOSSIER<br>SITE NAME  Tredegar Park                                     <br>REF. NO.  PGW (Gt) 48     <br>OS MAP  l7l   GRID REF.  ST 290853<br>FORMER COUNTY  Gwent   UNITARY AUTHORITY  Newport B.C.   COMMUNITY COUNCIL  Duffryn<br>OWNER/S  Single/multiple         Public/private<br>Tredegar House:  Newport Borough Council<br>OCCUPIER/S<br>As above;  multiple<br>DESIGNATIONS   Listed building: Tredegar House:  grade I; Riding School and stables: grade II;  Gate piers and gates:  grade II<br>SAM (Mn 84: Tredegar Fort)   CA   TPO no. l04<br>SITE EVALUATION        Grade  II*<br>Primary reasons for grading<br>Survival of parts of grand late l7th-century layout, including garden walls, gatescreen, inorganic parterres, and avenue.<br>TYPE OF SITE<br>Restoration period formal walled gardens and part of park; late l8th-century landscape park with later planting<br>MAIN PHASES OF CONSTRUCTION<br>c. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s <br>VISITED BY/DATE  Elisabeth Whittle/August l99l; November l99l<br> HOUSE<br>Name  Tredegar House<br>Grid ref  ST 288852<br>Date/style late l5th century; c. l664 - l672/medieval manor house; Baroque<br>Brief description:  <br>Tredegar House stands on low-lying level ground west of the river Ebbw, on the western fringe of Newport. Until l95l the house was a private home, the principal residence of the Morgan family, who lived there from the early l5th century onwards. A substantial stone manor house was built on the site in the late l5th or early l6th century, of which only a small part, the south-west wing of the present house, survives.<br>\tBetween about l664 and l672 the present grand mansion was built by Sir William Morgan. It is a large two-storey square house built around a small courtyard. It is constructed of brick, with a pitched slate roof (originally higher, and surmounted by a cupola and balustrading) with attic storey. The main entrance is in the middle of the north-west side, with a fine doorway flanked by spiral twist columns supporting a broken split pediment. The elaborate stone porch on the north-east side is a l9th-century addition. The architect of the house is unknown. <br>\tApart from some internal rearrangements, the lowering of the roof, and the addition of the north-east porch, there were few subsequent alterations to the house. In l859 Sir Charles Morgan became the first Baron Tredegar. In l95l the last of the Morgans, John Morgan, sold the house to the Sisters of St Joseph for use as a Catholic girls' boarding school. In l974 house and grounds were purchased by Newport Borough Council.<br>OUTBUILDINGS<br>Name  Stable block<br>Grid ref  ST 287853<br>Date/style, and brief description<br>The stable block is a long low free-standing range lying to the north-west of the house, at right-angles to the main entrance. It is a fine building, constructed of brick, with a monumental central archway over which is a large pediment with a clock (l766). There are  Ionic brick pilasters  reaching half way up the wall, between the lower mullioned windows, above which are small oval windows. Inside, to the south of the archway are the hunting stables, and to the north is the riding school. Attached to the rear is an orangery (see Garden section). The stables probably date, on architectural grounds, to some time between c. l690 and c. l725, and were probably built by John Morgan (d. l7l9).<br>\tOther outbuildings lie to the south-east of the house, and are arranged loosely around a cobbled courtyard. They date mainly from the late l7th and l8th centuries. The Brewhouse (now restaurant) may have been part of the medieval stables, but was subsequently used as a brewhouse (until the early 20th century). On the east side of the yard are two large stone barns, the Greater Barn, and the Lesser Barn (originally one). Behind the latter is the Cattle byre (now Visitor Centre), and the Flour Mill. At right-angles to the Greater Barn was the Laundry and Dairy Block (now the school chapel for St Joseph's School).<br> THE PARK<br>Central grid ref  ST 284858<br>Date/style  Late l7th century;  late l8th century/formal; landscape<br> <br>GENERAL DESCRIPTION, HISTORY AND LAYOUT <br>Tredegar Park lies on the western edge of Newport, on low-lying ground in the Ebbw valley, and also on higher, rolling ground to the east and west. The original park is now severely truncated, built on, and transected by roads, including the M4 motorway. The main areas that remain open are to the north and north-west of the house.<br>\tThe park was first created after c. l664 by Sir William Morgan (who built the present house), and was an irregularly shaped area  to the N, S, E and W of the house, with the main ornamented area to the N and NE. Soon after it was created great axial avenues were planted, the main ones being the oak avenue running NW from the NW front of the house, and the double avenues focused on the Iron Age hillfort to the NE (Tredegar Fort), which ran SW-NE from the grounds up to the fort (a walnut avenue), and ESE-WNW from the fort towards Bassaleg church. A sweet chestnut avenue ran SE from the S gates of the walled gardens. Further avenues branched off the main ones or lined routes through the park. This layout is shown on the earliest map of the park, the survey by Robert Snell (c. l770), which also shows clumps of trees in the area to the N and NE of the house. By this time the park was about l000 acres in extent. The southern part, which is flat and low-lying, was the Home Farm, and was never ornamented, except for the chestnut avenue, and was managed mainly for sporting purposes. The part of the park to the NW of the house is referred to in the records as the 'New Park', and may have been added later than the other parts (the area to the N of the house is called the 'Old Park'). Part originally formed an earlier, medieval park, Cleppa Park, and within the Gwern-y-Cleppa wood are the ruins of a medieval house, reputedly once the home of Ivor-hael (early l4th century). Some ruined park walling remains in this part of the park.<br>\tThe next phase of development came in the l790s when the landscape designer Adam Mickle landscaped the grounds and park, removing most of the formal elements. Mickle made three main changes (although his initial plans involved even more radical change, including the demolition of the stables and removal of all but one of the walled gardens). He removed most of the avenues, leaving only the oak avenue on the N front, the chestnut avenue to the S, parts of the avenues to the hillfort, and some minor ones in the park. He built the new turnpike road through the park (now the A48), moving the old one further away from the house (to the N). This work included the screening of the road from the house and grounds by a wall and grass bank (Mickle signed the contract for the walling in l790). In conjunction with the new road Mickle realigned the main drive to its present position, and made a long sinuous lake, the 'Great Pool', to the N of the house, between the house and the road. It is dammed at its S end, and Mickle landscaped the south end  with shrubberies of holly, laurel and box (partly to screen the kitchen gardens). The water level is higher than the ground floor of the house.  <br>\tIn the late l9th century the gates and lodges were built at the entrance to the grounds, N of the house. When the M4 motorway was built, cutting the park in half, the A48 in front of the NW front of the house was realigned and the grass bank screening it was removed.<br>\tWhat remains of the park lies to the N and NW of the house, and is transected by the A48, the M4 and the A4072 roads. Mickle's lake remains, surrounded by later planting, including wellingtonias and rhododendrons planted in the l9th century, and a l930s plantation to the E of the lake. Only fragments of park planting remain to the E of the lake, most of which is taken up by office development. To the N, between the A48 and the M4, is an area of Recreation Ground, bounded on the N by the canalized river Ebbw. A few clumps of trees remain in this area. Above, to the N, is the steep-sided hill topped by the Tredegar Fort. This remains open, although modern housing estates have been built right up to the park boundary. The hill is rough grass and bracken covered, and no avenues remain. Between the M4 and the A4072 Bassaleg road the park has been converted into a golf course. A little planting remains in this area.<br>\tTo the NW of the house, NW of the M4, is the area of the 'New Park'. This is rolling agricultural land, with two areas of woodland. The only landscaping remaining in this area is the great oak avenue to the NW of the house. This begins on level ground to the S of the M4, which cuts through it, and continues NW up the hillside past Graig-y-Saeson Farm. Only a few of the trees in it are thought to be original.<br>\tThe park to the W of the house has been developed for office accommodation, and the only remnants of the park in this area are the 'Red Pond', a small  pond W of the stable block, which dates from before l770, and a stretch of avenue flanking the track leading to the 'New Park' area. To the S of the house most of the park is now covered with housing development.\t  <br>STRUCTURAL COMPONENTS<br>The present drive is that created by Adam Mickle in the l790s, when he move the  turnpike road  further away from the house. (At the same time a new approach was made to the rear of the house across the Home Farm.) The drive to the house enters the park off the A48 at ST 285858. It is gravelled, and runs parallel with the W side of the lake, curving round towards the house at the S end, where it divides, one branch to the outer court to the NW of the house, the other to the circle in front of the NE side of the house.<br>WATER FEATURES <br>The Ebbw river runs through the eastern side of the park. It is now canalized, but in the late l8th-early l9th centuries it provided a picturesque feature, with rocky banks. <br>The Red Pond (ST 286853) lies to the E of the stable block. This dates from before l775, and was formed when an embankment was built to carry the road from the house to the 'New Park'. The pond is roughly rectangular and  is naturalistic in outline.<br>Pond in the 'New Park' (ST 274851). A small artificial pond in a valley bottom, dammed along its S side. This is shown on the l770 survey map.<br>The lake, or Great Pool (ST 288855). This is a long, sinuous,  narrow artificial lake, dammed at its S end, which lies between the house and the A48 road to the N. It was created by excavation by Adam Mickle in the l790s (some of the spoil covered the Orangery Garden, see Garden Section). At the NW end, where the lake narrows, there are two islands. At the dam end there is a recently rebuilt bridge (replacing a l9th-century one) over the overflow outlet, with a rebuilt small cascade below it (possibly originally a Mickle feature). The SW edge, in front of the NE front of the house, is edged with a low brick wall topped with dressed stone, with stone plinths (with central iron pins to take ? urns) at intervals. <br>BUILT FEATURES <br>Park walls, lodges and gates<br>The park entrance (ST 285858) consists of a late l9th-century grouping of double wrought-iron gates between square stone gate piers topped by decorative stone urns. These are flanked by side pedestrian gates, railings on low brick walls, and then twin pavilion lodges. These are small square buildings of brick with stone dressings, with hipped slate roofs and central chimneys. (This entrance is now disused.) There is a short stretch of contemporary brick wall with stone capping  W of the lodges, which is continued by a modern stone wall, where the alignment of the A48 has been altered. To the E there is a similar short stretch of wall, ending in a pier topped by a ball finial. The boundary between the park and the A48 then continues as a low wall topped by iron railings.<br>\tIn the NW part of the park (the 'New Park') there are remains of the park boundary wall. It is of rubble construction, standing to an uneven height, but c. 2 m. high where complete, and runs along the S edge of Gwern-y-Cleppa wood (demolished recently at its E end, by the former lodge), westwards across a small valley, and then SSW across pasture land.<br>Boathouse<br>On the S side of the lake, near the SE end. This wooden boathouse is an early 20th-century construction.<br>Other remaining buildings in the park are of a utilitarian nature:  there is a stone barn in the 'Old Park' (ST 284865), a ruined stone barn, Cleppa Park (ST 275851), in the 'New Park', built between l770 and l827. On the S edge of Gwern-y-Cleppa wood is a former lodge (inhabited) (ST 273856).<br>\tJust N of the S end of the main drive is a stone Gorsedd Circle (20th-century).<br>PLANTED COMPONENTS <br>What remains of the park divides into five main areas:  between the house and the A48, the recreation ground, the Tredegar Fort hill, the golf course, and the western part of the park.<br>l. Between the house and the A48<br>This is a flat area to the NW and N of the house. To the NW it is grassed, with the southern end of the oak avenue between the outer court and the A48. The avenue will be taken as a whole here, including the part to the NW of the M4, the trees of which are protected under TPO no. l04 (T8 - T4l). The oaks in the avenue are of mixed age. There are three different species (Quercus robur (English oak), Q. cerris (Turkey oak), and Q. rubra (Red oak). The avenue is broken by the A48, roundabout, and M4, and continues up the slope in the NW part of the park. It will be considered here as a whole. It is a single avenue, with 49 remaining trees of varying ages and sizes. It is thought that the avenue was originally planted with Q. robur, and that there may be four of the original trees still alive. Replantings have been mainly with Q. cerris and Q. rubra, and to the S of the A48 the planting distances have been halved. <br>\tTo the NE of the southern end of the avenue the drive is flanked on its W side by a row of cedars (20th-century), with a group of wellingtonias (20th-century) at the southern end.\t<br>\tThe lake is fringed by trees all around. On the S side, opposite the NE front of the house it is lined with six large alders (Alnus cordata), which predate the building of the lake retaining wall here in the second half of the l9th century. Huge wellingtonias and pines (l9th-century) grow at regular intervals around the lake (wellingtonias at the S end, pines at the N end). The S end of the lake is well wooded, and some large hollies may be all that is left of Mickle's shrubbery. Along the E side of the lake are the remnants of a l930s plantation of conifers (mainly Sitka spruce,  Corsican pine and Scots pine), with some deciduous trees mixed in. The islands in the lake are well wooded (mainly deciduous), and there is mixed semi-natural woodland around the Red Pond.<br>2. The Recreation Ground.<br>This is a level grass area, with a childrens' playground, miniature golf course and hard tennis courts in the eastern corner. The rest of the area is laid out with playing fields. Scattered throughout are isolated deciduous trees (mostly 20th-century). There are four large clumps of oak trees, each with 5-7 trees. A clumped avenue is shown in this area (extending N into the golf-course area) on the l770 and (partly) on the l827 maps, and these clumps may be replantings of some of these clumps.<br>3. Tredegar Fort hill.<br>This hill is bracken and rough grass covered. There are no traces of the former avenues.<br>4. The golf course.<br>This is a level grass area through which winds the river Ebbw, flanked by trees. On the western edge of the area the ground begins to rise quite steeply above the river's flood plain, and this steep slope is planted with conifers, including pines, at its southern end. There is a large clump of mature trees (mainly oak, with one very large oak) near the southern end, to the E of a small watercourse. There are a few small clumps of oaks scattered throughout the area, and along the northern half of the western boundary with the A4072 road is a row of large oaks, which is all that is left of an avenue (a branch off the oak avenue NW of the house), which is shown on the l770 map.<br>5. The NW part of the park.<br>The remaining part of the park in this area, which was known as the 'New Park' is agricultural, mainly permanent pasture. There are two areas of deciduous woodland, Gwern-y-Cleppa wood (ST 273856), and the wood W of Cleppa Park (ST 273850). Some of the woodland (narrow strip E of Gwern-y-Cleppa, ref. Wl) and trees (to the S, ref. T47 - T53, and A4) are protected by TPO no. l04. One small stretch of avenue (horse chestnut) survives W of the A48, flanking the track that led from the house to the park (ST 282852, TPO no. l04, ref. A3). A few trees of it remain to the E of the A48. The oak avenue in the northern part of the park has been described in l. above. <br>BOUNDARIES <br>NE of house:  wooden fence on E boundary of wood next to lake; wall and railings on boundary with A48 road;  housing estates E of Tredegar Fort hill.<br>N of house:   roads and housing<br>NW of house:  field boundaries, including ruined walling<br>SW of house:  stone wall (recently built)<br>SE of house:  garden boundary<br>ESSENTIAL SETTING, VIEWPOINTS AND CONTINUATIONS OF FEATURES BEYOND THE PARK, EYECATCHERS ETC. <br>Essential setting: farmland to the NW of the park<br>Views: NW from the house up the oak avenue (A)<br>LAND-USE<br>Public park; recreation ground; golf course; public open space (Tredegar Fort hill); agricultural land (permanent pasture and woodland)<br>ELEMENTS OF BOTANICAL OR OTHER NATURE CONSERVATION INTEREST<br>Area around the Red Pond is being developed as an area of nature conservation interest<br>SURVIVAL OF INDIVIDUAL COMPONENTS<br>Structural components:  most<br>Water features:  all<br>Built components:  most<br>Planted components:  little<br>---------------------<br>THE PLEASURE GARDEN <br>Grid ref  ST 287852<br>Date/style  c. l670s - l920s/walled; sunken 'Italian'<br>GENERAL DESCRIPTION, HISTORY  AND LAYOUT <br>The gardens of Tredegar House lie on level ground to the SW, NW and NE of the house. They consist mainly of three large walled compartments (and a further smaller one at the NW end)  aligned on a NW-SE axis to the SW of the house. The high walls are of brick, and on the central axis are openings flanked by square piers topped by stone urn finials. Each compartment is laid out differently.  The northernmost, smaller one, is a yard devoid of garden features. The next, the Orangery Garden, is now bare, having been excavated in l99l. Along most of its NE side is a brick orangery, attached to the stable block. The next compartment, the 'Cedar Garden', lies next to the house, with a wrought iron gate through into the forecourt. It is square, and is largely laid out to lawn, with a wide perimeter gravel path, wall borders, and a large cedar tree in the lawn. In the centre is the grave of the horse Sir Briggs, surrounded by yew hedging. The southernmost compartment is the largest. The central axis is continued with a path, now grassed over. The rest of the compartment is grassed, and planted informally with  specimen trees and shrubs. At the southern end of the central axis is a gateway flanked by tall square brick piers topped by stone urn finials.<br>\t  To the NW of the house is a forecourt, laid out recently with grass squares and wide gravel paths, which is enclosed by brick walls and  on the NW side by a pair of wrought-iron gates (the Edney Gates) flanked by low walls topped by iron railings. Beyond is an outer court enclosed by modern brick walls and the stable block (on the SW side). The court is divided into quadrants by wide cobbled paths with a central oval area (recently restored).  <br>\tThe ground between the NE front of the house and the lake is largely lawn, with a screening plantation of trees and shrubs, and a low wall  along its NW side. The gravel drive enters the area through wrought iron gates flanked by square piers on the NW side. In front of the house is a wide turning circle, with a grass centre in the middle of which is a circular stone basin. To the NE of this is a small sunken rectangular garden, restored as a rose garden,  with a small Italianate 'tempietta' at the southern end.<br>\tThe earliest part of the gardens are the walled compartments to the SW of the house, and the forecourt to the NW. A l9th-century tracing by Octavius Morgan of a plan of the house and gardens as they were in the early l7th century, before William Morgan's rebuilding, shows approximately the same layout of compartments, with a forecourt and 'great bowling greene' on the NW side, a 'wilderness or maze' roughly where the Orangery garden now is, a square garden with 'the great walke about' around it, laid out with a formal parterre, roughly where the 'Cedar Garden' now is, and the southern compartment laid out as orchard. To the SW were two further large orchards. The present brick walls (except for those newly built around the outer court on the NW side) date from the late l7th or early l8th century, as do the Edney Gates, the stable block and the orangery which are incorporated into the layout. It has been discovered by excavation (De Bois Landscape Group, l99l) that the Orangery Garden was laid out, probably c. l720, with an elaborate arrangement of paths, flowerbeds and two inorganic parterres in front of the orangery.<br>\tThe next phase of development was in the late l8th century, when Adam Mickle supervised the landscaping of the park. At this stage, probably in the late l780s, a thick layer of soil from the dredging of the lake was deposited over the Orangery Garden, thus preserving the early l8th-century layout, which has now been excavated. The present layout of the Cedar Garden and the southern compartment dates largely from this period. The gardens are said to have been laid out by a Mr Walker, who was head gardener from l782 to l80l, using newly imported American species. Extensive hot-houses were built in l79l, but these have entirely disappeared, and their exact original location is not known.<br>\tAlterations were made to the NE front of the house in the l870s, with the opening up of the central door, the making of the drive, and the planting of the screen of trees. A fountain was added to the centre of the outer court in front of the stable block some time in the l9th century (now gone). The sunken Italianate garden dates from the l920s, and has been recently restored. <br>STRUCTURAL COMPONENTS <br>The Outer Court<br>This is divided in two (E-W) by the Edney Gates and gatescreen. The inner compartment has been restored with four large grass squares and wide gravel paths (similar to those in the Thomas Dineley sketch of the Outer Court, before the Edney Gates, done in l684). The outer compartment has been restored with its original layout of cobbled cross paths, a central cobbled oval, and gravel.<br>The walled gardens<br>No structural features remain visible in the northernmost compartment.<br>All the following paths and other features are thought to be of late l7th-early l8th century date. <br>\tIn the Orangery Garden, the next compartment to the south, the main structural feature visible is the central gravel path which runs NW-SE down the central axis. In the W corner there is a small mound which may be the remains of a viewing platform. In front of the orangery is a slightly raised terrace.<br>\tThe next compartment, the Cedar Garden, has a wide gravel path continuing the central axis of the walled gardens, and a wide perimeter path around the wall borders (recently restored). The central axis is continued in the southernmost compartment with a raised, grassed over path. <br>The north-east front<br>The main drive, of gravel,  enters the gardens NE of the house on their NW side. It sweeps round in a large turning circle in front of the house. The Italian Garden to the E is sunken, with stepped sides. It is divided into four by stone flagged paths.<br> <br> BUILT COMPONENTS <br>Outer Court<br>The two compartments of the Outer Court are surrounded by brick walls, the house and the stable block. The walls of the outer compartment are modern. Dividing the area in two are the 'Edney' gates and gatescreen. These were installed in l7l3-l8, built for John Morgan by th", 'nodegroup_id': '11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '<p>Tredegar Park makes up the original grounds to Tredegar House, one of the most magnificent 17th century buildings in South Wales. Important parts of its grand late l7th-century layout survive, including garden walls enclosing both `restored&#39; and modern gardens, with a gatescreen, and avenue. The basis for restoration of the spectacular restored `inorganic parterre&#39; is questionable. Much of the l8th-century landscape park and later planting survives, but there are also modern gardens, some of them in the modern public park.c. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s<br />\n<br />\nRedevelopment of part of the formal gardens documented during RCAHMW aerial reconnaissance on 24th June 2003.<br />\nT. Driver</p>\n\n<p>WELSH COUNTRY HOMES 1 TREDEGAR HOUSE South Wales Echo 30th October 1909<br />\n..Tredegar Oaks are famous<br />\nand the grounds near the house contain many fine examples of choice trees. such as the Cedar of lebanon, the American Pine, and the Tupil Tree, while the groves of Sweet Chestnut and evergreen oak are especially worthy of remark. The river Ebbw, black with its burden of coal washings, flanks the part at its eastern end. A short walk from the house through a leafy copse takes the visitor to the edge of a beautiful lake from which the waters of the river Ebbw are rigidly excluded, for it is clear as crystaland the ? rise briskly on every hand. The shrubbery was planted about 1795, at the same time taht the lake was made.<br />\n---------<br />\nTwo aerial photographs taken in 1937 over the site. Where are the originals?<br />\n------------<br />\nFrom Briggs 1991a<br />\nA century after Dineley, plans were made for Tredegar Park which would &#39;sweep away courtyards and stables, break..avenues and divert the turnpike road..forming a substantial lake&#39;(Jacques 1983, 116). Although the stables were left intact, its formal garden disappeared. Now, under local authority ownership, a praiseworthy effort is being made to recover and restore for display features of the seventeenth century garden, by carefully excavating the parterres and walled areas (Freeman 1989). In summer 1990 excavations directed by John Phibbs re-located some of these features, turning up buried geometric parterres originally coloured by coaldust, broken brick and shells. Trawscoed, now leased to the Property Services Agency, was completely remodelled during the nineteenth century, as was Picton, still in private ownership, which, in common with Ruperra, has apparently lost its Renaissance features. Nevertheless, that it has been possible to uncover intact some parts of Tredegar&#39;s formal garden should be a salutary warning of the need to be ever watchful when later landscaping is restored or ground disturbance undertaken around any early gentry or manor houses.<br />\n-------------------------------------------<br />\nTredegar House<br />\nThe formal walled gardens at Tredegar House (Newport, Gwent), have long been considered &#39;one of the most important early eighteenth century garden landscapes in Wales&#39;(Freeman 1992 which is the main source of the interpretative account which follows). Probably originally constituting a Tudor five-enclosure garden layout to the SW of the medieval house, in the early 1700s its garden walls were realigned and reconstructed in brick, rather than stone, to make three enclosures linked by one central gravel path running parallel to the house.<br />\nThe largest, most southerly enclosure contained the orchard and gardener&#39;s cottage with ancillary buildings. Next came the Cedar Garden, on its present pattern no more than a century old; dominated by a great Cedrus Libani, framed by herbaceous borders, and carrying an obelisk horse memorial and three headstones to pet dogs. At one time, this enclosure may have been laid out in the Dutch style, but in the later eighteenth century it appears to have become an American garden (i.e.it carried some elements of American shrub flora).<br />\nThe third enclosure, the Orangery Garden, was geophysically survey, then excavated, between 1989 and 1991 by the Debois Partnership. Apparently the pattern of parterre uncovered was surfaced &#39;with different coloured materials including sea shells, crushed lime mortar, brickdust, coal dust, white sand, yellow sand, orange sand and grass, all arranged in a formal pattern and sometimes bordered with low clipped box hedging&#39;. It should be added that until this discovery, as Freeman candidly remarks &#39;materials used for late seventeenth-early eighteenth century parterres have only been known from contemporary garden writers&#39;..[but at Tredegar it was felt]..they had miraculously survived together with evidence of the garden layout&#39;(Freeman 1992). Another source apparently indicates that spoil excavated from a lake created during landscaping at Tredegar in 1790 was actually spread over this garden at that time, &#39;burying the baroque layout&#39;(Whittle 1993,66).<br />\nAs a direct result of the Debois Partnership investigation, commissioned to inform a process restoring the enclosure to c 1680-1800 (but with the idea of Dutch parterres uppermost), the Authorities subsequently adopted an imaginative layout in which coloured minerals form its component pattern. As an experiment in re-creation, this was a most interesting departure; visually the result is attractive and exciting (though extremely difficult to maintain).<br />\nUnfortunately, critical examination of the evidence underlying the restorers&#39; guidelines is currently hampered by absence of any published excavation report. But whilst it may seem ungracious to venture appraisal of unpublished findings, the finished restoration is in itself a convincing, if not rather opulent statement, which has already attracted considerable acclaim from visitors and garden historians alike. This enclosure and Tredegar House&#39;s other gardens are in fact widely venerated for their historic importance and archaeological potential (Phibbs 1991). Critical comment is therefore justified in view of the probability that future work of a similar type may be intended there. Accordingly, whilst access to a complete excavation archive has not been possible, the following observations are offered from an examination of the investigation accounts so far presented to Newport Borough Council (Ewart 1990;Ewart and Phibbs 1990):<br />\nFirst, the Orangery Garden, some 200 ft long by 150 ft wide, said to be one of the most important of its type and period, was investigated and stripped mainly by machine. Apparently less than three weeks&#39; work went into this exercise, and an important part of the excavators&#39; commission was to make only a &#39;watching brief&#39; of the machine digging. At the outset,it appears that inadequate human resourcing was afforded for effective observation, recording, sampling and research on such a massive and complex site (whatever its acclaimed importance).<br />\nSecondly, without doubt a broad spectrum of earthen, clay and mineral deposits was found and described. However, these deposits occurred in contexts which hardly recommend their unequivocal interpretation in the manner now re-created. In fact, vital contributions absent from the excavators&#39; statement include reports from a pedologist, petrographer and sedimentologist defining or identifying key elements of the crucial stratified deposits on which the garden&#39;s history was interpreted. Equally strange is an absence of any apparent sampling strategy for pollen or plant macro-remains.<br />\nThirdly, available notes on the excavated deposits seem to offer few real clues which demonstrate convincing complete patterns of garden beds at any particular period. Indeed, two large rectangular features interpreted as having been &#39;beds&#39; might equally have been lost buildings with their brick or stone footings robbed out. In fact, overall, the site&#39;s extremely complex history was demonstrated by stratigraphic exposures in each of a dozen or so trenches (most of them &#39;keyhole&#39; excavations). Although trenched to assist an understanding of the site, these appeared to raise far more questions than they answered. Fortunately, it seems that only a limited amount of topsoil was machined from the area, and much earlier stratigraphy was deliberately covered over by the garden re-creation to be left for future investigation.<br />\nFrom the documentation so far presented, it is difficult to escape the view that a dating of c 1700 afforded the eventual reconstruction (of a &#39;Dutch parterre&#39;), &#39;the only one of its kind in Britain&#39;(Whittle 1993,66), is in fact almost entirely conjectural. Although some of these misgivings will no doubt be allayed by full publication of this important investigation, it is difficult to escape the view that alternative interpretations to the present re-creation may eventually present themselves when the garden&#39;s deposits are thoroughly re-examined at some future time.<br />\n<br />\ncsb 1996<br />\n------------------------------------------------<br />\nCADW/ICOMOS REGISTER OF PARKS AND GARDENS<br />\nOF SPECIAL HISTORIC INTEREST IN WALES<br />\nSITE DOSSIER<br />\nSITE NAME Tredegar Park<br />\nREF. NO. PGW (Gt) 48<br />\nOS MAP l7l GRID REF. ST 290853<br />\nFORMER COUNTY Gwent UNITARY AUTHORITY Newport B.C. COMMUNITY COUNCIL Duffryn<br />\nOWNER/S Single/multiple Public/private<br />\nTredegar House: Newport Borough Council<br />\nOCCUPIER/S<br />\nAs above; multiple<br />\nDESIGNATIONS Listed building: Tredegar House: grade I; Riding School and stables: grade II; Gate piers and gates: grade II<br />\nSAM (Mn 84: Tredegar Fort) CA TPO no. l04<br />\nSITE EVALUATION Grade II*<br />\nPrimary reasons for grading<br />\nSurvival of parts of grand late l7th-century layout, including garden walls, gatescreen, inorganic parterres, and avenue.<br />\nTYPE OF SITE<br />\nRestoration period formal walled gardens and part of park; late l8th-century landscape park with later planting<br />\nMAIN PHASES OF CONSTRUCTION<br />\nc. l680 - l720; l790s; l870s<br />\nVISITED BY/DATE Elisabeth Whittle/August l99l; November l99l<br />\nHOUSE<br />\nName Tredegar House<br />\nGrid ref ST 288852<br />\nDate/style late l5th century; c. l664 - l672/medieval manor house; Baroque<br />\nBrief description:<br />\nTredegar House stands on low-lying level ground west of the river Ebbw, on the western fringe of Newport. Until l95l the house was a private home, the principal residence of the Morgan family, who lived there from the early l5th century onwards. A substantial stone manor house was built on the site in the late l5th or early l6th century, of which only a small part, the south-west wing of the present house, survives.<br />\nBetween about l664 and l672 the present grand mansion was built by Sir William Morgan. It is a large two-storey square house built around a small courtyard. It is constructed of brick, with a pitched slate roof (originally higher, and surmounted by a cupola and balustrading) with attic storey. The main entrance is in the middle of the north-west side, with a fine doorway flanked by spiral twist columns supporting a broken split pediment. The elaborate stone porch on the north-east side is a l9th-century addition. The architect of the house is unknown.<br />\nApart from some internal rearrangements, the lowering of the roof, and the addition of the north-east porch, there were few subsequent alterations to the house. In l859 Sir Charles Morgan became the first Baron Tredegar. In l95l the last of the Morgans, John Morgan, sold the house to the Sisters of St Joseph for use as a Catholic girls&#39; boarding school. In l974 house and grounds were purchased by Newport Borough Council.<br />\nOUTBUILDINGS<br />\nName Stable block<br />\nGrid ref ST 287853<br />\nDate/style, and brief description<br />\nThe stable block is a long low free-standing range lying to the north-west of the house, at right-angles to the main entrance. It is a fine building, constructed of brick, with a monumental central archway over which is a large pediment with a clock (l766). There are Ionic brick pilasters reaching half way up the wall, between the lower mullioned windows, above which are small oval windows. Inside, to the south of the archway are the hunting stables, and to the north is the riding school. Attached to the rear is an orangery (see Garden section). The stables probably date, on architectural grounds, to some time between c. l690 and c. l725, and were probably built by John Morgan (d. l7l9).<br />\nOther outbuildings lie to the south-east of the house, and are arranged loosely around a cobbled courtyard. They date mainly from the late l7th and l8th centuries. The Brewhouse (now restaurant) may have been part of the medieval stables, but was subsequently used as a brewhouse (until the early 20th century). On the east side of the yard are two large stone barns, the Greater Barn, and the Lesser Barn (originally one). Behind the latter is the Cattle byre (now Visitor Centre), and the Flour Mill. At right-angles to the Greater Barn was the Laundry and Dairy Block (now the school chapel for St Joseph&#39;s School).<br />\nTHE PARK<br />\nCentral grid ref ST 284858<br />\nDate/style Late l7th century; late l8th century/formal; landscape<br />\n<br />\nGENERAL DESCRIPTION, HISTORY AND LAYOUT<br />\nTredegar Park lies on the western edge of Newport, on low-lying ground in the Ebbw valley, and also on higher, rolling ground to the east and west. The original park is now severely truncated, built on, and transected by roads, including the M4 motorway. The main areas that remain open are to the north and north-west of the house.<br />\nThe park was first created after c. l664 by Sir William Morgan (who built the present house), and was an irregularly shaped area to the N, S, E and W of the house, with the main ornamented area to the N and NE. Soon after it was created great axial avenues were planted, the main ones being the oak avenue running NW from the NW front of the house, and the double avenues focused on the Iron Age hillfort to the NE (Tredegar Fort), which ran SW-NE from the grounds up to the fort (a walnut avenue), and ESE-WNW from the fort towards Bassaleg church. A sweet chestnut avenue ran SE from the S gates of the walled gardens. Further avenues branched off the main ones or lined routes through the park. This layout is shown on the earliest map of the park, the survey by Robert Snell (c. l770), which also shows clumps of trees in the area to the N and NE of the house. By this time the park was about l000 acres in extent. The southern part, which is flat and low-lying, was the Home Farm, and was never ornamented, except for the chestnut avenue, and was managed mainly for sporting purposes. The part of the park to the NW of the house is referred to in the records as the &#39;New Park&#39;, and may have been added later than the other parts (the area to the N of the house is called the &#39;Old Park&#39;). Part originally formed an earlier, medieval park, Cleppa Park, and within the Gwern-y-Cleppa wood are the ruins of a medieval house, reputedly once the home of Ivor-hael (early l4th century). Some ruined park walling remains in this part of the park.<br />\nThe next phase of development came in the l790s when the landscape designer Adam Mickle landscaped the grounds and park, removing most of the formal elements. Mickle made three main changes (although his initial plans involved even more radical change, including the demolition of the stables and removal of all but one of the walled gardens). He removed most of the avenues, leaving only the oak avenue on the N front, the chestnut avenue to the S, parts of the avenues to the hillfort, and some minor ones in the park. He built the new turnpike road through the park (now the A48), moving the old one further away from the house (to the N). This work included the screening of the road from the house and grounds by a wall and grass bank (Mickle signed the contract for the walling in l790). In conjunction with the new road Mickle realigned the main drive to its present position, and made a long sinuous lake, the &#39;Great Pool&#39;, to the N of the house, between the house and the road. It is dammed at its S end, and Mickle landscaped the south end with shrubberies of holly, laurel and box (partly to screen the kitchen gardens). The water level is higher than the ground floor of the house.<br />\nIn the late l9th century the gates and lodges were built at the entrance to the grounds, N of the house. When the M4 motorway was built, cutting the park in half, the A48 in front of the NW front of the house was realigned and the grass bank screening it was removed.<br />\nWhat remains of the park lies to the N and NW of the house, and is transected by the A48, the M4 and the A4072 roads. Mickle&#39;s lake remains, surrounded by later planting, including wellingtonias and rhododendrons planted in the l9th century, and a l930s plantation to the E of the lake. Only fragments of park planting remain to the E of the lake, most of which is taken up by office development. To the N, between the A48 and the M4, is an area of Recreation Ground, bounded on the N by the canalized river Ebbw. A few clumps of trees remain in this area. Above, to the N, is the steep-sided hill topped by the Tredegar Fort. This remains open, although modern housing estates have been built right up to the park boundary. The hill is rough grass and bracken covered, and no avenues remain. Between the M4 and the A4072 Bassaleg road the park has been converted into a golf course. A little planting remains in this area.<br />\nTo the NW of the house, NW of the M4, is the area of the &#39;New Park&#39;. This is rolling agricultural land, with two areas of woodland. The only landscaping remaining in this area is the great oak avenue to the NW of the house. This begins on level ground to the S of the M4, which cuts through it, and continues NW up the hillside past Graig-y-Saeson Farm. Only a few of the trees in it are thought to be original.<br />\nThe park to the W of the house has been developed for office accommodation, and the only remnants of the park in this area are the &#39;Red Pond&#39;, a small pond W of the stable block, which dates from before l770, and a stretch of avenue flanking the track leading to the &#39;New Park&#39; area. To the S of the house most of the park is now covered with housing development.<br />\nSTRUCTURAL COMPONENTS<br />\nThe present drive is that created by Adam Mickle in the l790s, when he move the turnpike road further away from the house. (At the same time a new approach was made to the rear of the house across the Home Farm.) The drive to the house enters the park off the A48 at ST 285858. It is gravelled, and runs parallel with the W side of the lake, curving round towards the house at the S end, where it divides, one branch to the outer court to the NW of the house, the other to the circle in front of the NE side of the house.<br />\nWATER FEATURES<br />\nThe Ebbw river runs through the eastern side of the park. It is now canalized, but in the late l8th-early l9th centuries it provided a picturesque feature, with rocky banks.<br />\nThe Red Pond (ST 286853) lies to the E of the stable block. This dates from before l775, and was formed when an embankment was built to carry the road from the house to the &#39;New Park&#39;. The pond is roughly rectangular and is naturalistic in outline.<br />\nPond in the &#39;New Park&#39; (ST 274851). A small artificial pond in a valley bottom, dammed along its S side. This is shown on the l770 survey map.<br />\nThe lake, or Great Pool (ST 288855). This is a long, sinuous, narrow artificial lake, dammed at its S end, which lies between the house and the A48 road to the N. It was created by excavation by Adam Mickle in the l790s (some of the spoil covered the Orangery Garden, see Garden Section). At the NW end, where the lake narrows, there are two islands. At the dam end there is a recently rebuilt bridge (replacing a l9th-century one) over the overflow outlet, with a rebuilt small cascade below it (possibly originally a Mickle feature). The SW edge, in front of the NE front of the house, is edged with a low brick wall topped with dressed stone, with stone plinths (with central iron pins to take ? urns) at intervals.<br />\nBUILT FEATURES<br />\nPark walls, lodges and gates<br />\nThe park entrance (ST 285858) consists of a late l9th-century grouping of double wrought-iron gates between square stone gate piers topped by decorative stone urns. These are flanked by side pedestrian gates, railings on low brick walls, and then twin pavilion lodges. These are small square buildings of brick with stone dressings, with hipped slate roofs and central chimneys. (This entrance is now disused.) There is a short stretch of contemporary brick wall with stone capping W of the lodges, which is continued by a modern stone wall, where the alignment of the A48 has been altered. To the E there is a similar short stretch of wall, ending in a pier topped by a ball finial. The boundary between the park and the A48 then continues as a low wall topped by iron railings.<br />\nIn the NW part of the park (the &#39;New Park&#39;) there are remains of the park boundary wall. It is of rubble construction, standing to an uneven height, but c. 2 m. high where complete, and runs along the S edge of Gwern-y-Cleppa wood (demolished recently at its E end, by the former lodge), westwards across a small valley, and then SSW across pasture land.<br />\nBoathouse<br />\nOn the S side of the lake, near the SE end. This wooden boathouse is an early 20th-century construction.<br />\nOther remaining buildings in the park are of a utilitarian nature: there is a stone barn in the &#39;Old Park&#39; (ST 284865), a ruined stone barn, Cleppa Park (ST 275851), in the &#39;New Park&#39;, built between l770 and l827. On the S edge of Gwern-y-Cleppa wood is a former lodge (inhabited) (ST 273856).<br />\nJust N of the S end of the main drive is a stone Gorsedd Circle (20th-century).<br />\nPLANTED COMPONENTS<br />\nWhat remains of the park divides into five main areas: between the house and the A48, the recreation ground, the Tredegar Fort hill, the golf course, and the western part of the park.<br />\nl. Between the house and the A48<br />\nThis is a flat area to the NW and N of the house. To the NW it is grassed, with the southern end of the oak avenue between the outer court and the A48. The avenue will be taken as a whole here, including the part to the NW of the M4, the trees of which are protected under TPO no. l04 (T8 - T4l). The oaks in the avenue are of mixed age. There are three different species (Quercus robur (English oak), Q. cerris (Turkey oak), and Q. rubra (Red oak). The avenue is broken by the A48, roundabout, and M4, and continues up the slope in the NW part of the park. It will be considered here as a whole. It is a single avenue, with 49 remaining trees of varying ages and sizes. It is thought that the avenue was originally planted with Q. robur, and that there may be four of the original trees still alive. Replantings have been mainly with Q. cerris and Q. rubra, and to the S of the A48 the planting distances have been halved.<br />\nTo the NE of the southern end of the avenue the drive is flanked on its W side by a row of cedars (20th-century), with a group of wellingtonias (20th-century) at the southern end.<br />\nThe lake is fringed by trees all around. On the S side, opposite the NE front of the house it is lined with six large alders (Alnus cordata), which predate the building of the lake retaining wall here in the second half of the l9th century. Huge wellingtonias and pines (l9th-century) grow at regular intervals around the lake (wellingtonias at the S end, pines at the N end). The S end of the lake is well wooded, and some large hollies may be all that is left of Mickle&#39;s shrubbery. Along the E side of the lake are the remnants of a l930s plantation of conifers (mainly Sitka spruce, Corsican pine and Scots pine), with some deciduous trees mixed in. The islands in the lake are well wooded (mainly deciduous), and there is mixed semi-natural woodland around the Red Pond.<br />\n2. The Recreation Ground.<br />\nThis is a level grass area, with a childrens&#39; playground, miniature golf course and hard tennis courts in the eastern corner. The rest of the area is laid out with playing fields. Scattered throughout are isolated deciduous trees (mostly 20th-century). There are four large clumps of oak trees, each with 5-7 trees. A clumped avenue is shown in this area (extending N into the golf-course area) on the l770 and (partly) on the l827 maps, and these clumps may be replantings of some of these clumps.<br />\n3. Tredegar Fort hill.<br />\nThis hill is bracken and rough grass covered. There are no traces of the former avenues.<br />\n4. The golf course.<br />\nThis is a level grass area through which winds the river Ebbw, flanked by trees. On the western edge of the area the ground begins to rise quite steeply above the river&#39;s flood plain, and this steep slope is planted with conifers, including pines, at its southern end. There is a large clump of mature trees (mainly oak, with one very large oak) near the southern end, to the E of a small watercourse. There are a few small clumps of oaks scattered throughout the area, and along the northern half of the western boundary with the A4072 road is a row of large oaks, which is all that is left of an avenue (a branch off the oak avenue NW of the house), which is shown on the l770 map.<br />\n5. The NW part of the park.<br />\nThe remaining part of the park in this area, which was known as the &#39;New Park&#39; is agricultural, mainly permanent pasture. There are two areas of deciduous woodland, Gwern-y-Cleppa wood (ST 273856), and the wood W of Cleppa Park (ST 273850). Some of the woodland (narrow strip E of Gwern-y-Cleppa, ref. Wl) and trees (to the S, ref. T47 - T53, and A4) are protected by TPO no. l04. One small stretch of avenue (horse chestnut) survives W of the A48, flanking the track that led from the house to the park (ST 282852, TPO no. l04, ref. A3). A few trees of it remain to the E of the A48. The oak avenue in the northern part of the park has been described in l. above.<br />\nBOUNDARIES<br />\nNE of house: wooden fence on E boundary of wood next to lake; wall and railings on boundary with A48 road; housing estates E of Tredegar Fort hill.<br />\nN of house: roads and housing<br />\nNW of house: field boundaries, including ruined walling<br />\nSW of house: stone wall (recently built)<br />\nSE of house: garden boundary<br />\nESSENTIAL SETTING, VIEWPOINTS AND CONTINUATIONS OF FEATURES BEYOND THE PARK, EYECATCHERS ETC.<br />\nEssential setting: farmland to the NW of the park<br />\nViews: NW from the house up the oak avenue (A)<br />\nLAND-USE<br />\nPublic park; recreation ground; golf course; public open space (Tredegar Fort hill); agricultural land (permanent pasture and woodland)<br />\nELEMENTS OF BOTANICAL OR OTHER NATURE CONSERVATION INTEREST<br />\nArea around the Red Pond is being developed as an area of nature conservation interest<br />\nSURVIVAL OF INDIVIDUAL COMPONENTS<br />\nStructural components: most<br />\nWater features: all<br />\nBuilt components: most<br />\nPlanted components: little<br />\n---------------------<br />\nTHE PLEASURE GARDEN<br />\nGrid ref ST 287852<br />\nDate/style c. l670s - l920s/walled; sunken &#39;Italian&#39;<br />\nGENERAL DESCRIPTION, HISTORY AND LAYOUT<br />\nThe gardens of Tredegar House lie on level ground to the SW, NW and NE of the house. They consist mainly of three large walled compartments (and a further smaller one at the NW end) aligned on a NW-SE axis to the SW of the house. The high walls are of brick, and on the central axis are openings flanked by square piers topped by stone urn finials. Each compartment is laid out differently. The northernmost, smaller one, is a yard devoid of garden features. The next, the Orangery Garden, is now bare, having been excavated in l99l. Along most of its NE side is a brick orangery, attached to the stable block. The next compartment, the &#39;Cedar Garden&#39;, lies next to the house, with a wrought iron gate through into the forecourt. It is square, and is largely laid out to lawn, with a wide perimeter gravel path, wall borders, and a large cedar tree in the lawn. In the centre is the grave of the horse Sir Briggs, surrounded by yew hedging. The southernmost compartment is the largest. The central axis is continued with a path, now grassed over. The rest of the compartment is grassed, and planted informally with specimen trees and shrubs. At the southern end of the central axis is a gateway flanked by tall square brick piers topped by stone urn finials.<br />\nTo the NW of the house is a forecourt, laid out recently with grass squares and wide gravel paths, which is enclosed by brick walls and on the NW side by a pair of wrought-iron gates (the Edney Gates) flanked by low walls topped by iron railings. Beyond is an outer court enclosed by modern brick walls and the stable block (on the SW side). The court is divided into quadrants by wide cobbled paths with a central oval area (recently restored).<br />\nThe ground between the NE front of the house and the lake is largely lawn, with a screening plantation of trees and shrubs, and a low wall along its NW side. The gravel drive enters the area through wrought iron gates flanked by square piers on the NW side. In front of the house is a wide turning circle, with a grass centre in the middle of which is a circular stone basin. To the NE of this is a small sunken rectangular garden, restored as a rose garden, with a small Italianate &#39;tempietta&#39; at the southern end.<br />\nThe earliest part of the gardens are the walled compartments to the SW of the house, and the forecourt to the NW. A l9th-century tracing by Octavius Morgan of a plan of the house and gardens as they were in the early l7th century, before William Morgan&#39;s rebuilding, shows approximately the same layout of compartments, with a forecourt and &#39;great bowling greene&#39; on the NW side, a &#39;wilderness or maze&#39; roughly where the Orangery garden now is, a square garden with &#39;the great walke about&#39; around it, laid out with a formal parterre, roughly where the &#39;Cedar Garden&#39; now is, and the southern compartment laid out as orchard. To the SW were two further large orchards. The present brick walls (except for those newly built around the outer court on the NW side) date from the late l7th or early l8th century, as do the Edney Gates, the stable block and the orangery which are incorporated into the layout. It has been discovered by excavation (De Bois Landscape Group, l99l) that the Orangery Garden was laid out, probably c. l720, with an elaborate arrangement of paths, flowerbeds and two inorganic parterres in front of the orangery.<br />\nThe next phase of development was in the late l8th century, when Adam Mickle supervised the landscaping of the park. At this stage, probably in the late l780s, a thick layer of soil from the dredging of the lake was deposited over the Orangery Garden, thus preserving the early l8th-century layout, which has now been excavated. The present layout of the Cedar Garden and the southern compartment dates largely from this period. The gardens are said to have been laid out by a Mr Walker, who was head gardener from l782 to l80l, using newly imported American species. Extensive hot-houses were built in l79l, but these have entirely disappeared, and their exact original location is not known.<br />\nAlterations were made to the NE front of the house in the l870s, with the opening up of the central door, the making of the drive, and the planting of the screen of trees. A fountain was added to the centre of the outer court in front of the stable block some time in the l9th century (now gone). The sunken Italianate garden dates from the l920s, and has been recently restored.<br />\nSTRUCTURAL COMPONENTS<br />\nThe Outer Court<br />\nThis is divided in two (E-W) by the Edney Gates and gatescreen. The inner compartment has been restored with four large grass squares and wide gravel paths (similar to those in the Thomas Dineley sketch of the Outer Court, before the Edney Gates, done in l684). The outer compartment has been restored with its original layout of cobbled cross paths, a central cobbled oval, and gravel.<br />\nThe walled gardens<br />\nNo structural features remain visible in the northernmost compartment.<br />\nAll the following paths and other features are thought to be of late l7th-early l8th century date.<br />\nIn the Orangery Garden, the next compartment to the south, the main structural feature visible is the central gravel path which runs NW-SE down the central axis. In the W corner there is a small mound which may be the remains of a viewing platform. In front of the orangery is a slightly raised terrace.<br />\nThe next compartment, the Cedar Garden, has a wide gravel path continuing the central axis of the walled gardens, and a wide perimeter path around the wall borders (recently restored). The central axis is continued in the southernmost compartment with a raised, grassed over path.<br />\nThe north-east front<br />\nThe main drive, of gravel, enters the gardens NE of the house on their NW side. It sweeps round in a large turning circle in front of the house. The Italian Garden to the E is sunken, with stepped sides. It is divided into four by stone flagged paths.<br />\n<br />\nBUILT COMPONENTS<br />\nOuter Court<br />\nThe two compartments of the Outer Court are surrounded by brick walls, the house and the stable block. The walls of the outer compartment are modern. Dividing the area in two are the &#39;Edney&#39; gates and gatescreen. These were installed in l7l3-l8, built for John Morgan by th</p>\n', 'nodegroup_id': '11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}], 'dates': [{'date': 19970627, 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'nodeid': '5e5d39f4-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'date': 20061107, 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'nodeid': 'a0788be0-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'date': 19970627, 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'nodeid': '5e5d39f4-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'date': 20220223, 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'nodeid': 'a0788be0-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}], 'domains': [{'label': 'Coedkernew', 'conceptid': '61e2bd42-d5d3-4716-b921-2f1d7b834dc7', 'valueid': '2df33bfd-ddde-426d-81c4-e53d8a1c20fb', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f3d-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'Monmouthshire', 'conceptid': 'fd039d44-49f7-465f-9efc-588795f3cdb7', 'valueid': '375d64bf-1434-4e2e-9eef-ae755eae0f34', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f61-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'Newport', 'conceptid': '95cdeb8d-0568-4e38-98cf-cbedf9f9abaa', 'valueid': '62731377-53f0-43f6-a426-f5eada08fdba', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f46-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'Grid reference taken at a central point within extensive site or complex', 'conceptid': '7ed5175f-16c2-4bb6-99ac-333331e1df7b', 'valueid': '9927bd78-9c99-47f1-9feb-9cc361fe0f16', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'COUNTRY HOUSE GARDEN', 'conceptid': 'c38ec122-d8d7-3626-a23f-5995fb981e6c', 'valueid': '84a97f23-a0f3-4e8e-8dc4-903fa8462db6', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'No Preference', 'conceptid': 'bdf4c70c-6a13-4cd0-a33f-33241d81302e', 'valueid': '078f