86d704': 'f6c80c3a-0352-4d89-8a00-ed6bb9fa7d2a'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': '43e3979f-52d8-4a0e-bfb3-8ac673475463', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('5a0b7254-d150-4160-9b90-96bd60c85186'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'46bf8db2-a005-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '54496ff2-4693-4138-ba03-277eff940f11', '60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '4356a4e8-73c9-46b6-8d56-46d351b3ffde'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': 'b44f5e28-3375-4dc4-ae48-0837e11b5921', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('5a0b7254-d150-4160-9b90-96bd60c85186'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '26 IX 00  <br>                                                      Aberglasney<br>                                                      by<br>                                           C. Stephen Briggs,<br>Royal Commission on Ancient and Historical Monuments (Wales) 1<br>Contents<br>INTRODUCTION: The Recent History and Management of House and Estate; Objectives of Research; The Aberglasney Restoration Trust (ART)<br>SITE  HISTORY: the Evidence for a Nunnery; Aberglasney: Home of Rhydderch?; The Rudds; The Dyers; The Nabob Philipps Family; Aberglasney and Grongar in Topographical History; Illustrations; Map Evidence.<br>SITE DESCRIPTION: The Walled Garden Complex; The Parapet Walkway and \'Cloister Enclosure\'; \'Pond Garden\';  Parkland Plantings; The Yew Tunnel. <br>LANDSCAPE THEORY AND HISTORICAL INTERPRETATION: \'The Most Important Formal Garden in Wales\'?; The Poems Grongar Hill and Country Walk as Topographical Evidence<br>DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSIONS: A Proposed Sequence for Farm and Estate Layout;  Prospect.<br>Appendices:<br>1.\tThe Excavations 1961-2 (G.R.Jones2 and W.B.Morris);<br>2.\tThe Gatehouse  (D.J.Roberts and R.F.Suggett1);<br>3.  The Trees and Shrubs in 1994 (I. and J.Stokes3);<br>4.  Dendrochronology: Tree-ring dating of building timbers, the Yew Tunnel and other trees at Aberglasney (D.W.H.Miles 4and M.C.Bridge5);<br>5.\tListing Details of the Walkway and Gatehouse  from Cadw,6 1990.<br>6.\tThe 1991 Geophysical Survey (summary from J.Gater7 et al).<br>1.Crown Building, Plas Crug, Aberystwyth SY23 1NJ<br>2.6 Balvicar Drive, Queen\'s Park, Glasgow G42 8QS<br>3.Pantcoch Cottage, Carmel, Llanelli, Carmarthenshire SA14 7SG<br>4. Oxford Dendrochronology Laboratory, Mill Farm, Mapledurham,  So. Oxon, RG4 7TX<br>5.Institute of Archaeology, University College London, 31-34 Gordon Square London,WC1H 0PY<br>6.Cadw: Welsh Historic Monuments, Government Buildings, The Welsh Assembly, Cathays Park, Cardiff CF1 <br>7.Geophysical Surveys of Bradford, 12 Reservoir View, Thornton, Bradford BD13 3NT<br>                                                                 Aberglasney: The Theory,  History and Archaeology of a Post-Medieval landscape<br>                                                                   by      C.Stephen Briggs, <br>[We hope] ..\'to encourage amongst the experts..criticism and discussion, so that we may possibly be able to discover some truths which are now buried in the almost impenetrable darkness of past ages\'. <br>Abstract<br>Printed and documentary sources, archaeological excavation, dendrochronology and geophysical survey are employed to investigate the history of Aberglasney, a small country house near Llandeilo, Carmarthenshire (SN 5815 2213). Traditions about its garden layout, parapet walkway, a gatehouse and a yew \'tunnel\' are examined. Circa 1600, Bishop Anthony Rudd (1549-1615) probably built a \'cwrt\' enclosure aligned north-south. The gatehouse belonged to this or a later house. In 1770 or later, the Dyer family re-built the house, probably re-designed and re-walled the entire estate layout, when an earlier farm building was converted into the parapet walkway and stock pens, later known as the \'cloistered court\'. In Victorian times this feature, originally a farmyard, became a pleasure garden, part of a typical Georgian-Victorian complex including a kitchen garden, glasshouses, orchards and fishpond. A yew grove was established, most likely c 1805, when the Philipps family began planting in a picturesque style. Abandoned c 1950, house and garden became dilapidated. The site is now the object of a radical development programme, involving inter alia the stabilisation of all buildings fabric, and imposing sixteenth-seventeenth century style formal gardens over the 18th-19th century kitchen garden and orchard.      <br>                                             INTRODUCTION <br>The Recent History and Management of House and Estate<br>An important outcome of the establishment of a Welsh Historic Gardens Trust in 1989 was the attention it initially focused upon the historic estates around Llandeilo (FIG.1).  Some, like Dynevor (Dinefwr) Castle  and Golden Grove (Gelli Aur),  were already familiar to historians and scholars of the Welsh landscape. Among the less well-known were Aberglasney and Middleton,  the latter bereft of its house - lost by fire - nevertheless still an interesting eighteenth century parkland estate, its site now occupied by the Welsh National Botanical Garden.  These all lie in the Tywi (Towy) Valley,  an area particularly well-endowed with picturesque properties and peppered in farmsteads with medieval or post-medieval tenurial interest. <br>Probably of medieval origin, Aberglasney passed successively from the Rudds (c 1600); to the Dyers from 1710; then from 1803 to the Phillipps family. Importantly, John Dyer the poet (1699-1757) spent some of his boyhood there. As with many landed estates, its agricultural basis long since retreated and activities shrank to nucleate around core buildings. The conventional accoutrements of a Victorian gentleman\'s residence remained in 1990 (FIG.2): estate cottages, lodge and farmbuildings, decaying glasshouses, overgrown kennels and aviaries. <br>Set in about 8 acres [c 3.5 ha] of partly wooded grounds, Aberglasney, although abandoned and in poor repair (Pl.1), is Listed Grade II* (since re-Listing in 1990). It lies in the parish of Llangathen  Near the house stand three unusual features: a stone-built \'arched walkway\'(henceforth the parapet walk) now enclosing an area recently named \'the cloister garden\', also Grade II*, and a post-medieval gatehouse. This gatehouse and associated estate buildings are protected Grade II for Group Value. The third unusual feature is a tunnel formed of yew, planted in front of the eighteenth century (north-facing) facade of the house.  <br>Lying within a relatively unspoilt rural settlement, overlooked by Grongar Hill, the site was in 1995 incorporated into Llangathen Village Conservation Area by Dinefwr District (now Carmarthen County) Council. By then, several nineteenth century estate buildings had been converted to residential use and a comprehensive Tree Preservation Order, served by the Council in 1984 covered a variety of interesting, if not of specimen trees (see Appendix 3), some then hiding the decayed house from general view.  <br>Objectives of Research<br>The present writer first routinely examined all available maps and documents for the Welsh Historic Gardens Trust,   around 1990. The stimulus to enquire further came from the paper given by Prof. J.Dixon Hunt at the Trust\'s inaugural Conference in April 1990. <br>Dixon Hunt\'s paper examined two main traditions: the first, a prospective and retrospective of John Dyer\'s poems Grongar Hill and Country Walk. It proposed Dyer\'s texts as literal topographical description for identifying specific eighteenth century landscape features around Grongar and Aberglasney. Dyer\'s lyrics were also explored to see how the neighbouring landscape might have been contrived to accommodate Dyer\'s literary legacy. <br>The second tradition concerned the origins and architectural history of Aberglasney\'s \'arched walkway\'. Acknowledging that at one time it had been ascribed a medieval ecclesiastical foundation, Dixon Hunt placed it within the European Renaissance Garden tradition, favour being found in a late sixteenth century derivation from Italy.  The integrity of this pedigree is partly dependent on the taste of Bishop Anthony Rudd, during whose incumbency at St Davids (1594-1614) the house and grounds were probably remodelled c.1600. The likelihood of Italian influence depends not so much on the popularity of Italian garden building fashions in Wales, but more on the testimony of Aberglasney\'s surviving fabric and ascertainable garden layout. <br>Current interpretation of the site is best summarised by Mr William Wilkins: Aberglasney is \'believed to be the only 17th century formal garden left in Wales and among only a handful in Britain\'  and \'although the present walls were built in the 1590s, they may have followed the layout of the original gardens referred to by a 15th century bard\'.  This particular interpretation underlies regular reportage in the national broadsheets (since 1991),  publication of a popular book  and a site guide,  and a television series.  If historically demonstrable, such early features would indeed make this site extremely important to garden history.  Presentation of the fruits of the recent investigations and research undertaken by R.C.A.M.W. staff (and others) now offers an opportunity to explore this interpretation as well as other explanations of the site\'s history.. <br>In 1961, having scrutinised its apparent historical pedigree, Rhys Dafys Williams questioned current interpretations of Aberglasney\'s early history and concluded that enduring answers were unlikely without archaeological excavation.  The main strands of his argument are explained below. Unfortunately, however, Williams\'s analysis was overlooked in Dixon Hunt\'s influential paper, and the arguments which accompanied the walkway\'s up-graded status in Cadw\'s re-Listing Schedule only three months after the 1990 conference were also referenced without respect to Williams. <br>The Aberglasney Restoration Trust<br>Although there was digging in the garden in 1961-2 (Appendix 1 and FIG.6) and again in 1991,   the site was to become a focus of intensive archaeological activity only in 1995. The events leading to this can be outlined briefly. During the early 1990s it became clear that unless radical steps were taken, house, buildings and nineteenth century plantings would be lost through neglect.  Following heightened media attention about the site\'s advancing decrepitude, a Trust (the Aberglasney Restoration Trust or ART) was formed in 1994 with the objectives of conserving the house shell, re-creating and opening the garden to the public, and restoring its buildings.<br>This proposal was to involve major landscape change resulting in extensive soil disturbances preparatory to building stabilisation and the introduction of imaginative new gardens. Due to its protected status and historical connections, the site obviously merited excavation  and full architectural record, requirements later to be recognised in the conditions attached to some project funding arrangements.<br>Although incomplete at the time of writing, four years of intermittent digging already offer important clues about the site\'s history. Archaeology, together with historical research, a re-appraisal of John Dyer\'s art  and a keener awareness of estate and garden evolution in Renaissance, Picturesque and Industrial Wales,  now assist a clearer evaluation of Aberglasney\'s development.<br>It is interesting that the present enquiry should comprehend not only Dyer\'s poems, which lie at the head of an eighteenth century Landscape Tradition, but also includes a fifteenth century Welsh bardic poem, one of many written in that genre, which is currently attracting scholarly attention.  <br>                                                                   SITE HISTORY<br>The evidence for a Nunnery<br>In his thorough treatment of the historical sources bearing upon Llangathen and its locality, Williams  posed the question: was Aberglasney of lay or ecclesiastical origin in late medieval times? Although neither parish nor house figures in the records of Talley Abbey, searches of the Court of Augmentations for lands disposed of from more distant monastic establishments have so far failed to reveal anything about the sale of Llangathen lands before 1593-4, when the lands of Dame Katherine Edgcumb, formerly of Rice Griffiths [Rhys ap Gruffydd], were disposed of to John Herbert, Jane his wife, and John his son by way of lease for a term of lives.  Williams quotes from Bacon\'s Liber Regis of 1786, which was printed in T.W.Barker\'s researches on Carmarthenshire Endowments.  It runs:<br>Llangathen alias Llan Galthen (St Michael and All Saints). NUNNERY OF CHESTER, Propr., Bishop of Chester, Impropriator and Patron. Yearly value #40. King\'s Books, #6/13/4.<br>Unfortunately, these Herbert lands were more probably of Cwrt Henry than of Aberglasney,  and the Chester Endowment is to Llangathen Church, so need not relate to this inquiry. Whereas it is possible that further medieval tenurial documentation may await discovery, having exhausted the known manuscript sources, Williams  observed that only excavation would more certainly reveal the foundations of \'this establishment granted by the Crown to Chester Nunnery in the 14th century\', before he concluded that searches of the Court of Augmentation Records for Cheshire might help resolve any question of ecclesiastical tenure. <br>Aberglasney: Home of Rhydderch?<br>Bardic poetry enjoyed an important currency in Wales during the later Middle Ages. Its descriptive potential for contemporary homes and landscapes was first explored by Enid Pierce Roberts  who drew attention to a number of properties. These included Mysoglen, Llanfair-y-Cwmwd (SH 44 66), described by Robert ap Ifan in the late sixteenth century; Bodorgan, Llangadwaladr, Anglesey (SH 3859 6736), found in a piece by Sion Brwynog of around 1550; Gwydir, Caernarfonshire (SH 7960 6104), where topiary was mentioned by Ifan Sion, and Plas-y-ward, Llanynys, Denbighshire (SJ 11 60), where a lady\'s garden was described by Simwnt Fychan. Unfortunately, the equation of these poetic descriptions with the known fabric or present topographies of the sites has met with very limited success. Furthermore, over 50,000 bardic poems are known encompassing the medieval period to the eighteenth century,  and their investigation is only now beginning. Nonetheless, many are known to describe courtly gardens and their settings. <br>These poems were metrically structured with descriptions composed to varying formulae. Consequently, equating descriptive detail with specific structural features is likely to be difficult, if not impossible, since the lyrics were imaginative and sometimes highly romanticised. When using evocative prose in any historical research, an appreciation of the writer\'s social standing can be as telling as can any analysis of his texts. Fields of artistic patronage do tend to lend themselves to the persuasive pen. (Though it is useful to note that there is, at least, one Welsh poem, of 1605, describing garden and house construction for Sir Harry Williams at (Old) Gwernyfed, Breconshire, details of which do bear real likeness to some of the surviving remains.) <br>One bard is believed to have described Aberglasney later in the fifteenth century.  Major Francis Jones demonstrated that Rhys ap Gruffydd ap Llywelyn Foethus begat Rhydderch through a descendent of the eleventh century ruler Elystan Glodrudd. Rhydderch lived at Llangathen during the later fifteenth century, and Jones felt there was little doubt that he had resided at Aberglasney.   Lewis Glyn Cothi (fl 1447-86) bestowed an ode upon Rhydderch, which, after extolling his wealth, wide learning, knowledge of the Scriptures and status as \'Pope of the land of Cetheiniawg\', proclaimed:<br>Iddo ef mae neuadd falch<br>Acun wengaer gam wyngalch<br>Ac o gylch ogylch i hon<br>Naw o arddau yn wrddion<br>Perllanwydd a gwinwydd gwyr<br>Derw ieuainc hyd yr awyr <br> <br>Johnson  suggests a translation as follows:<br>He has a proud hall, a fortress made bright by whitewash,<br>and encompassing it all around nine green gardens.<br>Orchard trees and crooked vines, young oaks reaching up to the skies.<br>Although an attractive proposition to equate these evocative lines with Aberglasney, having long ago reviewed their relevance, R.D.Williams dismissed them as insignificant to the site\'s history.  The places mentioned by Lewis Glyn Cothi are generally difficult to locate, and, from its topographical detail, it is possible that this poem was alluding to Cwrt Henry rather than Aberglasney.  Because his laudatory lyrics probably followed a regular formula, several sites may have shared similar word pictures. So whereas the nine gardens may have been a notional array of square or rectangular raised plots of the type excavated, for example, from a fifteenth-sixteenth century milieu at Haverfordwest Priory,   or at the possibly Cistercian monastic grange farm, Bwlch yr Oerfa in Ceredigion,  Lewis Glyn Cothi could have been enunciating a specific number for his patron to hear, a figure pregnant with hidden meaning or contemporary superstition. Besides demonstrating deftness in composition, their mystery and personal allusion gave these odes an entertaining and flattering edge. But they seldom provided reliable descriptions. <br>Whether or not Lewis Glyn Cothi\'s poem was alluding to Aberglasney, it might reasonably be argued that Lewis Glyn Cothi conveys some idea of how fifteenth century courtly houses of the Tywi Valley were meant to look at the time. <br>Whether or Aberglasney had been Rhydderch\'s home, his grandson, Sir William Thomas (c.1479-1542),  who became Sheriff of Carmarthenshire in 1541, certainly owned the Aberglasney estate. Rising to prominence after the fall of Dinefwr in 1531, he did well out of Henry VIII\'s later regime  becoming a notable figure locally and nationally.   The connection  between Sir William and the estate\'s eighteenth century occupants is fascinating, for John Dyer himself testified how his \'grandmother [had been] the daughter of Williams esqr. of Brinhavod in the parish of Langathen, descended from Sir William Thomas of Aberglasney who sold that house and estate to Bishop Rudd; \'twas then called Place Langathen\'.   This, then, usefully answers one part of R.D.Williams\'s question: whatever its tenurial complexities, Aberglasney, originally Plas Llangathen, appears to have been of lay origin. <br>The Rudds  <br>Bishop Anthony Rudd held the See of St Davids from 1594 to 1614  and Aberglasney was one of several properties he owned at the time of his death.  The exact date he acquired the place is unclear.  Among very limited evidence for Rudd\'s life is a biography compiled by Edward Yardley (1739-1779) for his Menevia Sacra. Yardley relates how Rudd was \'Buried at Llangathen Church in which he had purchased a good estate and built a handsome seat with a very decent chapel with a curious pulpit, ornamented with painted glass, called Aberglasney\'. <br>Significantly, by Rudd\'s time, the Bishop\'s Palace at St David\'s was abandoned and the new Protestant episcopate had already been half a century at Abergwili, east of Carmarthen. In common with his predecessor Marmaduke Middleton (who stated in 1583 that with but one exception all the bishop\'s houses were \'down to the ground\'), Rudd found the palaces so \'ruinous and decayed for want of reparation that they were not fit for any man, especially a bishop, to dwell and keep hospitality in\'.  Hardly surprising, then, is the general belief that Rudd had lived instead at Aberglasney.  Scant though the documentation is, it is nonetheless possible to rehearse other circumstances affecting the place whence Rudd might have best commuted to St David\'s. An initial pointer, coming from Francis Jones\'s research, explains how \'he paid attention to his official residence, spending considerable sums in restoring the episcopal palaces at Abergwili and St David\'s\'.  So both were probably made habitable, and he would have resided there particularly if, as seems likely, he had owned no property of status when he first came to the area. Here, some significance must attach to the fact that Abergwili is 14km. closer to St David\'s Cathedral than is Aberglasney.  <br>Another important issue is the date at which Rudd acquired Aberglasney. It seems unlikely \'that [acquisition] probably took place shortly after his elevation to the See\' (in 1594),  as time would have been required for him to accrue sufficient wealth with which to \'build up an estate for his children..by thrift and by leases of ecclesiastical property\'.  Unfortunately, the testimony of the tree-rings from the earliest roofing timbers on the site give too imprecise a felling date to place the first known building closer than c.1600 (Appendix 4), and it remains possible that that could have been erected by Rudd\'s predecessor. <br>Also significant to an understanding of his residency, was the existence of Rudd\'s impressive inventory of furnishings, decor and personalia, obviously housed away from Aberglasney, and presumably at Abergwili, for, in the will written shortly before his death, Rudd directed how specific items \'be carried to my house at Llangathen that my loveinge wife Anne Rudd may have the use and command of them during her natural life..\'  So although he chose burial in Llangathen,  it seems likely he had resided in a palace after taking up office, and had probably accepted conventional episcopal incumbency with residence at Abergwili Palace in spite of its appalling condition. <br>Whilst it has been acknowledged that there is no surviving evidence for plantings by Rudd and \'nothing of any use in clarifying if and when the Rudds created [the] garden spaces at Aberglasney has come to light in the archives\', the Rudds are nonetheless credited with having had sufficient \'resources and probably the vision to create what now remains\'.. during a period..\'for many reasons the most likely time for [the] garden terrace [i.e. the walkway] to have been established\'.   <br>Religious demands may well have influenced the design of an Elizabethan bishop\'s estate, official and private. Whereas courtiers and diplomats certainly travelled in Italy, absorbed and returned with Renaissance culture, Anglican clerics are less likely to have pursued the same course. In fact they more likely travelled in Germany.  <br>And then there is the problem of his palaces. Whether or not he resided at Aberglasney, would he have built a private garden to rival the Church\'s official residences, places he was obliged to repair at great expense? Furthermore, would an incumbent of the New Faith so overtly decorate the environs of his own farm with an icon of Renaissance culture, with its undertones of the Roman Faith? It seems unlikely.<br>Seeing Aberglasney as a future family seat, Anthony Rudd may have furnished it appropriately externally and internally. This would probably have meant laying out the site in a formal style, similarly to the new late sixteenth century offices created from the former monastic buildings at Brecon Priory.  He was succeeded at Aberglasney by his son Rice (1594-1664), created baronet in 1628, then by his great-grandson, also Rice Rudd (1643-1701).  By 1670, the house was being assessed at 30 hearths,  ranking alongside Golden Grove  as a major county seat. This hints at considerable growth from the size of place the Bishop is thought to have occupied (although no real details are available), and it seems inconceivable that by the later seventeenth century a mansion of such status would not have had a formal layout. As luck would have it, the Duke of Beaufort did not pass this way in 1684 when Thomas Dineley made idiosyncratic sketches of several important Welsh formal gardens and described, among others, Muddlescwm, Carmarthenshire. <br>Clues as to the possible whereabouts of this layout will be considered later.<br>The Dyers<br>The Dyers\' occupancy at Aberglasney has been dealt with in some detail by Green,  by Williams,  and in summary by Francis Jones.  It gave rise to the important connection with John Dyer, landscape artist and poet. <br>For the first part of the century, Aberglasney was bought and managed by Dyer\'s father, Robert (d.1710), followed by John\'s brother Robert (R.A.). R.A. was inept with money, and quit Llangathen in 1770, leaving his son William Herbert (born 1747) to sort out the mess. W.H.Dyer had no greater financial skills than his father, so he, too, staggered from one financial crisis to another until eventually obliged to sell up. <br>In 1776, W.H. was unfortunate to be chosen as High Sheriff of the county. A job not all willingly aspired to, it tended to incur immense costs in entertaining and peer group social obligation. Owing to his family\'s social ambitions on the one hand, and its debts on the other, Dyer should have been in a quandary about acceptance. But there was little choice in these matters and he would have taken on the job knowing how it would further challenge his pocket.<br>Holding office as Sheriff could be just one crippling pressure on the eighteenth century gentry. In the Dyers\' case their eventual sale of Aberglasney around 1800 probably resulted from an over-anxiety to be accepted among Carmarthenshire\'s county families. Of more than passing interest to the present argument: how they perceived their estate should have looked in contemporary style, may have been partly responsible for their financial ruin. <br>In this regard, tree-ring evidence from roofing timbers (Appendix 4) suggests that around 1770 the family undertook a major house re-building. How far they also re-designed the estate layout and introduced high-walled gardens and a new home farm facility in line with current fashion, is a matter for speculation. But such re-modelling could be anticipated during a period when the older gentry families of the Tywi Valley were already re-casting their parks\' core functions.   Dyer would also have been well aware of the radical agricultural changes being promoted by neighbouring gentry.  The establishment of a Carmarthenshire Agricultural Society on his doorstep in 1772   cannot have escaped his attention and might well have affected the way he conceived and ran his own business.<br>The influence Aberglasney had on John Dyer will be discussed below (OOO). <br>The Nabob Philipps family <br>In 1803 the estate was bought out by a certain Thomas Philipps who had made his money whilst a surgeon in India.  Apparently when Philipps acquired the property, it had been described in 1798 (on a poster printed by John Ross of Carmarthen) as having views, and being already with \'extensive outbuildings, a good garden, and excellent WATER at command\'.  Gulston\'s \'terras\'(see below p.000) was not then obviously a selling point. <br>During 1803-5 Philipps\' improvements included repairing a fishpond, tiling a pigeon house, making frames for hotbeds for the garden and buying young trees from Miller and Sweet, Bristol nurserymen. Although some of this construction and maintenance work is documented from accounts housed at the Carmarthenshire Record Office, it has not been found possible to verify in detail all the statements on gardening made by Francis Jones. However, Philipps\'s flurry of horticultural activity shows him to have been a man anxious to make a fashionable garden commensurate with his station, one offering visual amenity comparable to that of his neighbours.<br>Nor did this work stop in the garden. Philipps was also having walls built by Morgan John, Mason (bills of May 11th and 25th); John Jones, a Tyler, undertook \'New Tyling\' on the \'Mansion House, Gutters joining Kitchen, Roughcasting Chimneys, Library, Parapet Wall, Passage to Chapel, Dairy, Privy and Cott\'. Pointing was effected on the \'Pine End [of the] Mansion House, Cotts, Coach House, Kiln House, Kitchen, Passage, Dairy, Cellar, and Over the Door\'(September 21st 1805).  In November 1805 Philipps bought cherries, peaches, gooseberries, Dutch, white and black currants, and Antwerp, Beehive and red raspberries, again from Miller of Bristol (Miller-Philipps 1st November 1805).  <br>It is reasonable to suppose that the walled gardens had already been built by the Dyers, and that Philipps had acquired a bargain, off-the-peg, fully-fashioned gentleman\'s estate. This is borne out by the fact that Philipps\' bills relate more to maintenance than to new building activity, and the advert of 1798 actually boasts a full complement of garden and estate structures. <br>Certain other mid-nineteenth century Philipps estate documents do survive relating to two drainage projects on unspecified parcels of land.  But of greater interest is an advert about the estate in its Victorian heyday, which appeared in the Tenby Observer (for 1872) and described Aberglasney as:<br>A country Residence, with shooting over 1000 acres .. the house .. beautifully situate, in a sheltered position at the front of Grongar Hills nearly surrounded by park lands, and screened by forest trees and ornamental timber .. The Pleasure Grounds comprise a Croquet Lawn and Archery Ground, tastefully arranged Flower Beds and Borders, a three-sided Terrace Walk, constructed on the walls of an ancient fortress. There is a Vinery, 40 ft.[6.096m.] by 20 ft.[12.192m.], a large pinery, Melon Grounds, and 3 other Glass Houses, and an Orchard; the large walled Kitchen Garden is profusely stocked with choice fruit trees. The outbuildings comprise an enclosed Stable-yard and Farmery. The far-famed salmon river Towey is within a short distance.  <br>The apparent absence from this agent\'s list of a \'yew tunnel\' is noteworthy. The glass houses mentioned here are probably those which appeared on the First Edition 25-inch O.S. map in 1887 (FIG.4). All have now gone.<br>Aberglasney and Grongar in Topographical History<br>Without doubt, Dyer\'s poetry was always an important influence in attracting visitors to the area.  Grongar Hill, both poem and wooded eminence, were enthusiastically acclaimed by many passers-by taking Antiquarian and Picturesque Tours between c.1770 and 1820.  Grongar was part of a regular circuit which also included Newton (Dinefwr) and Dryslwyn castles. But it is curious that no tourists seem to have visited Aberglasney itself to investigate or confirm the Dyer connection, and even stranger that those with an eye to recording features of antiquarian interest are known to have frequented his home and be shown the buildings later vested with antiquity.  <br>By contrast, Grongar is continuously mentioned and illustrated throughout the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Numerous artists and writers came there to pay their homage, sharing Dyer\'s spiritual dream of an incipient Picturesque, if not just coming to imbibe for themselves the breathtaking views of local medieval castellated antiquity set amidst wood- and farmlands. None of this is surprising, for in the early nineteenth century, attractive popular tradition boasted how Dyer conceived his poem on the spot. By 1833, for example, subscribers to the Revd Samuel Lewis\'s Topographical Dictionary could confidently be shown the very tree beneath which Dyer\'s masterpiece was supposedly written. Furthermore, echoing (among others) Thomas Johnes of Hafod\'s erection of the Hafod Arms at the Devil\'s Bridge over forty years before, Aberglasney\'s \'proprietor [had] recently erected a neat and comfortable inn for the accommodation of visitors, who [were] attracted by the luxuriant and magnificent prospects for which the situation is celebrated, or by the remains of antiquity which are found in the vicinity\'. <br>The eighth edition of Black\'s Guide to South Wales of 1889,  endorsed Lewis, affording the site great prominence to Victorian visitors by exclaiming how Grongar Hill lay \'within sight of Golden Grove, on the opposite bank of the Towy\' before boasting how \'the view from this eminence is remarkably beautiful, and has been rendered famous by association with the history and writings of the poet Dyer, who was born in 1700, at..Aberglasney..[where]..the hawthorn under which he sat to contemplate the scene, and compose his admirable descriptive poem, is still shown. Burke says of this spot [Golden Grove, Grongar Hill or Aberglasney?], "No place do I remember in which the combinations of water, wood, mountain, and ruin assume such exquisite variety"\'. <br>Against this background, it was only natural that when the Cambrian Archaeological Association had its summer meeting at Llandeilo in 1855, members were conveyed to Aberglasney and Llangathen Church to be shown Bishop Rudd\'s important memorial.  Then again, in 1892, they acknowledged the Rudds\' significance to local history.  Curiously, however, in neither these, nor in any other references to Aberglasney, Dyers or Rudds, did the Cambrians note the existence of a curious gatehouse, arcade or yew tunnel.  These seem significant omissions for such an intellectually curious body, when it is recalled that the house was then an impressive, if not fashionable, gentleman\'s residence.  Could this omission be taken to indicate that at least some of these unusual features originated either within the recall of its current incumbents or of their '}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': {'16': {'value': {'11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '<p>This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Carmarthenshire XXXIII, sheet 14 (1906). Its main elements on that map include walled garden, carriage drive, woodland, parterres, greenhouse and a pond.</p>\n\n<p>26 IX 00<br />\nAberglasney<br />\nby<br />\nC. Stephen Briggs,<br />\nRoyal Commission on Ancient and Historical Monuments (Wales) 1<br />\nContents<br />\nINTRODUCTION: The Recent History and Management of House and Estate; Objectives of Research; The Aberglasney Restoration Trust (ART)<br />\nSITE HISTORY: the Evidence for a Nunnery; Aberglasney: Home of Rhydderch?; The Rudds; The Dyers; The Nabob Philipps Family; Aberglasney and Grongar in Topographical History; Illustrations; Map Evidence.<br />\nSITE DESCRIPTION: The Walled Garden Complex; The Parapet Walkway and &#39;Cloister Enclosure&#39;; &#39;Pond Garden&#39;; Parkland Plantings; The Yew Tunnel.<br />\nLANDSCAPE THEORY AND HISTORICAL INTERPRETATION: &#39;The Most Important Formal Garden in Wales&#39;?; The Poems Grongar Hill and Country Walk as Topographical Evidence<br />\nDISCUSSION AND CONCLUSIONS: A Proposed Sequence for Farm and Estate Layout; Prospect.<br />\nAppendices:<br />\n1. The Excavations 1961-2 (G.R.Jones2 and W.B.Morris);<br />\n2. The Gatehouse (D.J.Roberts and R.F.Suggett1);<br />\n3. The Trees and Shrubs in 1994 (I. and J.Stokes3);<br />\n4. Dendrochronology: Tree-ring dating of building timbers, the Yew Tunnel and other trees at Aberglasney (D.W.H.Miles 4and M.C.Bridge5);<br />\n5. Listing Details of the Walkway and Gatehouse from Cadw,6 1990.<br />\n6. The 1991 Geophysical Survey (summary from J.Gater7 et al).<br />\n1.Crown Building, Plas Crug, Aberystwyth SY23 1NJ<br />\n2.6 Balvicar Drive, Queen&#39;s Park, Glasgow G42 8QS<br />\n3.Pantcoch Cottage, Carmel, Llanelli, Carmarthenshire SA14 7SG<br />\n4. Oxford Dendrochronology Laboratory, Mill Farm, Mapledurham, So. Oxon, RG4 7TX<br />\n5.Institute of Archaeology, University College London, 31-34 Gordon Square London,WC1H 0PY<br />\n6.Cadw: Welsh Historic Monuments, Government Buildings, The Welsh Assembly, Cathays Park, Cardiff CF1<br />\n7.Geophysical Surveys of Bradford, 12 Reservoir View, Thornton, Bradford BD13 3NT<br />\nAberglasney: The Theory, History and Archaeology of a Post-Medieval landscape<br />\nby C.Stephen Briggs,<br />\n[We hope] ..&#39;to encourage amongst the experts..criticism and discussion, so that we may possibly be able to discover some truths which are now buried in the almost impenetrable darkness of past ages&#39;.<br />\nAbstract<br />\nPrinted and documentary sources, archaeological excavation, dendrochronology and geophysical survey are employed to investigate the history of Aberglasney, a small country house near Llandeilo, Carmarthenshire (SN 5815 2213). Traditions about its garden layout, parapet walkway, a gatehouse and a yew &#39;tunnel&#39; are examined. Circa 1600, Bishop Anthony Rudd (1549-1615) probably built a &#39;cwrt&#39; enclosure aligned north-south. The gatehouse belonged to this or a later house. In 1770 or later, the Dyer family re-built the house, probably re-designed and re-walled the entire estate layout, when an earlier farm building was converted into the parapet walkway and stock pens, later known as the &#39;cloistered court&#39;. In Victorian times this feature, originally a farmyard, became a pleasure garden, part of a typical Georgian-Victorian complex including a kitchen garden, glasshouses, orchards and fishpond. A yew grove was established, most likely c 1805, when the Philipps family began planting in a picturesque style. Abandoned c 1950, house and garden became dilapidated. The site is now the object of a radical development programme, involving inter alia the stabilisation of all buildings fabric, and imposing sixteenth-seventeenth century style formal gardens over the 18th-19th century kitchen garden and orchard.<br />\nINTRODUCTION<br />\nThe Recent History and Management of House and Estate<br />\nAn important outcome of the establishment of a Welsh Historic Gardens Trust in 1989 was the attention it initially focused upon the historic estates around Llandeilo (FIG.1). Some, like Dynevor (Dinefwr) Castle and Golden Grove (Gelli Aur), were already familiar to historians and scholars of the Welsh landscape. Among the less well-known were Aberglasney and Middleton, the latter bereft of its house - lost by fire - nevertheless still an interesting eighteenth century parkland estate, its site now occupied by the Welsh National Botanical Garden. These all lie in the Tywi (Towy) Valley, an area particularly well-endowed with picturesque properties and peppered in farmsteads with medieval or post-medieval tenurial interest.<br />\nProbably of medieval origin, Aberglasney passed successively from the Rudds (c 1600); to the Dyers from 1710; then from 1803 to the Phillipps family. Importantly, John Dyer the poet (1699-1757) spent some of his boyhood there. As with many landed estates, its agricultural basis long since retreated and activities shrank to nucleate around core buildings. The conventional accoutrements of a Victorian gentleman&#39;s residence remained in 1990 (FIG.2): estate cottages, lodge and farmbuildings, decaying glasshouses, overgrown kennels and aviaries.<br />\nSet in about 8 acres [c 3.5 ha] of partly wooded grounds, Aberglasney, although abandoned and in poor repair (Pl.1), is Listed Grade II* (since re-Listing in 1990). It lies in the parish of Llangathen Near the house stand three unusual features: a stone-built &#39;arched walkway&#39;(henceforth the parapet walk) now enclosing an area recently named &#39;the cloister garden&#39;, also Grade II*, and a post-medieval gatehouse. This gatehouse and associated estate buildings are protected Grade II for Group Value. The third unusual feature is a tunnel formed of yew, planted in front of the eighteenth century (north-facing) facade of the house.<br />\nLying within a relatively unspoilt rural settlement, overlooked by Grongar Hill, the site was in 1995 incorporated into Llangathen Village Conservation Area by Dinefwr District (now Carmarthen County) Council. By then, several nineteenth century estate buildings had been converted to residential use and a comprehensive Tree Preservation Order, served by the Council in 1984 covered a variety of interesting, if not of specimen trees (see Appendix 3), some then hiding the decayed house from general view.<br />\nObjectives of Research<br />\nThe present writer first routinely examined all available maps and documents for the Welsh Historic Gardens Trust, around 1990. The stimulus to enquire further came from the paper given by Prof. J.Dixon Hunt at the Trust&#39;s inaugural Conference in April 1990.<br />\nDixon Hunt&#39;s paper examined two main traditions: the first, a prospective and retrospective of John Dyer&#39;s poems Grongar Hill and Country Walk. It proposed Dyer&#39;s texts as literal topographical description for identifying specific eighteenth century landscape features around Grongar and Aberglasney. Dyer&#39;s lyrics were also explored to see how the neighbouring landscape might have been contrived to accommodate Dyer&#39;s literary legacy.<br />\nThe second tradition concerned the origins and architectural history of Aberglasney&#39;s &#39;arched walkway&#39;. Acknowledging that at one time it had been ascribed a medieval ecclesiastical foundation, Dixon Hunt placed it within the European Renaissance Garden tradition, favour being found in a late sixteenth century derivation from Italy. The integrity of this pedigree is partly dependent on the taste of Bishop Anthony Rudd, during whose incumbency at St Davids (1594-1614) the house and grounds were probably remodelled c.1600. The likelihood of Italian influence depends not so much on the popularity of Italian garden building fashions in Wales, but more on the testimony of Aberglasney&#39;s surviving fabric and ascertainable garden layout.<br />\nCurrent interpretation of the site is best summarised by Mr William Wilkins: Aberglasney is &#39;believed to be the only 17th century formal garden left in Wales and among only a handful in Britain&#39; and &#39;although the present walls were built in the 1590s, they may have followed the layout of the original gardens referred to by a 15th century bard&#39;. This particular interpretation underlies regular reportage in the national broadsheets (since 1991), publication of a popular book and a site guide, and a television series. If historically demonstrable, such early features would indeed make this site extremely important to garden history. Presentation of the fruits of the recent investigations and research undertaken by R.C.A.M.W. staff (and others) now offers an opportunity to explore this interpretation as well as other explanations of the site&#39;s history..<br />\nIn 1961, having scrutinised its apparent historical pedigree, Rhys Dafys Williams questioned current interpretations of Aberglasney&#39;s early history and concluded that enduring answers were unlikely without archaeological excavation. The main strands of his argument are explained below. Unfortunately, however, Williams&#39;s analysis was overlooked in Dixon Hunt&#39;s influential paper, and the arguments which accompanied the walkway&#39;s up-graded status in Cadw&#39;s re-Listing Schedule only three months after the 1990 conference were also referenced without respect to Williams.<br />\nThe Aberglasney Restoration Trust<br />\nAlthough there was digging in the garden in 1961-2 (Appendix 1 and FIG.6) and again in 1991, the site was to become a focus of intensive archaeological activity only in 1995. The events leading to this can be outlined briefly. During the early 1990s it became clear that unless radical steps were taken, house, buildings and nineteenth century plantings would be lost through neglect. Following heightened media attention about the site&#39;s advancing decrepitude, a Trust (the Aberglasney Restoration Trust or ART) was formed in 1994 with the objectives of conserving the house shell, re-creating and opening the garden to the public, and restoring its buildings.<br />\nThis proposal was to involve major landscape change resulting in extensive soil disturbances preparatory to building stabilisation and the introduction of imaginative new gardens. Due to its protected status and historical connections, the site obviously merited excavation and full architectural record, requirements later to be recognised in the conditions attached to some project funding arrangements.<br />\nAlthough incomplete at the time of writing, four years of intermittent digging already offer important clues about the site&#39;s history. Archaeology, together with historical research, a re-appraisal of John Dyer&#39;s art and a keener awareness of estate and garden evolution in Renaissance, Picturesque and Industrial Wales, now assist a clearer evaluation of Aberglasney&#39;s development.<br />\nIt is interesting that the present enquiry should comprehend not only Dyer&#39;s poems, which lie at the head of an eighteenth century Landscape Tradition, but also includes a fifteenth century Welsh bardic poem, one of many written in that genre, which is currently attracting scholarly attention.<br />\nSITE HISTORY<br />\nThe evidence for a Nunnery<br />\nIn his thorough treatment of the historical sources bearing upon Llangathen and its locality, Williams posed the question: was Aberglasney of lay or ecclesiastical origin in late medieval times? Although neither parish nor house figures in the records of Talley Abbey, searches of the Court of Augmentations for lands disposed of from more distant monastic establishments have so far failed to reveal anything about the sale of Llangathen lands before 1593-4, when the lands of Dame Katherine Edgcumb, formerly of Rice Griffiths [Rhys ap Gruffydd], were disposed of to John Herbert, Jane his wife, and John his son by way of lease for a term of lives. Williams quotes from Bacon&#39;s Liber Regis of 1786, which was printed in T.W.Barker&#39;s researches on Carmarthenshire Endowments. It runs:<br />\nLlangathen alias Llan Galthen (St Michael and All Saints). NUNNERY OF CHESTER, Propr., Bishop of Chester, Impropriator and Patron. Yearly value #40. King&#39;s Books, #6/13/4.<br />\nUnfortunately, these Herbert lands were more probably of Cwrt Henry than of Aberglasney, and the Chester Endowment is to Llangathen Church, so need not relate to this inquiry. Whereas it is possible that further medieval tenurial documentation may await discovery, having exhausted the known manuscript sources, Williams observed that only excavation would more certainly reveal the foundations of &#39;this establishment granted by the Crown to Chester Nunnery in the 14th century&#39;, before he concluded that searches of the Court of Augmentation Records for Cheshire might help resolve any question of ecclesiastical tenure.<br />\nAberglasney: Home of Rhydderch?<br />\nBardic poetry enjoyed an important currency in Wales during the later Middle Ages. Its descriptive potential for contemporary homes and landscapes was first explored by Enid Pierce Roberts who drew attention to a number of properties. These included Mysoglen, Llanfair-y-Cwmwd (SH 44 66), described by Robert ap Ifan in the late sixteenth century; Bodorgan, Llangadwaladr, Anglesey (SH 3859 6736), found in a piece by Sion Brwynog of around 1550; Gwydir, Caernarfonshire (SH 7960 6104), where topiary was mentioned by Ifan Sion, and Plas-y-ward, Llanynys, Denbighshire (SJ 11 60), where a lady&#39;s garden was described by Simwnt Fychan. Unfortunately, the equation of these poetic descriptions with the known fabric or present topographies of the sites has met with very limited success. Furthermore, over 50,000 bardic poems are known encompassing the medieval period to the eighteenth century, and their investigation is only now beginning. Nonetheless, many are known to describe courtly gardens and their settings.<br />\nThese poems were metrically structured with descriptions composed to varying formulae. Consequently, equating descriptive detail with specific structural features is likely to be difficult, if not impossible, since the lyrics were imaginative and sometimes highly romanticised. When using evocative prose in any historical research, an appreciation of the writer&#39;s social standing can be as telling as can any analysis of his texts. Fields of artistic patronage do tend to lend themselves to the persuasive pen. (Though it is useful to note that there is, at least, one Welsh poem, of 1605, describing garden and house construction for Sir Harry Williams at (Old) Gwernyfed, Breconshire, details of which do bear real likeness to some of the surviving remains.)<br />\nOne bard is believed to have described Aberglasney later in the fifteenth century. Major Francis Jones demonstrated that Rhys ap Gruffydd ap Llywelyn Foethus begat Rhydderch through a descendent of the eleventh century ruler Elystan Glodrudd. Rhydderch lived at Llangathen during the later fifteenth century, and Jones felt there was little doubt that he had resided at Aberglasney. Lewis Glyn Cothi (fl 1447-86) bestowed an ode upon Rhydderch, which, after extolling his wealth, wide learning, knowledge of the Scriptures and status as &#39;Pope of the land of Cetheiniawg&#39;, proclaimed:<br />\nIddo ef mae neuadd falch<br />\nAcun wengaer gam wyngalch<br />\nAc o gylch ogylch i hon<br />\nNaw o arddau yn wrddion<br />\nPerllanwydd a gwinwydd gwyr<br />\nDerw ieuainc hyd yr awyr<br />\n<br />\nJohnson suggests a translation as follows:<br />\nHe has a proud hall, a fortress made bright by whitewash,<br />\nand encompassing it all around nine green gardens.<br />\nOrchard trees and crooked vines, young oaks reaching up to the skies.<br />\nAlthough an attractive proposition to equate these evocative lines with Aberglasney, having long ago reviewed their relevance, R.D.Williams dismissed them as insignificant to the site&#39;s history. The places mentioned by Lewis Glyn Cothi are generally difficult to locate, and, from its topographical detail, it is possible that this poem was alluding to Cwrt Henry rather than Aberglasney. Because his laudatory lyrics probably followed a regular formula, several sites may have shared similar word pictures. So whereas the nine gardens may have been a notional array of square or rectangular raised plots of the type excavated, for example, from a fifteenth-sixteenth century milieu at Haverfordwest Priory, or at the possibly Cistercian monastic grange farm, Bwlch yr Oerfa in Ceredigion, Lewis Glyn Cothi could have been enunciating a specific number for his patron to hear, a figure pregnant with hidden meaning or contemporary superstition. Besides demonstrating deftness in composition, their mystery and personal allusion gave these odes an entertaining and flattering edge. But they seldom provided reliable descriptions.<br />\nWhether or not Lewis Glyn Cothi&#39;s poem was alluding to Aberglasney, it might reasonably be argued that Lewis Glyn Cothi conveys some idea of how fifteenth century courtly houses of the Tywi Valley were meant to look at the time.<br />\nWhether or Aberglasney had been Rhydderch&#39;s home, his grandson, Sir William Thomas (c.1479-1542), who became Sheriff of Carmarthenshire in 1541, certainly owned the Aberglasney estate. Rising to prominence after the fall of Dinefwr in 1531, he did well out of Henry VIII&#39;s later regime becoming a notable figure locally and nationally. The connection between Sir William and the estate&#39;s eighteenth century occupants is fascinating, for John Dyer himself testified how his &#39;grandmother [had been] the daughter of Williams esqr. of Brinhavod in the parish of Langathen, descended from Sir William Thomas of Aberglasney who sold that house and estate to Bishop Rudd; &#39;twas then called Place Langathen&#39;. This, then, usefully answers one part of R.D.Williams&#39;s question: whatever its tenurial complexities, Aberglasney, originally Plas Llangathen, appears to have been of lay origin.<br />\nThe Rudds<br />\nBishop Anthony Rudd held the See of St Davids from 1594 to 1614 and Aberglasney was one of several properties he owned at the time of his death. The exact date he acquired the place is unclear. Among very limited evidence for Rudd&#39;s life is a biography compiled by Edward Yardley (1739-1779) for his Menevia Sacra. Yardley relates how Rudd was &#39;Buried at Llangathen Church in which he had purchased a good estate and built a handsome seat with a very decent chapel with a curious pulpit, ornamented with painted glass, called Aberglasney&#39;.<br />\nSignificantly, by Rudd&#39;s time, the Bishop&#39;s Palace at St David&#39;s was abandoned and the new Protestant episcopate had already been half a century at Abergwili, east of Carmarthen. In common with his predecessor Marmaduke Middleton (who stated in 1583 that with but one exception all the bishop&#39;s houses were &#39;down to the ground&#39;), Rudd found the palaces so &#39;ruinous and decayed for want of reparation that they were not fit for any man, especially a bishop, to dwell and keep hospitality in&#39;. Hardly surprising, then, is the general belief that Rudd had lived instead at Aberglasney. Scant though the documentation is, it is nonetheless possible to rehearse other circumstances affecting the place whence Rudd might have best commuted to St David&#39;s. An initial pointer, coming from Francis Jones&#39;s research, explains how &#39;he paid attention to his official residence, spending considerable sums in restoring the episcopal palaces at Abergwili and St David&#39;s&#39;. So both were probably made habitable, and he would have resided there particularly if, as seems likely, he had owned no property of status when he first came to the area. Here, some significance must attach to the fact that Abergwili is 14km. closer to St David&#39;s Cathedral than is Aberglasney.<br />\nAnother important issue is the date at which Rudd acquired Aberglasney. It seems unlikely &#39;that [acquisition] probably took place shortly after his elevation to the See&#39; (in 1594), as time would have been required for him to accrue sufficient wealth with which to &#39;build up an estate for his children..by thrift and by leases of ecclesiastical property&#39;. Unfortunately, the testimony of the tree-rings from the earliest roofing timbers on the site give too imprecise a felling date to place the first known building closer than c.1600 (Appendix 4), and it remains possible that that could have been erected by Rudd&#39;s predecessor.<br />\nAlso significant to an understanding of his residency, was the existence of Rudd&#39;s impressive inventory of furnishings, decor and personalia, obviously housed away from Aberglasney, and presumably at Abergwili, for, in the will written shortly before his death, Rudd directed how specific items &#39;be carried to my house at Llangathen that my loveinge wife Anne Rudd may have the use and command of them during her natural life..&#39; So although he chose burial in Llangathen, it seems likely he had resided in a palace after taking up office, and had probably accepted conventional episcopal incumbency with residence at Abergwili Palace in spite of its appalling condition.<br />\nWhilst it has been acknowledged that there is no surviving evidence for plantings by Rudd and &#39;nothing of any use in clarifying if and when the Rudds created [the] garden spaces at Aberglasney has come to light in the archives&#39;, the Rudds are nonetheless credited with having had sufficient &#39;resources and probably the vision to create what now remains&#39;.. during a period..&#39;for many reasons the most likely time for [the] garden terrace [i.e. the walkway] to have been established&#39;.<br />\nReligious demands may well have influenced the design of an Elizabethan bishop&#39;s estate, official and private. Whereas courtiers and diplomats certainly travelled in Italy, absorbed and returned with Renaissance culture, Anglican clerics are less likely to have pursued the same course. In fact they more likely travelled in Germany.<br />\nAnd then there is the problem of his palaces. Whether or not he resided at Aberglasney, would he have built a private garden to rival the Church&#39;s official residences, places he was obliged to repair at great expense? Furthermore, would an incumbent of the New Faith so overtly decorate the environs of his own farm with an icon of Renaissance culture, with its undertones of the Roman Faith? It seems unlikely.<br />\nSeeing Aberglasney as a future family seat, Anthony Rudd may have furnished it appropriately externally and internally. This would probably have meant laying out the site in a formal style, similarly to the new late sixteenth century offices created from the former monastic buildings at Brecon Priory. He was succeeded at Aberglasney by his son Rice (1594-1664), created baronet in 1628, then by his great-grandson, also Rice Rudd (1643-1701). By 1670, the house was being assessed at 30 hearths, ranking alongside Golden Grove as a major county seat. This hints at considerable growth from the size of place the Bishop is thought to have occupied (although no real details are available), and it seems inconceivable that by the later seventeenth century a mansion of such status would not have had a formal layout. As luck would have it, the Duke of Beaufort did not pass this way in 1684 when Thomas Dineley made idiosyncratic sketches of several important Welsh formal gardens and described, among others, Muddlescwm, Carmarthenshire.<br />\nClues as to the possible whereabouts of this layout will be considered later.<br />\nThe Dyers<br />\nThe Dyers&#39; occupancy at Aberglasney has been dealt with in some detail by Green, by Williams, and in summary by Francis Jones. It gave rise to the important connection with John Dyer, landscape artist and poet.<br />\nFor the first part of the century, Aberglasney was bought and managed by Dyer&#39;s father, Robert (d.1710), followed by John&#39;s brother Robert (R.A.). R.A. was inept with money, and quit Llangathen in 1770, leaving his son William Herbert (born 1747) to sort out the mess. W.H.Dyer had no greater financial skills than his father, so he, too, staggered from one financial crisis to another until eventually obliged to sell up.<br />\nIn 1776, W.H. was unfortunate to be chosen as High Sheriff of the county. A job not all willingly aspired to, it tended to incur immense costs in entertaining and peer group social obligation. Owing to his family&#39;s social ambitions on the one hand, and its debts on the other, Dyer should have been in a quandary about acceptance. But there was little choice in these matters and he would have taken on the job knowing how it would further challenge his pocket.<br />\nHolding office as Sheriff could be just one crippling pressure on the eighteenth century gentry. In the Dyers&#39; case their eventual sale of Aberglasney around 1800 probably resulted from an over-anxiety to be accepted among Carmarthenshire&#39;s county families. Of more than passing interest to the present argument: how they perceived their estate should have looked in contemporary style, may have been partly responsible for their financial ruin.<br />\nIn this regard, tree-ring evidence from roofing timbers (Appendix 4) suggests that around 1770 the family undertook a major house re-building. How far they also re-designed the estate layout and introduced high-walled gardens and a new home farm facility in line with current fashion, is a matter for speculation. But such re-modelling could be anticipated during a period when the older gentry families of the Tywi Valley were already re-casting their parks&#39; core functions. Dyer would also have been well aware of the radical agricultural changes being promoted by neighbouring gentry. The establishment of a Carmarthenshire Agricultural Society on his doorstep in 1772 cannot have escaped his attention and might well have affected the way he conceived and ran his own business.<br />\nThe influence Aberglasney had on John Dyer will be discussed below (OOO).<br />\nThe Nabob Philipps family<br />\nIn 1803 the estate was bought out by a certain Thomas Philipps who had made his money whilst a surgeon in India. Apparently when Philipps acquired the property, it had been described in 1798 (on a poster printed by John Ross of Carmarthen) as having views, and being already with &#39;extensive outbuildings, a good garden, and excellent WATER at command&#39;. Gulston&#39;s &#39;terras&#39;(see below p.000) was not then obviously a selling point.<br />\nDuring 1803-5 Philipps&#39; improvements included repairing a fishpond, tiling a pigeon house, making frames for hotbeds for the garden and buying young trees from Miller and Sweet, Bristol nurserymen. Although some of this construction and maintenance work is documented from accounts housed at the Carmarthenshire Record Office, it has not been found possible to verify in detail all the statements on gardening made by Francis Jones. However, Philipps&#39;s flurry of horticultural activity shows him to have been a man anxious to make a fashionable garden commensurate with his station, one offering visual amenity comparable to that of his neighbours.<br />\nNor did this work stop in the garden. Philipps was also having walls built by Morgan John, Mason (bills of May 11th and 25th); John Jones, a Tyler, undertook &#39;New Tyling&#39; on the &#39;Mansion House, Gutters joining Kitchen, Roughcasting Chimneys, Library, Parapet Wall, Passage to Chapel, Dairy, Privy and Cott&#39;. Pointing was effected on the &#39;Pine End [of the] Mansion House, Cotts, Coach House, Kiln House, Kitchen, Passage, Dairy, Cellar, and Over the Door&#39;(September 21st 1805). In November 1805 Philipps bought cherries, peaches, gooseberries, Dutch, white and black currants, and Antwerp, Beehive and red raspberries, again from Miller of Bristol (Miller-Philipps 1st November 1805).<br />\nIt is reasonable to suppose that the walled gardens had already been built by the Dyers, and that Philipps had acquired a bargain, off-the-peg, fully-fashioned gentleman&#39;s estate. This is borne out by the fact that Philipps&#39; bills relate more to maintenance than to new building activity, and the advert of 1798 actually boasts a full complement of garden and estate structures.<br />\nCertain other mid-nineteenth century Philipps estate documents do survive relating to two drainage projects on unspecified parcels of land. But of greater interest is an advert about the estate in its Victorian heyday, which appeared in the Tenby Observer (for 1872) and described Aberglasney as:<br />\nA country Residence, with shooting over 1000 acres .. the house .. beautifully situate, in a sheltered position at the front of Grongar Hills nearly surrounded by park lands, and screened by forest trees and ornamental timber .. The Pleasure Grounds comprise a Croquet Lawn and Archery Ground, tastefully arranged Flower Beds and Borders, a three-sided Terrace Walk, constructed on the walls of an ancient fortress. There is a Vinery, 40 ft.[6.096m.] by 20 ft.[12.192m.], a large pinery, Melon Grounds, and 3 other Glass Houses, and an Orchard; the large walled Kitchen Garden is profusely stocked with choice fruit trees. The outbuildings comprise an enclosed Stable-yard and Farmery. The far-famed salmon river Towey is within a short distance.<br />\nThe apparent absence from this agent&#39;s list of a &#39;yew tunnel&#39; is noteworthy. The glass houses mentioned here are probably those which appeared on the First Edition 25-inch O.S. map in 1887 (FIG.4). All have now gone.<br />\nAberglasney and Grongar in Topographical History<br />\nWithout doubt, Dyer&#39;s poetry was always an important influence in attracting visitors to the area. Grongar Hill, both poem and wooded eminence, were enthusiastically acclaimed by many passers-by taking Antiquarian and Picturesque Tours between c.1770 and 1820. Grongar was part of a regular circuit which also included Newton (Dinefwr) and Dryslwyn castles. But it is curious that no tourists seem to have visited Aberglasney itself to investigate or confirm the Dyer connection, and even stranger that those with an eye to recording features of antiquarian interest are known to have frequented his home and be shown the buildings later vested with antiquity.<br />\nBy contrast, Grongar is continuously mentioned and illustrated throughout the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Numerous artists and writers came there to pay their homage, sharing Dyer&#39;s spiritual dream of an incipient Picturesque, if not just coming to imbibe for themselves the breathtaking views of local medieval castellated antiquity set amidst wood- and farmlands. None of this is surprising, for in the early nineteenth century, attractive popular tradition boasted how Dyer conceived his poem on the spot. By 1833, for example, subscribers to the Revd Samuel Lewis&#39;s Topographical Dictionary could confidently be shown the very tree beneath which Dyer&#39;s masterpiece was supposedly written. Furthermore, echoing (among others) Thomas Johnes of Hafod&#39;s erection of the Hafod Arms at the Devil&#39;s Bridge over forty years before, Aberglasney&#39;s &#39;proprietor [had] recently erected a neat and comfortable inn for the accommodation of visitors, who [were] attracted by the luxuriant and magnificent prospects for which the situation is celebrated, or by the remains of antiquity which are found in the vicinity&#39;.<br />\nThe eighth edition of Black&#39;s Guide to South Wales of 1889, endorsed Lewis, affording the site great prominence to Victorian visitors by exclaiming how Grongar Hill lay &#39;within sight of Golden Grove, on the opposite bank of the Towy&#39; before boasting how &#39;the view from this eminence is remarkably beautiful, and has been rendered famous by association with the history and writings of the poet Dyer, who was born in 1700, at..Aberglasney..[where]..the hawthorn under which he sat to contemplate the scene, and compose his admirable descriptive poem, is still shown. Burke says of this spot [Golden Grove, Grongar Hill or Aberglasney?], &quot;No place do I remember in which the combinations of water, wood, mountain, and ruin assume such exquisite variety&quot;&#39;.<br />\nAgainst this background, it was only natural that when the Cambrian Archaeological Association had its summer meeting at Llandeilo in 1855, members were conveyed to Aberglasney and Llangathen Church to be shown Bishop Rudd&#39;s important memorial. Then again, in 1892, they acknowledged the Rudds&#39; significance to local history. Curiously, however, in neither these, nor in any other references to Aberglasney, Dyers or Rudds, did the Cambrians note the existence of a curious gatehouse, arcade or yew tunnel. These seem significant omissions for such an intellectually curious body, when it is recalled that the house was then an impressive, if not fashionable, gentleman&#39;s residence. Could this omission be taken to indicate that at least some of these unusual features originated either within the recall of its current incumbents or of their</p>\n'}, 'action': 'update', 'status': 'review', 'reviewer': None, 'timestamp': '2022-06-09T10:55:07.245389Z', 'reviewtimestamp': None}}}, {'tileid': '4e317921-34b7-4dd1-b7ef-6cedd30d8028', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('5a0b7254-d150-4160-9b90-96bd60c85186'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '7e820b1d-61ed-4ac0-8e69-ae5ea7e6d39e', '92cc3b9c-a005-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '54496ff2-4693-4138-ba03-277eff940f11', 'af6d3fd4-a006-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '078fdac7-cb0c-4ab6-b252-0db193d97a9c'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': 'f5db32d0-2727-44ad-a709-46b26f5a2439', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('5a0b7254-d150-4160-9b90-96bd60c85186'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'Aberglasney, Garden, Llangathen', '60120f6f-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '30307785-4739-466f-9941-f85256fc9919'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': {'16': {'value': {'60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'Aberglasney Garden, Llangathen', '60120f6f-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '30307785-4739-466f-9941-f85256fc9919'}, 'action': 'update', 'status': 'review', 'reviewer': None, 'timestamp': '2022-06-09T10:36:57.633952Z', 'reviewtimestamp': None}}}], 'permissions': {'users_without_read_perm': [], 'users_without_edit_perm': [], 'users_without_delete_perm': [], 'users_with_no_access': []}, 'strings': [{'string': 'Llangathen', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f3d-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Carmarthenshire', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f61-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Carmarthenshire', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f46-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': "This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Carmarthenshire XXXIII, sheet  14 (1906). Its main elements on that map include walled garden, carriage drive, woodland, parterres, greenhouse and a pond.<br>\n<br>\nPrinted and documentary sources, archaeological excavation, dendrochronology and geophysical survey show that Aberglasney, a small country house was formerly a minor gentry farm, near Llandeilo, Carmarthenshire (SN 5815 2213). Traditions about its garden layout, parapet walkway, a gatehouse and a yew 'tunnel' have been scrutinised. Circa 1600, Bishop Anthony Rudd (1549-1615) probably built a 'cwrt' enclosure aligned north-south. The gatehouse may have belonged to this or a later house. In 1770 or later, the Dyer family re-built the house; probably later re-designing and re-walling the entire estate layout, when an earlier farm building was also converted into the 'parapet walkway' and spaces beneath which were originally stock pens. This farm building and its muckyard later became known as the 'cloistered court'. In Victorian times this feature became a pleasure garden, part of a typical Georgian-Victorian complex the complement of which included a kitchen garden, glasshouses, orchards and fishpond. According to dendrochrnology, a yew grove was established, c 1805 or later, when the Philipps family began planting in a picturesque style. Abandoned c 1950, house and garden became dilapidated. The site has been the object of a radical development programme since 1994, involving inter alia the stabilisation of all buildings fabric, and imposing modern, sixteenth-seventeenth century style formal gardens over the 18th-19th century kitchen garden and orchard. Although promotional literature for the site still (2002)  claims this garden has its origins in the 16th century, this claim is currently unsupported either archaeologically or historically.        <br>\nC.S.Briggs 13.07.05<br>\n", 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'English Description', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'SN5804522080', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'}, {'string': 'SN5822', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'SN52SE', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Grid reference taken at centre of site', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'NG', 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'CHN', 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'COUNTRY HOUSE GARDEN', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No Preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '10G', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f55-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f55-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'PGW(Dy)5(CAM)', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'PGW2*', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Cadw', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Documents', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '26 IX 00  <br>                                                      Aberglasney<br>                                                      by<br>                                           C. Stephen Briggs,<br>Royal Commission on Ancient and Historical Monuments (Wales) 1<br>Contents<br>INTRODUCTION: The Recent History and Management of House and Estate; Objectives of Research; The Aberglasney Restoration Trust (ART)<br>SITE  HISTORY: the Evidence for a Nunnery; Aberglasney: Home of Rhydderch?; The Rudds; The Dyers; The Nabob Philipps Family; Aberglasney and Grongar in Topographical History; Illustrations; Map Evidence.<br>SITE DESCRIPTION: The Walled Garden Complex; The Parapet Walkway and \'Cloister Enclosure\'; \'Pond Garden\';  Parkland Plantings; The Yew Tunnel. <br>LANDSCAPE THEORY AND HISTORICAL INTERPRETATION: \'The Most Important Formal Garden in Wales\'?; The Poems Grongar Hill and Country Walk as Topographical Evidence<br>DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSIONS: A Proposed Sequence for Farm and Estate Layout;  Prospect.<br>Appendices:<br>1.\tThe Excavations 1961-2 (G.R.Jones2 and W.B.Morris);<br>2.\tThe Gatehouse  (D.J.Roberts and R.F.Suggett1);<br>3.  The Trees and Shrubs in 1994 (I. and J.Stokes3);<br>4.  Dendrochronology: Tree-ring dating of building timbers, the Yew Tunnel and other trees at Aberglasney (D.W.H.Miles 4and M.C.Bridge5);<br>5.\tListing Details of the Walkway and Gatehouse  from Cadw,6 1990.<br>6.\tThe 1991 Geophysical Survey (summary from J.Gater7 et al).<br>1.Crown Building, Plas Crug, Aberystwyth SY23 1NJ<br>2.6 Balvicar Drive, Queen\'s Park, Glasgow G42 8QS<br>3.Pantcoch Cottage, Carmel, Llanelli, Carmarthenshire SA14 7SG<br>4. Oxford Dendrochronology Laboratory, Mill Farm, Mapledurham,  So. Oxon, RG4 7TX<br>5.Institute of Archaeology, University College London, 31-34 Gordon Square London,WC1H 0PY<br>6.Cadw: Welsh Historic Monuments, Government Buildings, The Welsh Assembly, Cathays Park, Cardiff CF1 <br>7.Geophysical Surveys of Bradford, 12 Reservoir View, Thornton, Bradford BD13 3NT<br>                                                                 Aberglasney: The Theory,  History and Archaeology of a Post-Medieval landscape<br>                                                                   by      C.Stephen Briggs, <br>[We hope] ..\'to encourage amongst the experts..criticism and discussion, so that we may possibly be able to discover some truths which are now buried in the almost impenetrable darkness of past ages\'. <br>Abstract<br>Printed and documentary sources, archaeological excavation, dendrochronology and geophysical survey are employed to investigate the history of Aberglasney, a small country house near Llandeilo, Carmarthenshire (SN 5815 2213). Traditions about its garden layout, parapet walkway, a gatehouse and a yew \'tunnel\' are examined. Circa 1600, Bishop Anthony Rudd (1549-1615) probably built a \'cwrt\' enclosure aligned north-south. The gatehouse belonged to this or a later house. In 1770 or later, the Dyer family re-built the house, probably re-designed and re-walled the entire estate layout, when an earlier farm building was converted into the parapet walkway and stock pens, later known as the \'cloistered court\'. In Victorian times this feature, originally a farmyard, became a pleasure garden, part of a typical Georgian-Victorian complex including a kitchen garden, glasshouses, orchards and fishpond. A yew grove was established, most likely c 1805, when the Philipps family began planting in a picturesque style. Abandoned c 1950, house and garden became dilapidated. The site is now the object of a radical development programme, involving inter alia the stabilisation of all buildings fabric, and imposing sixteenth-seventeenth century style formal gardens over the 18th-19th century kitchen garden and orchard.      <br>                                             INTRODUCTION <br>The Recent History and Management of House and Estate<br>An important outcome of the establishment of a Welsh Historic Gardens Trust in 1989 was the attention it initially focused upon the historic estates around Llandeilo (FIG.1).  Some, like Dynevor (Dinefwr) Castle  and Golden Grove (Gelli Aur),  were already familiar to historians and scholars of the Welsh landscape. Among the less well-known were Aberglasney and Middleton,  the latter bereft of its house - lost by fire - nevertheless still an interesting eighteenth century parkland estate, its site now occupied by the Welsh National Botanical Garden.  These all lie in the Tywi (Towy) Valley,  an area particularly well-endowed with picturesque properties and peppered in farmsteads with medieval or post-medieval tenurial interest. <br>Probably of medieval origin, Aberglasney passed successively from the Rudds (c 1600); to the Dyers from 1710; then from 1803 to the Phillipps family. Importantly, John Dyer the poet (1699-1757) spent some of his boyhood there. As with many landed estates, its agricultural basis long since retreated and activities shrank to nucleate around core buildings. The conventional accoutrements of a Victorian gentleman\'s residence remained in 1990 (FIG.2): estate cottages, lodge and farmbuildings, decaying glasshouses, overgrown kennels and aviaries. <br>Set in about 8 acres [c 3.5 ha] of partly wooded grounds, Aberglasney, although abandoned and in poor repair (Pl.1), is Listed Grade II* (since re-Listing in 1990). It lies in the parish of Llangathen  Near the house stand three unusual features: a stone-built \'arched walkway\'(henceforth the parapet walk) now enclosing an area recently named \'the cloister garden\', also Grade II*, and a post-medieval gatehouse. This gatehouse and associated estate buildings are protected Grade II for Group Value. The third unusual feature is a tunnel formed of yew, planted in front of the eighteenth century (north-facing) facade of the house.  <br>Lying within a relatively unspoilt rural settlement, overlooked by Grongar Hill, the site was in 1995 incorporated into Llangathen Village Conservation Area by Dinefwr District (now Carmarthen County) Council. By then, several nineteenth century estate buildings had been converted to residential use and a comprehensive Tree Preservation Order, served by the Council in 1984 covered a variety of interesting, if not of specimen trees (see Appendix 3), some then hiding the decayed house from general view.  <br>Objectives of Research<br>The present writer first routinely examined all available maps and documents for the Welsh Historic Gardens Trust,   around 1990. The stimulus to enquire further came from the paper given by Prof. J.Dixon Hunt at the Trust\'s inaugural Conference in April 1990. <br>Dixon Hunt\'s paper examined two main traditions: the first, a prospective and retrospective of John Dyer\'s poems Grongar Hill and Country Walk. It proposed Dyer\'s texts as literal topographical description for identifying specific eighteenth century landscape features around Grongar and Aberglasney. Dyer\'s lyrics were also explored to see how the neighbouring landscape might have been contrived to accommodate Dyer\'s literary legacy. <br>The second tradition concerned the origins and architectural history of Aberglasney\'s \'arched walkway\'. Acknowledging that at one time it had been ascribed a medieval ecclesiastical foundation, Dixon Hunt placed it within the European Renaissance Garden tradition, favour being found in a late sixteenth century derivation from Italy.  The integrity of this pedigree is partly dependent on the taste of Bishop Anthony Rudd, during whose incumbency at St Davids (1594-1614) the house and grounds were probably remodelled c.1600. The likelihood of Italian influence depends not so much on the popularity of Italian garden building fashions in Wales, but more on the testimony of Aberglasney\'s surviving fabric and ascertainable garden layout. <br>Current interpretation of the site is best summarised by Mr William Wilkins: Aberglasney is \'believed to be the only 17th century formal garden left in Wales and among only a handful in Britain\'  and \'although the present walls were built in the 1590s, they may have followed the layout of the original gardens referred to by a 15th century bard\'.  This particular interpretation underlies regular reportage in the national broadsheets (since 1991),  publication of a popular book  and a site guide,  and a television series.  If historically demonstrable, such early features would indeed make this site extremely important to garden history.  Presentation of the fruits of the recent investigations and research undertaken by R.C.A.M.W. staff (and others) now offers an opportunity to explore this interpretation as well as other explanations of the site\'s history.. <br>In 1961, having scrutinised its apparent historical pedigree, Rhys Dafys Williams questioned current interpretations of Aberglasney\'s early history and concluded that enduring answers were unlikely without archaeological excavation.  The main strands of his argument are explained below. Unfortunately, however, Williams\'s analysis was overlooked in Dixon Hunt\'s influential paper, and the arguments which accompanied the walkway\'s up-graded status in Cadw\'s re-Listing Schedule only three months after the 1990 conference were also referenced without respect to Williams. <br>The Aberglasney Restoration Trust<br>Although there was digging in the garden in 1961-2 (Appendix 1 and FIG.6) and again in 1991,   the site was to become a focus of intensive archaeological activity only in 1995. The events leading to this can be outlined briefly. During the early 1990s it became clear that unless radical steps were taken, house, buildings and nineteenth century plantings would be lost through neglect.  Following heightened media attention about the site\'s advancing decrepitude, a Trust (the Aberglasney Restoration Trust or ART) was formed in 1994 with the objectives of conserving the house shell, re-creating and opening the garden to the public, and restoring its buildings.<br>This proposal was to involve major landscape change resulting in extensive soil disturbances preparatory to building stabilisation and the introduction of imaginative new gardens. Due to its protected status and historical connections, the site obviously merited excavation  and full architectural record, requirements later to be recognised in the conditions attached to some project funding arrangements.<br>Although incomplete at the time of writing, four years of intermittent digging already offer important clues about the site\'s history. Archaeology, together with historical research, a re-appraisal of John Dyer\'s art  and a keener awareness of estate and garden evolution in Renaissance, Picturesque and Industrial Wales,  now assist a clearer evaluation of Aberglasney\'s development.<br>It is interesting that the present enquiry should comprehend not only Dyer\'s poems, which lie at the head of an eighteenth century Landscape Tradition, but also includes a fifteenth century Welsh bardic poem, one of many written in that genre, which is currently attracting scholarly attention.  <br>                                                                   SITE HISTORY<br>The evidence for a Nunnery<br>In his thorough treatment of the historical sources bearing upon Llangathen and its locality, Williams  posed the question: was Aberglasney of lay or ecclesiastical origin in late medieval times? Although neither parish nor house figures in the records of Talley Abbey, searches of the Court of Augmentations for lands disposed of from more distant monastic establishments have so far failed to reveal anything about the sale of Llangathen lands before 1593-4, when the lands of Dame Katherine Edgcumb, formerly of Rice Griffiths [Rhys ap Gruffydd], were disposed of to John Herbert, Jane his wife, and John his son by way of lease for a term of lives.  Williams quotes from Bacon\'s Liber Regis of 1786, which was printed in T.W.Barker\'s researches on Carmarthenshire Endowments.  It runs:<br>Llangathen alias Llan Galthen (St Michael and All Saints). NUNNERY OF CHESTER, Propr., Bishop of Chester, Impropriator and Patron. Yearly value #40. King\'s Books, #6/13/4.<br>Unfortunately, these Herbert lands were more probably of Cwrt Henry than of Aberglasney,  and the Chester Endowment is to Llangathen Church, so need not relate to this inquiry. Whereas it is possible that further medieval tenurial documentation may await discovery, having exhausted the known manuscript sources, Williams  observed that only excavation would more certainly reveal the foundations of \'this establishment granted by the Crown to Chester Nunnery in the 14th century\', before he concluded that searches of the Court of Augmentation Records for Cheshire might help resolve any question of ecclesiastical tenure. <br>Aberglasney: Home of Rhydderch?<br>Bardic poetry enjoyed an important currency in Wales during the later Middle Ages. Its descriptive potential for contemporary homes and landscapes was first explored by Enid Pierce Roberts  who drew attention to a number of properties. These included Mysoglen, Llanfair-y-Cwmwd (SH 44 66), described by Robert ap Ifan in the late sixteenth century; Bodorgan, Llangadwaladr, Anglesey (SH 3859 6736), found in a piece by Sion Brwynog of around 1550; Gwydir, Caernarfonshire (SH 7960 6104), where topiary was mentioned by Ifan Sion, and Plas-y-ward, Llanynys, Denbighshire (SJ 11 60), where a lady\'s garden was described by Simwnt Fychan. Unfortunately, the equation of these poetic descriptions with the known fabric or present topographies of the sites has met with very limited success. Furthermore, over 50,000 bardic poems are known encompassing the medieval period to the eighteenth century,  and their investigation is only now beginning. Nonetheless, many are known to describe courtly gardens and their settings. <br>These poems were metrically structured with descriptions composed to varying formulae. Consequently, equating descriptive detail with specific structural features is likely to be difficult, if not impossible, since the lyrics were imaginative and sometimes highly romanticised. When using evocative prose in any historical research, an appreciation of the writer\'s social standing can be as telling as can any analysis of his texts. Fields of artistic patronage do tend to lend themselves to the persuasive pen. (Though it is useful to note that there is, at least, one Welsh poem, of 1605, describing garden and house construction for Sir Harry Williams at (Old) Gwernyfed, Breconshire, details of which do bear real likeness to some of the surviving remains.) <br>One bard is believed to have described Aberglasney later in the fifteenth century.  Major Francis Jones demonstrated that Rhys ap Gruffydd ap Llywelyn Foethus begat Rhydderch through a descendent of the eleventh century ruler Elystan Glodrudd. Rhydderch lived at Llangathen during the later fifteenth century, and Jones felt there was little doubt that he had resided at Aberglasney.   Lewis Glyn Cothi (fl 1447-86) bestowed an ode upon Rhydderch, which, after extolling his wealth, wide learning, knowledge of the Scriptures and status as \'Pope of the land of Cetheiniawg\', proclaimed:<br>Iddo ef mae neuadd falch<br>Acun wengaer gam wyngalch<br>Ac o gylch ogylch i hon<br>Naw o arddau yn wrddion<br>Perllanwydd a gwinwydd gwyr<br>Derw ieuainc hyd yr awyr <br> <br>Johnson  suggests a translation as follows:<br>He has a proud hall, a fortress made bright by whitewash,<br>and encompassing it all around nine green gardens.<br>Orchard trees and crooked vines, young oaks reaching up to the skies.<br>Although an attractive proposition to equate these evocative lines with Aberglasney, having long ago reviewed their relevance, R.D.Williams dismissed them as insignificant to the site\'s history.  The places mentioned by Lewis Glyn Cothi are generally difficult to locate, and, from its topographical detail, it is possible that this poem was alluding to Cwrt Henry rather than Aberglasney.  Because his laudatory lyrics probably followed a regular formula, several sites may have shared similar word pictures. So whereas the nine gardens may have been a notional array of square or rectangular raised plots of the type excavated, for example, from a fifteenth-sixteenth century milieu at Haverfordwest Priory,   or at the possibly Cistercian monastic grange farm, Bwlch yr Oerfa in Ceredigion,  Lewis Glyn Cothi could have been enunciating a specific number for his patron to hear, a figure pregnant with hidden meaning or contemporary superstition. Besides demonstrating deftness in composition, their mystery and personal allusion gave these odes an entertaining and flattering edge. But they seldom provided reliable descriptions. <br>Whether or not Lewis Glyn Cothi\'s poem was alluding to Aberglasney, it might reasonably be argued that Lewis Glyn Cothi conveys some idea of how fifteenth century courtly houses of the Tywi Valley were meant to look at the time. <br>Whether or Aberglasney had been Rhydderch\'s home, his grandson, Sir William Thomas (c.1479-1542),  who became Sheriff of Carmarthenshire in 1541, certainly owned the Aberglasney estate. Rising to prominence after the fall of Dinefwr in 1531, he did well out of Henry VIII\'s later regime  becoming a notable figure locally and nationally.   The connection  between Sir William and the estate\'s eighteenth century occupants is fascinating, for John Dyer himself testified how his \'grandmother [had been] the daughter of Williams esqr. of Brinhavod in the parish of Langathen, descended from Sir William Thomas of Aberglasney who sold that house and estate to Bishop Rudd; \'twas then called Place Langathen\'.   This, then, usefully answers one part of R.D.Williams\'s question: whatever its tenurial complexities, Aberglasney, originally Plas Llangathen, appears to have been of lay origin. <br>The Rudds  <br>Bishop Anthony Rudd held the See of St Davids from 1594 to 1614  and Aberglasney was one of several properties he owned at the time of his death.  The exact date he acquired the place is unclear.  Among very limited evidence for Rudd\'s life is a biography compiled by Edward Yardley (1739-1779) for his Menevia Sacra. Yardley relates how Rudd was \'Buried at Llangathen Church in which he had purchased a good estate and built a handsome seat with a very decent chapel with a curious pulpit, ornamented with painted glass, called Aberglasney\'. <br>Significantly, by Rudd\'s time, the Bishop\'s Palace at St David\'s was abandoned and the new Protestant episcopate had already been half a century at Abergwili, east of Carmarthen. In common with his predecessor Marmaduke Middleton (who stated in 1583 that with but one exception all the bishop\'s houses were \'down to the ground\'), Rudd found the palaces so \'ruinous and decayed for want of reparation that they were not fit for any man, especially a bishop, to dwell and keep hospitality in\'.  Hardly surprising, then, is the general belief that Rudd had lived instead at Aberglasney.  Scant though the documentation is, it is nonetheless possible to rehearse other circumstances affecting the place whence Rudd might have best commuted to St David\'s. An initial pointer, coming from Francis Jones\'s research, explains how \'he paid attention to his official residence, spending considerable sums in restoring the episcopal palaces at Abergwili and St David\'s\'.  So both were probably made habitable, and he would have resided there particularly if, as seems likely, he had owned no property of status when he first came to the area. Here, some significance must attach to the fact that Abergwili is 14km. closer to St David\'s Cathedral than is Aberglasney.  <br>Another important issue is the date at which Rudd acquired Aberglasney. It seems unlikely \'that [acquisition] probably took place shortly after his elevation to the See\' (in 1594),  as time would have been required for him to accrue sufficient wealth with which to \'build up an estate for his children..by thrift and by leases of ecclesiastical property\'.  Unfortunately, the testimony of the tree-rings from the earliest roofing timbers on the site give too imprecise a felling date to place the first known building closer than c.1600 (Appendix 4), and it remains possible that that could have been erected by Rudd\'s predecessor. <br>Also significant to an understanding of his residency, was the existence of Rudd\'s impressive inventory of furnishings, decor and personalia, obviously housed away from Aberglasney, and presumably at Abergwili, for, in the will written shortly before his death, Rudd directed how specific items \'be carried to my house at Llangathen that my loveinge wife Anne Rudd may have the use and command of them during her natural life..\'  So although he chose burial in Llangathen,  it seems likely he had resided in a palace after taking up office, and had probably accepted conventional episcopal incumbency with residence at Abergwili Palace in spite of its appalling condition. <br>Whilst it has been acknowledged that there is no surviving evidence for plantings by Rudd and \'nothing of any use in clarifying if and when the Rudds created [the] garden spaces at Aberglasney has come to light in the archives\', the Rudds are nonetheless credited with having had sufficient \'resources and probably the vision to create what now remains\'.. during a period..\'for many reasons the most likely time for [the] garden terrace [i.e. the walkway] to have been established\'.   <br>Religious demands may well have influenced the design of an Elizabethan bishop\'s estate, official and private. Whereas courtiers and diplomats certainly travelled in Italy, absorbed and returned with Renaissance culture, Anglican clerics are less likely to have pursued the same course. In fact they more likely travelled in Germany.  <br>And then there is the problem of his palaces. Whether or not he resided at Aberglasney, would he have built a private garden to rival the Church\'s official residences, places he was obliged to repair at great expense? Furthermore, would an incumbent of the New Faith so overtly decorate the environs of his own farm with an icon of Renaissance culture, with its undertones of the Roman Faith? It seems unlikely.<br>Seeing Aberglasney as a future family seat, Anthony Rudd may have furnished it appropriately externally and internally. This would probably have meant laying out the site in a formal style, similarly to the new late sixteenth century offices created from the former monastic buildings at Brecon Priory.  He was succeeded at Aberglasney by his son Rice (1594-1664), created baronet in 1628, then by his great-grandson, also Rice Rudd (1643-1701).  By 1670, the house was being assessed at 30 hearths,  ranking alongside Golden Grove  as a major county seat. This hints at considerable growth from the size of place the Bishop is thought to have occupied (although no real details are available), and it seems inconceivable that by the later seventeenth century a mansion of such status would not have had a formal layout. As luck would have it, the Duke of Beaufort did not pass this way in 1684 when Thomas Dineley made idiosyncratic sketches of several important Welsh formal gardens and described, among others, Muddlescwm, Carmarthenshire. <br>Clues as to the possible whereabouts of this layout will be considered later.<br>The Dyers<br>The Dyers\' occupancy at Aberglasney has been dealt with in some detail by Green,  by Williams,  and in summary by Francis Jones.  It gave rise to the important connection with John Dyer, landscape artist and poet. <br>For the first part of the century, Aberglasney was bought and managed by Dyer\'s father, Robert (d.1710), followed by John\'s brother Robert (R.A.). R.A. was inept with money, and quit Llangathen in 1770, leaving his son William Herbert (born 1747) to sort out the mess. W.H.Dyer had no greater financial skills than his father, so he, too, staggered from one financial crisis to another until eventually obliged to sell up. <br>In 1776, W.H. was unfortunate to be chosen as High Sheriff of the county. A job not all willingly aspired to, it tended to incur immense costs in entertaining and peer group social obligation. Owing to his family\'s social ambitions on the one hand, and its debts on the other, Dyer should have been in a quandary about acceptance. But there was little choice in these matters and he would have taken on the job knowing how it would further challenge his pocket.<br>Holding office as Sheriff could be just one crippling pressure on the eighteenth century gentry. In the Dyers\' case their eventual sale of Aberglasney around 1800 probably resulted from an over-anxiety to be accepted among Carmarthenshire\'s county families. Of more than passing interest to the present argument: how they perceived their estate should have looked in contemporary style, may have been partly responsible for their financial ruin. <br>In this regard, tree-ring evidence from roofing timbers (Appendix 4) suggests that around 1770 the family undertook a major house re-building. How far they also re-designed the estate layout and introduced high-walled gardens and a new home farm facility in line with current fashion, is a matter for speculation. But such re-modelling could be anticipated during a period when the older gentry families of the Tywi Valley were already re-casting their parks\' core functions.   Dyer would also have been well aware of the radical agricultural changes being promoted by neighbouring gentry.  The establishment of a Carmarthenshire Agricultural Society on his doorstep in 1772   cannot have escaped his attention and might well have affected the way he conceived and ran his own business.<br>The influence Aberglasney had on John Dyer will be discussed below (OOO). <br>The Nabob Philipps family <br>In 1803 the estate was bought out by a certain Thomas Philipps who had made his money whilst a surgeon in India.  Apparently when Philipps acquired the property, it had been described in 1798 (on a poster printed by John Ross of Carmarthen) as having views, and being already with \'extensive outbuildings, a good garden, and excellent WATER at command\'.  Gulston\'s \'terras\'(see below p.000) was not then obviously a selling point. <br>During 1803-5 Philipps\' improvements included repairing a fishpond, tiling a pigeon house, making frames for hotbeds for the garden and buying young trees from Miller and Sweet, Bristol nurserymen. Although some of this construction and maintenance work is documented from accounts housed at the Carmarthenshire Record Office, it has not been found possible to verify in detail all the statements on gardening made by Francis Jones. However, Philipps\'s flurry of horticultural activity shows him to have been a man anxious to make a fashionable garden commensurate with his station, one offering visual amenity comparable to that of his neighbours.<br>Nor did this work stop in the garden. Philipps was also having walls built by Morgan John, Mason (bills of May 11th and 25th); John Jones, a Tyler, undertook \'New Tyling\' on the \'Mansion House, Gutters joining Kitchen, Roughcasting Chimneys, Library, Parapet Wall, Passage to Chapel, Dairy, Privy and Cott\'. Pointing was effected on the \'Pine End [of the] Mansion House, Cotts, Coach House, Kiln House, Kitchen, Passage, Dairy, Cellar, and Over the Door\'(September 21st 1805).  In November 1805 Philipps bought cherries, peaches, gooseberries, Dutch, white and black currants, and Antwerp, Beehive and red raspberries, again from Miller of Bristol (Miller-Philipps 1st November 1805).  <br>It is reasonable to suppose that the walled gardens had already been built by the Dyers, and that Philipps had acquired a bargain, off-the-peg, fully-fashioned gentleman\'s estate. This is borne out by the fact that Philipps\' bills relate more to maintenance than to new building activity, and the advert of 1798 actually boasts a full complement of garden and estate structures. <br>Certain other mid-nineteenth century Philipps estate documents do survive relating to two drainage projects on unspecified parcels of land.  But of greater interest is an advert about the estate in its Victorian heyday, which appeared in the Tenby Observer (for 1872) and described Aberglasney as:<br>A country Residence, with shooting over 1000 acres .. the house .. beautifully situate, in a sheltered position at the front of Grongar Hills nearly surrounded by park lands, and screened by forest trees and ornamental timber .. The Pleasure Grounds comprise a Croquet Lawn and Archery Ground, tastefully arranged Flower Beds and Borders, a three-sided Terrace Walk, constructed on the walls of an ancient fortress. There is a Vinery, 40 ft.[6.096m.] by 20 ft.[12.192m.], a large pinery, Melon Grounds, and 3 other Glass Houses, and an Orchard; the large walled Kitchen Garden is profusely stocked with choice fruit trees. The outbuildings comprise an enclosed Stable-yard and Farmery. The far-famed salmon river Towey is within a short distance.  <br>The apparent absence from this agent\'s list of a \'yew tunnel\' is noteworthy. The glass houses mentioned here are probably those which appeared on the First Edition 25-inch O.S. map in 1887 (FIG.4). All have now gone.<br>Aberglasney and Grongar in Topographical History<br>Without doubt, Dyer\'s poetry was always an important influence in attracting visitors to the area.  Grongar Hill, both poem and wooded eminence, were enthusiastically acclaimed by many passers-by taking Antiquarian and Picturesque Tours between c.1770 and 1820.  Grongar was part of a regular circuit which also included Newton (Dinefwr) and Dryslwyn castles. But it is curious that no tourists seem to have visited Aberglasney itself to investigate or confirm the Dyer connection, and even stranger that those with an eye to recording features of antiquarian interest are known to have frequented his home and be shown the buildings later vested with antiquity.  <br>By contrast, Grongar is continuously mentioned and illustrated throughout the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Numerous artists and writers came there to pay their homage, sharing Dyer\'s spiritual dream of an incipient Picturesque, if not just coming to imbibe for themselves the breathtaking views of local medieval castellated antiquity set amidst wood- and farmlands. None of this is surprising, for in the early nineteenth century, attractive popular tradition boasted how Dyer conceived his poem on the spot. By 1833, for example, subscribers to the Revd Samuel Lewis\'s Topographical Dictionary could confidently be shown the very tree beneath which Dyer\'s masterpiece was supposedly written. Furthermore, echoing (among others) Thomas Johnes of Hafod\'s erection of the Hafod Arms at the Devil\'s Bridge over forty years before, Aberglasney\'s \'proprietor [had] recently erected a neat and comfortable inn for the accommodation of visitors, who [were] attracted by the luxuriant and magnificent prospects for which the situation is celebrated, or by the remains of antiquity which are found in the vicinity\'. <br>The eighth edition of Black\'s Guide to South Wales of 1889,  endorsed Lewis, affording the site great prominence to Victorian visitors by exclaiming how Grongar Hill lay \'within sight of Golden Grove, on the opposite bank of the Towy\' before boasting how \'the view from this eminence is remarkably beautiful, and has been rendered famous by association with the history and writings of the poet Dyer, who was born in 1700, at..Aberglasney..[where]..the hawthorn under which he sat to contemplate the scene, and compose his admirable descriptive poem, is still shown. Burke says of this spot [Golden Grove, Grongar Hill or Aberglasney?], "No place do I remember in which the combinations of water, wood, mountain, and ruin assume such exquisite variety"\'. <br>Against this background, it was only natural that when the Cambrian Archaeological Association had its summer meeting at Llandeilo in 1855, members were conveyed to Aberglasney and Llangathen Church to be shown Bishop Rudd\'s important memorial.  Then again, in 1892, they acknowledged the Rudds\' significance to local history.  Curiously, however, in neither these, nor in any other references to Aberglasney, Dyers or Rudds, did the Cambrians note the existence of a curious gatehouse, arcade or yew tunnel.  These seem significant omissions for such an intellectually curious body, when it is recalled that the house was then an impressive, if not fashionable, gentleman\'s residence.  Could this omission be taken to indicate that at least some of these unusual features originated either within the recall of its current incumbents or of their ', 'nodegroup_id': '11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '<p>This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Carmarthenshire XXXIII, sheet 14 (1906). Its main elements on that map include walled garden, carriage drive, woodland, parterres, greenhouse and a pond.</p>\n\n<p>26 IX 00<br />\nAberglasney<br />\nby<br />\nC. Stephen Briggs,<br />\nRoyal Commission on Ancient and Historical Monuments (Wales) 1<br />\nContents<br />\nINTRODUCTION: The Recent History and Management of House and Estate; Objectives of Research; The Aberglasney Restoration Trust (ART)<br />\nSITE HISTORY: the Evidence for a Nunnery; Aberglasney: Home of Rhydderch?; The Rudds; The Dyers; The Nabob Philipps Family; Aberglasney and Grongar in Topographical History; Illustrations; Map Evidence.<br />\nSITE DESCRIPTION: The Walled Garden Complex; The Parapet Walkway and &#39;Cloister Enclosure&#39;; &#39;Pond Garden&#39;; Parkland Plantings; The Yew Tunnel.<br />\nLANDSCAPE THEORY AND HISTORICAL INTERPRETATION: &#39;The Most Important Formal Garden in Wales&#39;?; The Poems Grongar Hill and Country Walk as Topographical Evidence<br />\nDISCUSSION AND CONCLUSIONS: A Proposed Sequence for Farm and Estate Layout; Prospect.<br />\nAppendices:<br />\n1. The Excavations 1961-2 (G.R.Jones2 and W.B.Morris);<br />\n2. The Gatehouse (D.J.Roberts and R.F.Suggett1);<br />\n3. The Trees and Shrubs in 1994 (I. and J.Stokes3);<br />\n4. Dendrochronology: Tree-ring dating of building timbers, the Yew Tunnel and other trees at Aberglasney (D.W.H.Miles 4and M.C.Bridge5);<br />\n5. Listing Details of the Walkway and Gatehouse from Cadw,6 1990.<br />\n6. The 1991 Geophysical Survey (summary from J.Gater7 et al).<br />\n1.Crown Building, Plas Crug, Aberystwyth SY23 1NJ<br />\n2.6 Balvicar Drive, Queen&#39;s Park, Glasgow G42 8QS<br />\n3.Pantcoch Cottage, Carmel, Llanelli, Carmarthenshire SA14 7SG<br />\n4. Oxford Dendrochronology Laboratory, Mill Farm, Mapledurham, So. Oxon, RG4 7TX<br />\n5.Institute of Archaeology, University College London, 31-34 Gordon Square London,WC1H 0PY<br />\n6.Cadw: Welsh Historic Monuments, Government Buildings, The Welsh Assembly, Cathays Park, Cardiff CF1<br />\n7.Geophysical Surveys of Bradford, 12 Reservoir View, Thornton, Bradford BD13 3NT<br />\nAberglasney: The Theory, History and Archaeology of a Post-Medieval landscape<br />\nby C.Stephen Briggs,<br />\n[We hope] ..&#39;to encourage amongst the experts..criticism and discussion, so that we may possibly be able to discover some truths which are now buried in the almost impenetrable darkness of past ages&#39;.<br />\nAbstract<br />\nPrinted and documentary sources, archaeological excavation, dendrochronology and geophysical survey are employed to investigate the history of Aberglasney, a small country house near Llandeilo, Carmarthenshire (SN 5815 2213). Traditions about its garden layout, parapet walkway, a gatehouse and a yew &#39;tunnel&#39; are examined. Circa 1600, Bishop Anthony Rudd (1549-1615) probably built a &#39;cwrt&#39; enclosure aligned north-south. The gatehouse belonged to this or a later house. In 1770 or later, the Dyer family re-built the house, probably re-designed and re-walled the entire estate layout, when an earlier farm building was converted into the parapet walkway and stock pens, later known as the &#39;cloistered court&#39;. In Victorian times this feature, originally a farmyard, became a pleasure garden, part of a typical Georgian-Victorian complex including a kitchen garden, glasshouses, orchards and fishpond. A yew grove was established, most likely c 1805, when the Philipps family began planting in a picturesque style. Abandoned c 1950, house and garden became dilapidated. The site is now the object of a radical development programme, involving inter alia the stabilisation of all buildings fabric, and imposing sixteenth-seventeenth century style formal gardens over the 18th-19th century kitchen garden and orchard.<br />\nINTRODUCTION<br />\nThe Recent History and Management of House and Estate<br />\nAn important outcome of the establishment of a Welsh Historic Gardens Trust in 1989 was the attention it initially focused upon the historic estates around Llandeilo (FIG.1). Some, like Dynevor (Dinefwr) Castle and Golden Grove (Gelli Aur), were already familiar to historians and scholars of the Welsh landscape. Among the less well-known were Aberglasney and Middleton, the latter bereft of its house - lost by fire - nevertheless still an interesting eighteenth century parkland estate, its site now occupied by the Welsh National Botanical Garden. These all lie in the Tywi (Towy) Valley, an area particularly well-endowed with picturesque properties and peppered in farmsteads with medieval or post-medieval tenurial interest.<br />\nProbably of medieval origin, Aberglasney passed successively from the Rudds (c 1600); to the Dyers from 1710; then from 1803 to the Phillipps family. Importantly, John Dyer the poet (1699-1757) spent some of his boyhood there. As with many landed estates, its agricultural basis long since retreated and activities shrank to nucleate around core buildings. The conventional accoutrements of a Victorian gentleman&#39;s residence remained in 1990 (FIG.2): estate cottages, lodge and farmbuildings, decaying glasshouses, overgrown kennels and aviaries.<br />\nSet in about 8 acres [c 3.5 ha] of partly wooded grounds, Aberglasney, although abandoned and in poor repair (Pl.1), is Listed Grade II* (since re-Listing in 1990). It lies in the parish of Llangathen Near the house stand three unusual features: a stone-built &#39;arched walkway&#39;(henceforth the parapet walk) now enclosing an area recently named &#39;the cloister garden&#39;, also Grade II*, and a post-medieval gatehouse. This gatehouse and associated estate buildings are protected Grade II for Group Value. The third unusual feature is a tunnel formed of yew, planted in front of the eighteenth century (north-facing) facade of the house.<br />\nLying within a relatively unspoilt rural settlement, overlooked by Grongar Hill, the site was in 1995 incorporated into Llangathen Village Conservation Area by Dinefwr District (now Carmarthen County) Council. By then, several nineteenth century estate buildings had been converted to residential use and a comprehensive Tree Preservation Order, served by the Council in 1984 covered a variety of interesting, if not of specimen trees (see Appendix 3), some then hiding the decayed house from general view.<br />\nObjectives of Research<br />\nThe present writer first routinely examined all available maps and documents for the Welsh Historic Gardens Trust, around 1990. The stimulus to enquire further came from the paper given by Prof. J.Dixon Hunt at the Trust&#39;s inaugural Conference in April 1990.<br />\nDixon Hunt&#39;s paper examined two main traditions: the first, a prospective and retrospective of John Dyer&#39;s poems Grongar Hill and Country Walk. It proposed Dyer&#39;s texts as literal topographical description for identifying specific eighteenth century landscape features around Grongar and Aberglasney. Dyer&#39;s lyrics were also explored to see how the neighbouring landscape might have been contrived to accommodate Dyer&#39;s literary legacy.<br />\nThe second tradition concerned the origins and architectural history of Aberglasney&#39;s &#39;arched walkway&#39;. Acknowledging that at one time it had been ascribed a medieval ecclesiastical foundation, Dixon Hunt placed it within the European Renaissance Garden tradition, favour being found in a late sixteenth century derivation from Italy. The integrity of this pedigree is partly dependent on the taste of Bishop Anthony Rudd, during whose incumbency at St Davids (1594-1614) the house and grounds were probably remodelled c.1600. The likelihood of Italian influence depends not so much on the popularity of Italian garden building fashions in Wales, but more on the testimony of Aberglasney&#39;s surviving fabric and ascertainable garden layout.<br />\nCurrent interpretation of the site is best summarised by Mr William Wilkins: Aberglasney is &#39;believed to be the only 17th century formal garden left in Wales and among only a handful in Britain&#39; and &#39;although the present walls were built in the 1590s, they may have followed the layout of the original gardens referred to by a 15th century bard&#39;. This particular interpretation underlies regular reportage in the national broadsheets (since 1991), publication of a popular book and a site guide, and a television series. If historically demonstrable, such early features would indeed make this site extremely important to garden history. Presentation of the fruits of the recent investigations and research undertaken by R.C.A.M.W. staff (and others) now offers an opportunity to explore this interpretation as well as other explanations of the site&#39;s history..<br />\nIn 1961, having scrutinised its apparent historical pedigree, Rhys Dafys Williams questioned current interpretations of Aberglasney&#39;s early history and concluded that enduring answers were unlikely without archaeological excavation. The main strands of his argument are explained below. Unfortunately, however, Williams&#39;s analysis was overlooked in Dixon Hunt&#39;s influential paper, and the arguments which accompanied the walkway&#39;s up-graded status in Cadw&#39;s re-Listing Schedule only three months after the 1990 conference were also referenced without respect to Williams.<br />\nThe Aberglasney Restoration Trust<br />\nAlthough there was digging in the garden in 1961-2 (Appendix 1 and FIG.6) and again in 1991, the site was to become a focus of intensive archaeological activity only in 1995. The events leading to this can be outlined briefly. During the early 1990s it became clear that unless radical steps were taken, house, buildings and nineteenth century plantings would be lost through neglect. Following heightened media attention about the site&#39;s advancing decrepitude, a Trust (the Aberglasney Restoration Trust or ART) was formed in 1994 with the objectives of conserving the house shell, re-creating and opening the garden to the public, and restoring its buildings.<br />\nThis proposal was to involve major landscape change resulting in extensive soil disturbances preparatory to building stabilisation and the introduction of imaginative new gardens. Due to its protected status and historical connections, the site obviously merited excavation and full architectural record, requirements later to be recognised in the conditions attached to some project funding arrangements.<br />\nAlthough incomplete at the time of writing, four years of intermittent digging already offer important clues about the site&#39;s history. Archaeology, together with historical research, a re-appraisal of John Dyer&#39;s art and a keener awareness of estate and garden evolution in Renaissance, Picturesque and Industrial Wales, now assist a clearer evaluation of Aberglasney&#39;s development.<br />\nIt is interesting that the present enquiry should comprehend not only Dyer&#39;s poems, which lie at the head of an eighteenth century Landscape Tradition, but also includes a fifteenth century Welsh bardic poem, one of many written in that genre, which is currently attracting scholarly attention.<br />\nSITE HISTORY<br />\nThe evidence for a Nunnery<br />\nIn his thorough treatment of the historical sources bearing upon Llangathen and its locality, Williams posed the question: was Aberglasney of lay or ecclesiastical origin in late medieval times? Although neither parish nor house figures in the records of Talley Abbey, searches of the Court of Augmentations for lands disposed of from more distant monastic establishments have so far failed to reveal anything about the sale of Llangathen lands before 1593-4, when the lands of Dame Katherine Edgcumb, formerly of Rice Griffiths [Rhys ap Gruffydd], were disposed of to John Herbert, Jane his wife, and John his son by way of lease for a term of lives. Williams quotes from Bacon&#39;s Liber Regis of 1786, which was printed in T.W.Barker&#39;s researches on Carmarthenshire Endowments. It runs:<br />\nLlangathen alias Llan Galthen (St Michael and All Saints). NUNNERY OF CHESTER, Propr., Bishop of Chester, Impropriator and Patron. Yearly value #40. King&#39;s Books, #6/13/4.<br />\nUnfortunately, these Herbert lands were more probably of Cwrt Henry than of Aberglasney, and the Chester Endowment is to Llangathen Church, so need not relate to this inquiry. Whereas it is possible that further medieval tenurial documentation may await discovery, having exhausted the known manuscript sources, Williams observed that only excavation would more certainly reveal the foundations of &#39;this establishment granted by the Crown to Chester Nunnery in the 14th century&#39;, before he concluded that searches of the Court of Augmentation Records for Cheshire might help resolve any question of ecclesiastical tenure.<br />\nAberglasney: Home of Rhydderch?<br />\nBardic poetry enjoyed an important currency in Wales during the later Middle Ages. Its descriptive potential for contemporary homes and landscapes was first explored by Enid Pierce Roberts who drew attention to a number of properties. These included Mysoglen, Llanfair-y-Cwmwd (SH 44 66), described by Robert ap Ifan in the late sixteenth century; Bodorgan, Llangadwaladr, Anglesey (SH 3859 6736), found in a piece by Sion Brwynog of around 1550; Gwydir, Caernarfonshire (SH 7960 6104), where topiary was mentioned by Ifan Sion, and Plas-y-ward, Llanynys, Denbighshire (SJ 11 60), where a lady&#39;s garden was described by Simwnt Fychan. Unfortunately, the equation of these poetic descriptions with the known fabric or present topographies of the sites has met with very limited success. Furthermore, over 50,000 bardic poems are known encompassing the medieval period to the eighteenth century, and their investigation is only now beginning. Nonetheless, many are known to describe courtly gardens and their settings.<br />\nThese poems were metrically structured with descriptions composed to varying formulae. Consequently, equating descriptive detail with specific structural features is likely to be difficult, if not impossible, since the lyrics were imaginative and sometimes highly romanticised. When using evocative prose in any historical research, an appreciation of the writer&#39;s social standing can be as telling as can any analysis of his texts. Fields of artistic patronage do tend to lend themselves to the persuasive pen. (Though it is useful to note that there is, at least, one Welsh poem, of 1605, describing garden and house construction for Sir Harry Williams at (Old) Gwernyfed, Breconshire, details of which do bear real likeness to some of the surviving remains.)<br />\nOne bard is believed to have described Aberglasney later in the fifteenth century. Major Francis Jones demonstrated that Rhys ap Gruffydd ap Llywelyn Foethus begat Rhydderch through a descendent of the eleventh century ruler Elystan Glodrudd. Rhydderch lived at Llangathen during the later fifteenth century, and Jones felt there was little doubt that he had resided at Aberglasney. Lewis Glyn Cothi (fl 1447-86) bestowed an ode upon Rhydderch, which, after extolling his wealth, wide learning, knowledge of the Scriptures and status as &#39;Pope of the land of Cetheiniawg&#39;, proclaimed:<br />\nIddo ef mae neuadd falch<br />\nAcun wengaer gam wyngalch<br />\nAc o gylch ogylch i hon<br />\nNaw o arddau yn wrddion<br />\nPerllanwydd a gwinwydd gwyr<br />\nDerw ieuainc hyd yr awyr<br />\n<br />\nJohnson suggests a translation as follows:<br />\nHe has a proud hall, a fortress made bright by whitewash,<br />\nand encompassing it all around nine green gardens.<br />\nOrchard trees and crooked vines, young oaks reaching up to the skies.<br />\nAlthough an attractive proposition to equate these evocative lines with Aberglasney, having long ago reviewed their relevance, R.D.Williams dismissed them as insignificant to the site&#39;s history. The places mentioned by Lewis Glyn Cothi are generally difficult to locate, and, from its topographical detail, it is possible that this poem was alluding to Cwrt Henry rather than Aberglasney. Because his laudatory lyrics probably followed a regular formula, several sites may have shared similar word pictures. So whereas the nine gardens may have been a notional array of square or rectangular raised plots of the type excavated, for example, from a fifteenth-sixteenth century milieu at Haverfordwest Priory, or at the possibly Cistercian monastic grange farm, Bwlch yr Oerfa in Ceredigion, Lewis Glyn Cothi could have been enunciating a specific number for his patron to hear, a figure pregnant with hidden meaning or contemporary superstition. Besides demonstrating deftness in composition, their mystery and personal allusion gave these odes an entertaining and flattering edge. But they seldom provided reliable descriptions.<br />\nWhether or not Lewis Glyn Cothi&#39;s poem was alluding to Aberglasney, it might reasonably be argued that Lewis Glyn Cothi conveys some idea of how fifteenth century courtly houses of the Tywi Valley were meant to look at the time.<br />\nWhether or Aberglasney had been Rhydderch&#39;s home, his grandson, Sir William Thomas (c.1479-1542), who became Sheriff of Carmarthenshire in 1541, certainly owned the Aberglasney estate. Rising to prominence after the fall of Dinefwr in 1531, he did well out of Henry VIII&#39;s later regime becoming a notable figure locally and nationally. The connection between Sir William and the estate&#39;s eighteenth century occupants is fascinating, for John Dyer himself testified how his &#39;grandmother [had been] the daughter of Williams esqr. of Brinhavod in the parish of Langathen, descended from Sir William Thomas of Aberglasney who sold that house and estate to Bishop Rudd; &#39;twas then called Place Langathen&#39;. This, then, usefully answers one part of R.D.Williams&#39;s question: whatever its tenurial complexities, Aberglasney, originally Plas Llangathen, appears to have been of lay origin.<br />\nThe Rudds<br />\nBishop Anthony Rudd held the See of St Davids from 1594 to 1614 and Aberglasney was one of several properties he owned at the time of his death. The exact date he acquired the place is unclear. Among very limited evidence for Rudd&#39;s life is a biography compiled by Edward Yardley (1739-1779) for his Menevia Sacra. Yardley relates how Rudd was &#39;Buried at Llangathen Church in which he had purchased a good estate and built a handsome seat with a very decent chapel with a curious pulpit, ornamented with painted glass, called Aberglasney&#39;.<br />\nSignificantly, by Rudd&#39;s time, the Bishop&#39;s Palace at St David&#39;s was abandoned and the new Protestant episcopate had already been half a century at Abergwili, east of Carmarthen. In common with his predecessor Marmaduke Middleton (who stated in 1583 that with but one exception all the bishop&#39;s houses were &#39;down to the ground&#39;), Rudd found the palaces so &#39;ruinous and decayed for want of reparation that they were not fit for any man, especially a bishop, to dwell and keep hospitality in&#39;. Hardly surprising, then, is the general belief that Rudd had lived instead at Aberglasney. Scant though the documentation is, it is nonetheless possible to rehearse other circumstances affecting the place whence Rudd might have best commuted to St David&#39;s. An initial pointer, coming from Francis Jones&#39;s research, explains how &#39;he paid attention to his official residence, spending considerable sums in restoring the episcopal palaces at Abergwili and St David&#39;s&#39;. So both were probably made habitable, and he would have resided there particularly if, as seems likely, he had owned no property of status when he first came to the area. Here, some significance must attach to the fact that Abergwili is 14km. closer to St David&#39;s Cathedral than is Aberglasney.<br />\nAnother important issue is the date at which Rudd acquired Aberglasney. It seems unlikely &#39;that [acquisition] probably took place shortly after his elevation to the See&#39; (in 1594), as time would have been required for him to accrue sufficient wealth with which to &#39;build up an estate for his children..by thrift and by leases of ecclesiastical property&#39;. Unfortunately, the testimony of the tree-rings from the earliest roofing timbers on the site give too imprecise a felling date to place the first known building closer than c.1600 (Appendix 4), and it remains possible that that could have been erected by Rudd&#39;s predecessor.<br />\nAlso significant to an understanding of his residency, was the existence of Rudd&#39;s impressive inventory of furnishings, decor and personalia, obviously housed away from Aberglasney, and presumably at Abergwili, for, in the will written shortly before his death, Rudd directed how specific items &#39;be carried to my house at Llangathen that my loveinge wife Anne Rudd may have the use and command of them during her natural life..&#39; So although he chose burial in Llangathen, it seems likely he had resided in a palace after taking up office, and had probably accepted conventional episcopal incumbency with residence at Abergwili Palace in spite of its appalling condition.<br />\nWhilst it has been acknowledged that there is no surviving evidence for plantings by Rudd and &#39;nothing of any use in clarifying if and when the Rudds created [the] garden spaces at Aberglasney has come to light in the archives&#39;, the Rudds are nonetheless credited with having had sufficient &#39;resources and probably the vision to create what now remains&#39;.. during a period..&#39;for many reasons the most likely time for [the] garden terrace [i.e. the walkway] to have been established&#39;.<br />\nReligious demands may well have influenced the design of an Elizabethan bishop&#39;s estate, official and private. Whereas courtiers and diplomats certainly travelled in Italy, absorbed and returned with Renaissance culture, Anglican clerics are less likely to have pursued the same course. In fact they more likely travelled in Germany.<br />\nAnd then there is the problem of his palaces. Whether or not he resided at Aberglasney, would he have built a private garden to rival the Church&#39;s official residences, places he was obliged to repair at great expense? Furthermore, would an incumbent of the New Faith so overtly decorate the environs of his own farm with an icon of Renaissance culture, with its undertones of the Roman Faith? It seems unlikely.<br />\nSeeing Aberglasney as a future family seat, Anthony Rudd may have furnished it appropriately externally and internally. This would probably have meant laying out the site in a formal style, similarly to the new late sixteenth century offices created from the former monastic buildings at Brecon Priory. He was succeeded at Aberglasney by his son Rice (1594-1664), created baronet in 1628, then by his great-grandson, also Rice Rudd (1643-1701). By 1670, the house was being assessed at 30 hearths, ranking alongside Golden Grove as a major county seat. This hints at considerable growth from the size of place the Bishop is thought to have occupied (although no real details are available), and it seems inconceivable that by the later seventeenth century a mansion of such status would not have had a formal layout. As luck would have it, the Duke of Beaufort did not pass this way in 1684 when Thomas Dineley made idiosyncratic sketches of several important Welsh formal gardens and described, among others, Muddlescwm, Carmarthenshire.<br />\nClues as to the possible whereabouts of this layout will be considered later.<br />\nThe Dyers<br />\nThe Dyers&#39; occupancy at Aberglasney has been dealt with in some detail by Green, by Williams, and in summary by Francis Jones. It gave rise to the important connection with John Dyer, landscape artist and poet.<br />\nFor the first part of the century, Aberglasney was bought and managed by Dyer&#39;s father, Robert (d.1710), followed by John&#39;s brother Robert (R.A.). R.A. was inept with money, and quit Llangathen in 1770, leaving his son William Herbert (born 1747) to sort out the mess. W.H.Dyer had no greater financial skills than his father, so he, too, staggered from one financial crisis to another until eventually obliged to sell up.<br />\nIn 1776, W.H. was unfortunate to be chosen as High Sheriff of the county. A job not all willingly aspired to, it tended to incur immense costs in entertaining and peer group social obligation. Owing to his family&#39;s social ambitions on the one hand, and its debts on the other, Dyer should have been in a quandary about acceptance. But there was little choice in these matters and he would have taken on the job knowing how it would further challenge his pocket.<br />\nHolding office as Sheriff could be just one crippling pressure on the eighteenth century gentry. In the Dyers&#39; case their eventual sale of Aberglasney around 1800 probably resulted from an over-anxiety to be accepted among Carmarthenshire&#39;s county families. Of more than passing interest to the present argument: how they perceived their estate should have looked in contemporary style, may have been partly responsible for their financial ruin.<br />\nIn this regard, tree-ring evidence from roofing timbers (Appendix 4) suggests that around 1770 the family undertook a major house re-building. How far they also re-designed the estate layout and introduced high-walled gardens and a new home farm facility in line with current fashion, is a matter for speculation. But such re-modelling could be anticipated during a period when the older gentry families of the Tywi Valley were already re-casting their parks&#39; core functions. Dyer would also have been well aware of the radical agricultural changes being promoted by neighbouring gentry. The establishment of a Carmarthenshire Agricultural Society on his doorstep in 1772 cannot have escaped his attention and might well have affected the way he conceived and ran his own business.<br />\nThe influence Aberglasney had on John Dyer will be discussed below (OOO).<br />\nThe Nabob Philipps family<br />\nIn 1803 the estate was bought out by a certain Thomas Philipps who had made his money whilst a surgeon in India. Apparently when Philipps acquired the property, it had been described in 1798 (on a poster printed by John Ross of Carmarthen) as having views, and being already with &#39;extensive outbuildings, a good garden, and excellent WATER at command&#39;. Gulston&#39;s &#39;terras&#39;(see below p.000) was not then obviously a selling point.<br />\nDuring 1803-5 Philipps&#39; improvements included repairing a fishpond, tiling a pigeon house, making frames for hotbeds for the garden and buying young trees from Miller and Sweet, Bristol nurserymen. Although some of this construction and maintenance work is documented from accounts housed at the Carmarthenshire Record Office, it has not been found possible to verify in detail all the statements on gardening made by Francis Jones. However, Philipps&#39;s flurry of horticultural activity shows him to have been a man anxious to make a fashionable garden commensurate with his station, one offering visual amenity comparable to that of his neighbours.<br />\nNor did this work stop in the garden. Philipps was also having walls built by Morgan John, Mason (bills of May 11th and 25th); John Jones, a Tyler, undertook &#39;New Tyling&#39; on the &#39;Mansion House, Gutters joining Kitchen, Roughcasting Chimneys, Library, Parapet Wall, Passage to Chapel, Dairy, Privy and Cott&#39;. Pointing was effected on the &#39;Pine End [of the] Mansion House, Cotts, Coach House, Kiln House, Kitchen, Passage, Dairy, Cellar, and Over the Door&#39;(September 21st 1805). In November 1805 Philipps bought cherries, peaches, gooseberries, Dutch, white and black currants, and Antwerp, Beehive and red raspberries, again from Miller of Bristol (Miller-Philipps 1st November 1805).<br />\nIt is reasonable to suppose that the walled gardens had already been built by the Dyers, and that Philipps had acquired a bargain, off-the-peg, fully-fashioned gentleman&#39;s estate. This is borne out by the fact that Philipps&#39; bills relate more to maintenance than to new building activity, and the advert of 1798 actually boasts a full complement of garden and estate structures.<br />\nCertain other mid-nineteenth century Philipps estate documents do survive relating to two drainage projects on unspecified parcels of land. But of greater interest is an advert about the estate in its Victorian heyday, which appeared in the Tenby Observer (for 1872) and described Aberglasney as:<br />\nA country Residence, with shooting over 1000 acres .. the house .. beautifully situate, in a sheltered position at the front of Grongar Hills nearly surrounded by park lands, and screened by forest trees and ornamental timber .. The Pleasure Grounds comprise a Croquet Lawn and Archery Ground, tastefully arranged Flower Beds and Borders, a three-sided Terrace Walk, constructed on the walls of an ancient fortress. There is a Vinery, 40 ft.[6.096m.] by 20 ft.[12.192m.], a large pinery, Melon Grounds, and 3 other Glass Houses, and an Orchard; the large walled Kitchen Garden is profusely stocked with choice fruit trees. The outbuildings comprise an enclosed Stable-yard and Farmery. The far-famed salmon river Towey is within a short distance.<br />\nThe apparent absence from this agent&#39;s list of a &#39;yew tunnel&#39; is noteworthy. The glass houses mentioned here are probably those which appeared on the First Edition 25-inch O.S. map in 1887 (FIG.4). All have now gone.<br />\nAberglasney and Grongar in Topographical History<br />\nWithout doubt, Dyer&#39;s poetry was always an important influence in attracting visitors to the area. Grongar Hill, both poem and wooded eminence, were enthusiastically acclaimed by many passers-by taking Antiquarian and Picturesque Tours between c.1770 and 1820. Grongar was part of a regular circuit which also included Newton (Dinefwr) and Dryslwyn castles. But it is curious that no tourists seem to have visited Aberglasney itself to investigate or confirm the Dyer connection, and even stranger that those with an eye to recording features of antiquarian interest are known to have frequented his home and be shown the buildings later vested with antiquity.<br />\nBy contrast, Grongar is continuously mentioned and illustrated throughout the late eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Numerous artists and writers came there to pay their homage, sharing Dyer&#39;s spiritual dream of an incipient Picturesque, if not just coming to imbibe for themselves the breathtaking views of local medieval castellated antiquity set amidst wood- and farmlands. None of this is surprising, for in the early nineteenth century, attractive popular tradition boasted how Dyer conceived his poem on the spot. By 1833, for example, subscribers to the Revd Samuel Lewis&#39;s Topographical Dictionary could confidently be shown the very tree beneath which Dyer&#39;s masterpiece was supposedly written. Furthermore, echoing (among others) Thomas Johnes of Hafod&#39;s erection of the Hafod Arms at the Devil&#39;s Bridge over forty years before, Aberglasney&#39;s &#39;proprietor [had] recently erected a neat and comfortable inn for the accommodation of visitors, who [were] attracted by the luxuriant and magnificent prospects for which the situation is celebrated, or by the remains of antiquity which are found in the vicinity&#39;.<br />\nThe eighth edition of Black&#39;s Guide to South Wales of 1889, endorsed Lewis, affording the site great prominence to Victorian visitors by exclaiming how Grongar Hill lay &#39;within sight of Golden Grove, on the opposite bank of the Towy&#39; before boasting how &#39;the view from this eminence is remarkably beautiful, and has been rendered famous by association with the history and writings of the poet Dyer, who was born in 1700, at..Aberglasney..[where]..the hawthorn under which he sat to contemplate the scene, and compose his admirable descriptive poem, is still shown. Burke says of this spot [Golden Grove, Grongar Hill or Aberglasney?], &quot;No place do I remember in which the combinations of water, wood, mountain, and ruin assume such exquisite variety&quot;&#39;.<br />\nAgainst this background, it was only natural that when the Cambrian Archaeological Association had its summer meeting at Llandeilo in 1855, members were conveyed to Aberglasney and Llangathen Church to be shown Bishop Rudd&#39;s important memorial. Then again, in 1892, they acknowledged the Rudds&#39; significance to local history. Curiously, however, in neither these, nor in any other references to Aberglasney, Dyers or Rudds, did the Cambrians note the existence of a curious gatehouse, arcade or yew tunnel. These seem significant omissions for such an intellectually curious body, when it is recalled that the house was then an impressive, if not fashionable, gentleman&#39;s residence. Could this omission be taken to indicate that at least some of these unusual features originated either within the recall of its current incumbents or of their</p>\n', 'nodegroup_id': '11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'Post Medieval', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No Preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Aberglasney, Garden, Llangathen', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Aberglasney Garden, Llangathen', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}, {'string': 'No preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': True}], 'dates': [{'date': 19970923, 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'nodeid': '5e5d39f4-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'date': 20061009, 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'nodeid': 'a0788be0-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}], 'domains': [{'label': 'Llangathen', 'conceptid': '5b5f5f2b-a625-4b20-b248-cb831a420478', 'valueid': '44345f1c-451b-42db-8927-2ba78f61942d', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f3d-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'Carmarthenshire', 'conceptid': '13ee4b52-3de9-4b2d-ba97-e467d79143ec', 'valueid': '1e91bec2-cc46-4d4b-b13c-9e692fc028b6', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f61-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'Carmarthenshire', 'conceptid': '486e69a1-78f1-4f6a-aaba-045385df3344', 'valueid': 'c4db557f-abce-4232-aecb-a4575fd46151', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f46-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'English Description', 'conceptid': 'e5817822-5334-47c9-85a6-f4cec157aac9', 'valueid': '7a82d020-e0f5-4459-b07f-d1e634547fa1', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'Grid reference taken at centre of site', 'conceptid': '96114e65-b2fc-4c61-b327-524b251e8d42', 'valueid': '46e49d76-4b22-4c20-9ed9-ae45b5516369', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'COUNTRY HOUSE GARDEN', 'conceptid': 'c38ec122-d8d7-3626-a23f-5995fb981e6c', 'valueid': '84a97f23-a0f3-4e8e-8dc4-903fa8462db6', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'label': 'No Preference', 'conceptid': 'bdf4c70c-6a13-4cd0-a33f-33241d81302e', 'valueid': '078fdac7-cb0c-4ab6-b252-0db193d97a9c', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provision