2022-07-12 12:32:08,995 arches.app.search.search WARNING  2022-07-12 12:32:08.994442: WARNING: failed to index document: {'displaydescription': "PENPONT (House = NPRN 16026) possesses a small landscape park; formal and informal gardens; walled kitchen garden with heated glasshouses including a former pinery. It is remarkable for the survival, in a highly picturesque situation, of an early nineteenth-century park and garden, influenced by Repton, with remains of an earlier formal layout, including some of the first larch trees in Britain. Main phases of construction from 1660; c. 1740s-81; and 1781-1830s. Features continue to be put into the park, including a maze.\tCSB partly from first-hand, partly from Cadw register<br>\n<br>\nThis garden is depicted on the Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Brecknockshire XXVII, sheet  14 (1888, 1904). It comes in three main elements N-S. The most northerly is the kitchen garden, currently being employed for growing vegetables. Paths have been laid not and maintained, though entirely in keeping with the known 19th pattern or materials.   The pinery has been extensively studied, comprehensively published interpreted by JPR Williams. <br>\n<br>\nThe middle section, immediately S of the Usk, is the old estate nucleus, with the E-facing Georgian house, its axis having changed sometime during the early 18th century, when if faced S, rear to the river, probably set in a N-S rectangular enclosure the corners defined by small towers (teste contemporary map). Today the house is remarkable for the quality of its glass conservatory/orangery, rebuilt during the'90s.<br>\n<br>\nBeyond and S are the larches, and a moss house, sadly derelicted since c 1994. <br>\n<br>\nThe third and final component is the most southern, which abuts the main road (A40). This is a fine piece of woodland set around the old Llanspyddid Church and several estate buildings. <br>\n<br>\nC.S.Briggs 06.12.05", 'resourceinstanceid': 'b169f5cf-8857-4815-912e-11f002388a9d', 'g