2022-07-25 10:32:17,235 arches.app.search.search WARNING  2022-07-25 10:32:17.234095: WARNING: failed to index document: {'displaydescription': 'Parklands & Gardens of Wales No:PGW.`                            <br>\n1. PENLLERGAER (PENLLERGARE)  Although the house has gone, this site preserves a sizeable amount of a very important  large-scale picturesque and romantic landscape of formerly wooded park and grounds, including lakes and an artificial waterfall of the mid nineteenth century. It was created by John Dillwyn Llewelyn, a nationally important figure in horticulture. The site is unusual in that there are numerous contemporary photographs of it, taken by Llewelyn, who was also a pioneer of photography. Although much of his exotic planting has gone the structure of his landscaping remains, as do the ruins of his pioneering orchideous house in the walled kitchen garden.  The large walled kitchen garden complex, originally hosting a range of up to a dozen hot-houses, includes the ruins of a pioneering orchid house (1835). Otherwise the  main phases of construction were 1833-1882.<br>\nCSB<br>\n<br>\n2. This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Glamorgan XIV, sheet  11 (1898). Its main elements on that map include lake, observatory, kennels, woodland with vista paths, woodland, walled garden, well, summerhouse (possible), sunday school, parkland, pheasantry, lodges, greenhouses, possible formal garden, conservatory, carriage drive, relict hedgelines and orchard.  C.H. Nicholas, RCAHMW, 17th August 2006.<br>\n<br>\nAnd see NPRN 19645', 'resourceinstanceid': 'dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0', 'graph_id': '60120f3c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'map_popup': 'No preference', 'displayname': 'Penllergaer Park, Swansea', 'root_ontology_class': 'http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E18_Physical_Thing', 'legacyid': '232123', 'tiles': [{'tileid': 'ec7d568c-0116-431b-a003-4f2acf4c4e73', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6a