None}, {'tileid': '4a2cd58e-0e07-4043-b956-b92c8f295281', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f6e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'Parklands & Gardens of Wales No:PGW.`                            <br>\n1. PENLLERGAER (PENLLERGARE)  Although the house has gone, this site preserves a sizeable amount of a very important  large-scale picturesque and romantic landscape of formerly wooded park and grounds, including lakes and an artificial waterfall of the mid nineteenth century. It was created by John Dillwyn Llewelyn, a nationally important figure in horticulture. The site is unusual in that there are numerous contemporary photographs of it, taken by Llewelyn, who was also a pioneer of photography. Although much of his exotic planting has gone the structure of his landscaping remains, as do the ruins of his pioneering orchideous house in the walled kitchen garden.  The large walled kitchen garden complex, originally hosting a range of up to a dozen hot-houses, includes the ruins of a pioneering orchid house (1835). Otherwise the  main phases of construction were 1833-1882.<br>\nCSB<br>\n<br>\n2. This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Glamorgan XIV, sheet  11 (1898). Its main elements on that map include lake, observatory, kennels, woodland with vista paths, woodland, walled garden, well, summerhouse (possible), sunday school, parkland, pheasantry, lodges, greenhouses, possible formal garden, conservatory, carriage drive, relict hedgelines and orchard.  C.H. Nicholas, RCAHMW, 17th August 2006.<br>\n<br>\nAnd see NPRN 19645', '60120f70-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '7a82d020-e0f5-4459-b07f-d1e634547fa1'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': '76a6fdf5-675b-4207-b715-405ea19a3cf1', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f40-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': 'a643eee2-12b0-4ca5-8a54-2101712037f7', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': UUID('76a6fdf5-675b-4207-b715-405ea19a3cf1'), 'data': {'60120f6a-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f77-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': {'type': 'FeatureCollection', 'features': [{'id': '96c33644-27bd-4ea2-855d-ac0267d830fd', 'type': 'Feature', 'geometry': {'type': 'Point', 'coordinates': [-3.9934566, 51.673238]}, 'properties': {}}]}}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f6a-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': 'f8c02c52-7d0c-4f79-ba02-a118a8743ce5', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': UUID('76a6fdf5-675b-4207-b715-405ea19a3cf1'), 'data': {'60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f79-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'SS6225399045'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': 'b72ec2da-78df-4e63-a9ed-c16608674620', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'084fae54-9ff6-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'SS6299', '20bd6de6-9ff6-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'SS69NW', '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, 'ad6df3ec-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '46e49d76-4b22-4c20-9ed9-ae45b5516369'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': '60e9db9d-0a1d-425a-8f4b-e06890e02b10', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '5e5d39f4-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '1993-01-01', '83277d3a-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'UNK', 'a0788be0-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '2017-04-19', 'c9a63b84-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'LM'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': '5b830688-3a02-4ffc-8b62-69790238c29e', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'e32cf9ea-f04b-4b73-81e2-385a7c41cad4', '60120f71-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f72-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '078fdac7-cb0c-4ab6-b252-0db193d97a9c', '60120f78-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '54496ff2-4693-4138-ba03-277eff940f11'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': '652a188b-310c-4476-86cf-00b86fc860de', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'e32cf9ea-f04b-4b73-81e2-385a7c41cad4', '60120f71-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': None, '60120f72-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '078fdac7-cb0c-4ab6-b252-0db193d97a9c', '60120f78-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '54496ff2-4693-4138-ba03-277eff940f11'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': 'a05be089-5b20-4596-9221-b9eeced59b02', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f55-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '248ae010-35a2-4202-be20-41d0495a8c18', '60120f76-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '54496ff2-4693-4138-ba03-277eff940f11'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f55-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': 'c7e2d7df-45e0-43b7-9321-7bc058d957d5', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'PGW(GM)054(SWA)', '60120f73-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '0f721afd-8452-4738-84ce-a952ef4e72e6', '60120f74-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'f6c80c3a-0352-4d89-8a00-ed6bb9fa7d2a'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': '67193ef9-3757-465f-9444-dbdbd5645aea', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'46bf8db2-a005-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '54496ff2-4693-4138-ba03-277eff940f11', '60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '54d7e6d4-4604-49ee-a027-021778082062'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': 'cba651ac-7cbb-429a-a46c-569f49926e04', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': "Preliminary Notes to accompany a survey of the kitchen garden at Penllergare*, Swansea<br>Survey undertaken by C.S.Briggs and G.A.Ward  for RCAHMW January-August 2000<br>Introduction<br>Penllergare is one of the most extensive and distinguished Parkland and garden sites in Wales dating from the earlier nineteenth century. It was at one time the home of the Dillwyn Llewelyns, where photographic work was undertaken. More importantly for the present study, its garden environs were where scientific work developed on orchids and rhododendrons. The family residence and ancillary buildings were demolished in the 1950s and the M4 now runs alongside the N boundary of the site. They included an important conservatory and the garden to an Observatory, the structure of which has survived. The house site, with important trees and some buildings including the observatory, now accommodates council offices. <br>*Whereas the name Penllergaer now attaches to the local village, this account employs the earlier form of the name, still associated with the historic estate.<br> <br>The parkland (not dealt with here) demands a much fuller examination than has been afforded hitherto, in order to record any remaining neglected features before these become subsumed in recreational facilities for nearby residential housing. It seems particularly important to note the palaeoecological potential of the lake sediments for detecting lost exotic floral species. Beyond the kitchen garden complex, original parkland and ancillary farms was slowly overtaken during the earlier twentieth century, and more recently was heavily built over 1990-1998. In spite of this, many original paths, steps, seats, a summer house site and some specimen trees have survived. In 1991 the WHGT commissioned a report on the parkland, and the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (H Owen-John) made important representations to the local authority in order to secure proper investigation and preservation of parkland features. Current attempts to set up a Friends' group (by M Norman) have resulted in the publication of a book, and seem to have stimulated ongoing interest with the new Swansea Authority.<br>Even in its present derelict state, Penllergare's kitchen garden complex is one of the most extensive surviving in Wales outside a major stately home. Its original range of forcing houses must still rate it amongst the most comprehensive group ever built in Britain. Of particular interest to the history of botany, is the orchid house, a building at the leading edge of orchid cultivation from c 1835 and continuing during the middle decades of the nineteenth century.<br>Ede's Plan<br>A key aid to the interpretation of the garden's remaining structures is the sketch plan drawn from memory and recently provided to Mr Richard Morris by Mr William Ede, who ran a market garden there during the 1920s. Although schematic in places, the sketch offers some solutions which would otherwise not be available. There are one or two key areas where memory did not serve Ede well. First, he was unsure of the relationship between the W wall and the outside N-S track. However, this does not affect his recall that peach, nectarines and large black berries occupied the inside of that W wall. Neither does it matter that he effectively depicts 2 S walls. Most crucial to an appreciation of frame and glasshouse development is his inclusion of 2 otherwise unknown frames outside the more Tropical of the two major glasshouses (Structure C). The area of greatest potential interpretational difficulty relates to the frames and glasshouses F-M, where, the Pineapple House aside,  it is unclear how far his 'bed of mint' and three 'frames' relate to the structure G recognised in this survey, or to the other now lost glass houses formerly within the frames yard (H,I,J) or (K.L,M) which were at one time within the curtilage of the gardener's cottage (N).<br>Ede's plan does show what grew in the garden's four central quadrants, viz, NW Raspberries, SW gooseberries  and espalier pears trained on iron railings with cooking cherries on the wall and a large black cherry tree, with some redcurrents; and the SE quadrant was similar with red and white currents on the N-facing S wall. The E wall carried 'Apples Trained' throughout iuts entire length, and the N wall grew peaches.<br>Ede also provided a useful plan of the orchid house.<br> <br>Penllergare on the Map<br>Although well depicted on OS (25-inch) plans until the 1920s, by the time of the 1959     survey several important components of the garden, including the central layout and its pond, the ornamental pond (B) and most of the frame yard had disappeared, presumably because they were overgrown and unsurveyable. At this time, a N-S aligned building - almost certainly an asbestos garage belonging to the (then presumably still inhabited) gardener's house - is depicted as upstanding. And in common with the Bothy (P) and the kennels, the ranges E and F had been reduced to 'Ruin'. Such other significant structures as are then shown in outline must also have been ruined, though presumably to a greater degree of dereliction.<br> <br>The Present Day<br>Over forty years on, despite its decrepitude, the importance of the surviving estate core to the history of botany and estate horticulture remains undiminished. Although said to be threatened by the theft of stone and by vandalism (M Norman in lit), visits 1998-2000 have offered little evidence of the former, and only limited evidence of the latter, except, that is, for the intrusion of a building contractor's yard into the original entrance area to the west of the gardener's cottage (between February and March 2000) and a great deal of tree damage in the park, from deliberate fire-setting and other physical intervention. <br>An examination of estate records is currently under way by private researchers principally Richard Morris, whose results have kindly been made available to Commission staff  to assist interpretation of the site.<br>The Kitchen Garden Complex<br>For convenience of reference, the structures are identified alphabetically A-Q, more or less working from North to South now marked on an OS 25-inch map extract included in the parish files. Other features are discussed with reference to these major structures,. Some of the features are only present on one of the several Editions of the OS 25-inch plan; some are present on all. Most attempts to explain their origins and uses here rely to considerable degree on information collected by Mr Richard Morris. According to an account of 1886, 'the ground is laid out in convenient quarters for cropping, which are divided by gravel walks. The borders on either side of the principal walks are planted with espalier and pyramidal fruit trees at suitable distances from the walk'. There were 'trees on the walls and a 'good Peach wall ..with a projecting framework of glass under the coping'. At this time ' the different quarters in the garden were cropped systematically, each being filled with vegetables of one kind, the dwarf and choicer sorts by themselves, and the cooler and stronger-growing kinds were treated in like manner, a quarter being devoted to Rhubarb, Artichokes, Asparagus, Peas, &c. The smaller fruits -Gooseberries, Currants, Raspberries and Strawberries - were grown on the same principle'(Morris unpubl, p 30).   <br>A forged steel estate boundary fence of conventional type still encloses much of the interface of the original estate with that of the new houses to the west. Fragments survive running intermittently north from the kennels (Q). The line is broken to accommodate the contractor's site officers (at some time after February 2000). It then runs E by N, passing the stone buildings (E), before changing direction slightly and running slightly W of N to accommodate the walled pond garden, beyond which it joins the stone wall at the extreme NW corner of the compound.  The pond garden area is enclosed by a typical nineteenth century steel forged estate fence punctuated by one gate.<br>The garden wall (S) itself stands up to  4.5 m high. This is seen in section in  drawings 5A and 7. It is built of slab-cleaved Carboniferous sandstone and mortared in a strong lime cement, with a matrix coloured by occasional lumps of coke up to 5mm diam. The enclosure is trapezoidal, the N wall 41.45 m long; the E wall 79 m; the S wall 85 m and the W wall 96 m long. The wall is capped by large local Carboniferous flagstones average about 1-1.3 m long, about 50 cm wide and 10 cm thick.  Two open doorways still punctuate the W wall (both about 1.1m wide). There is also a gateway c 2 m wide accommodating ingress from the Pond Garden path. There are doorways in the NE corner, one on the N wall, the other, now blocked, on the outer, E wall. A further one is situated about 3/5ths the distance S along this wall (1.9m wide), and finally, there is a gateway midway along the S wall 2.2  m wide.<br>The area outside the wall, and even inside it in places, is overrun by rogue trees: mainly sycamore and ash, though with some oak; ivy covers much of the garden wall; brambles in season, rose bay willow herb. In the north eastern corner beyond the major glasshouse are two distinct varieties of tropical grass, some laurel and former cultivars of rhododendron. There is occasional over-mature wysteria. A significant healthy stand of holly and yew runs along the outer side of the West wall. Few exotic plants are in evidence, though around the orchid house site, three camelias survive, two of red, the other pink.<br>Structure A is a free standing hothouse (at SS 6236 9884)[drawings 2, 2a]. Aligned roughly NNW-SSE, it sits alongside a well-made path, about 2 m wide runs to which access is gained from the main N-S track through ascension up two well-made single-piece steps. This is an unusual kind of garden structure of brick and stones 14.7 m long, about 3.7 m wide and  1.7 m high with under-floor heating. In the 1920s it was known as the Heath House (inf W. Edge per R Morris), presumably because it was used to force ericaceous or alpine plants. There is some indication that it originally had a doorway roughly halfway along the north-facing wall, where there is now an in-filled  round-headed arch over. <br>Fundamental to this structure are 10 contiguous flue compartments capped about 0.9 m from the floor; each is separated from the next by brick pillars with slate flags over. These pieces are all well-cut 0.5 m wide and between 0.9 and 1.4 m long. The flue carries hot air from its boiler house dug in on the E end and only about 2 m wide. It is reached down (perhaps) 5 steps (woodland litter and rubble obscure any more accurate account). The air was carried through the flagged flue to the W end of the building, then piped across the brick wall (now very loose) and skywards through a brick-built chimney at the extreme NW corner. The latter is now almost demolished by ivy. A stone bench about 0.7 m wide runs the length of the N wall, about 0.7 m from the ground.   <br>The building was apparently a lean-to affair, though with a relatively shallow fall. This is indicated by the rebate made for roofing timbers in the W end wall. The N wall is capped with flags. <br>B is an enclosed garden centred upon a brick-lined pond [Drawing 2]. In the 1920s this was known as the Rock Garden. The whole garden enclosure has been raised to a level platform above the arterial N-S track, from which it is revetted by a well coursed sandstone slab wall  0.7 m high.   This garden is bounded on the S by a substantial upstanding stone wall 3 m high and about 26m long, the upper part, from  about 2 m high in brick. The whole is capped in a reduced dark-blue ceramic tile ridge, the tiles 0.52m by 0.17 by about 0.12m thick.  Along the inside base of this wall a random collection of stones is piled which suggests a rockery site. This rockery bed is less than 2 m wide. It fronts onto a path about 1.5 m wide running parallel with the wall. Less than halfway from its W end it a right angled junction takes the path around a central brick-lined shallow pond 7 m diam, no more than 0.5 m deep,  in the centre of which was an island  2.3 m diam. entirely of brick. This pond had an edge-set stone berm, possibly for seating aquatic plants. The garden is now overgrown with laurel as well as sycamore and ash, and although the entire is very much overgrown, in its NW corner there is further evidence of a rockery, more of which may survive beneath the heavy woodland litter. The structural integrity of this entire complex is now threatened by extensive root damage. According to Ede the Rock garden was overlooked from the E side of the W garden wall by a Large Fig Tree'.<br>C was 2 lean-to greenhouses and service buildings (at SS 6239 9883)[Drawings 3,3A]. This complex housed 2 massive glasshouses 31 m long, 5.5 m wide, leaning against the South side of a wall 4  m high (and 5.25 m + on the N side). The more westerly of these glasshouses (C1), which housed a number of unusual internal features, was 16 m long. It enclosed four distinct compartments. Against the wall were two benches, the more westerly 6 m long, separated by a gap of about 1 m from a similar bench just over 7 m long. These benches were made of upstanding stone-set steel brackets, 0.75 m high, which located edge-set custom-made dark-blue-black ceramic tiles c 0.9 m by  0.7 m by  5 cm thick.  Offset slightly from these, respectively, is a slightly raised area about 6.5 by 1.5 m, and a sunken area 6.5 by 2.5 m (see section drawing 3A). In the 1920s this was said to have been a stove house producing bananas etc on the western end, and peaches and nectarines at the east end. It is certainly possible to see the two outer parts of the floorspace as areas which might have accommodated this kind of produce.<br>The second greenhouse (C2), is 15 m long, with a narrow contiguous wall-set bed and a discernible access path running alongside. This house was originally given over to Burgundy grapes. By the 'twenties it grew 'yellow muscat grapes' and 'black grapes'. <br>According to Ede, during the 'twenties there were frames to the S and in front of these glasshouses, of which no trace now remains.<br>These glasshouses were served by an important group of six associated ground-based and underground structures, mainly given over to heating and storage (C3-6). All are placed on the N outside and against a spinal wall rising to 4 m on the S and over 5 m where the buildings are excavated down on the N. Aside from occasional punctuations for cast heating pipes, the glasshouses lack interconnection from these ancillary buildings. <br>Describing the ancillary buildings from E-W: C3 is a double-fenestrated room 4.5 m long and 2.6 m wide internally entered through the W wall, apparently without any heating, described by Ede as a potting shed in the '20s. Adjacent, in a subterranean position, is a boiler room 2.5 m wide internally and about 3 m wide. The remains of a substantial cast iron boiler are built up against the spinal wall, its cast iron outlet passing into the wall above en route for Burgundy grape house.  This room is open on the outside (N), presumably for the importation of coal. <br>C4 is a room lit by one window and about 5.4m by 2.5 m internally. This was presumably also for storage; it could have served for fuel, root or dried produce. It is connected to a further room (to the W), this one about 5.6 m long, but which was probably lit by a smaller window. It was also heated by a fire placed close to its juncture with the main wall.<br>C5 is the massive cellared boilerhouse which at one time served the western glasshouse. The heating apparatus are reached down 7 steps about 3 m long which lead beneath a brick span arch of 2.5 m width and 1.25 m height beneath C6. This final W room is irregularly shaped and about 6-7 m long and well-fenestrated (2 openings). It was described as a store in the 1920s.       <br>D is the orchid house (at SS 6242 9881). An orchid house was first built on this site in 1835, but it was later re-designed in 1843 to accommodate a more natural habitat. It was planned and dug at one stage by Richard Morris and is described in a survey by Michael Powell of Rees Richards and Partners. Its measurements were 9 m long, plus 2.25 m for the boiler and pit room, 8.4 m wide and 4 m by 2.25 m. Water was brought to the house from a pond on the Home Farm at a higher level than the house and cascaded into the central basin down a built-up arrangement of firmly-bedded flat cut rocks from the Penllergare quarry, some 3.45m high. The pipe carrying the water to the top of the cascade passed with a single coil through the boiler, so that the resulting spray was warm, and therefore did not lower the house temperature. There were also hot pipes running round the inside perimeter of the house (Yearsley and Morris, 24-5). Apparently the house was later used for raising Camelias (idem p 26) which would explain why 3 Camelia trees still survive there.  <br>E is a stone-built bothy range (at SS 6234 9874)[Drawings 4,4A], probably originally intended for housing junior gardeners, but most likely used in tandem as a storage facility. This fronts the N-S arterial trackway just inside the W estate boundary, only shaded from the fields and a view of the Home Farm by a single stand of hardwoods - principally sycamore. Beyond this range and immediately to the NW of the track, standing outside the SW entrance was a ty-bach.  Beneath the trees inbetween, there are semi-contiguous heaps of broken glass, mainly greenhouse glass but also with broken bottles. <br>Because its form is now so badly truncated, it is unclear exactly what form this building originally took (P 2000/0063/ 08,13). However, the ground floor plan is clear. On the E side, fronting into the frames yard were four rooms (E1-4), from N-S the 1st,2nd, and 4th opened into the yard (P 2000/0063/08). The first of these, a trapezoidal affair 4 m (3 m on the shorter side) by 3 m which is set against the extreme SW garden wall (E1)(P 2000/0063/12-13). This has a small brick-lined fireplace set in the N crotch against the garden wall. The third (E3), also accessed throughout the spinal wall, also possesses a neat brick-lined fireplace (P 2000/0063/09). According to Ede E1-3 were 'Bothy for young gardeners'.. As his plan is schematic, it is possible that he also intended room E5 as gardeners' accommodation. <br> <br>The western, outer group of rooms comprised a more northernly, squarish, room 4 m by 3 m internally with a fireplace and circular brick-lined ?bread oven (E5) (P 2000/0063/10). This was certainly a bakehouse and may also have served as a kitchen, though that function may be better attributed to the room immediately adjacent and accessed through the spinal wall (room E2). This is also accessible from E3. The fourth room (E4) is 5 m by 3 m internally, and also had a fireplace, though this one was set in the extreme SW. Ede suggests that by the 'twenties this was a 'Fruit Room'. However, so close to the original garden entrance, it is possible this could have been intended as an office. A clear understanding of its function and history may be dependent upon the degree of access it enjoyed into the 'Main Entrance Yard'. <br>The final outer (W) full room (E6) was 7.3 m long by 2 m wide. It was fenestrated by 2 narrow slits 0.6 m wide and was accessed externally through an open, pillared structure (E7) over 5 m long by 2 m wide. The two pillars are 70 cm by 45 cm. It was  probably a barrow or cart-shelter (P 2000/0063/14). According to Ede, it was a 'Store Shop'. <br>F-J is the frame yard and pineapple house (centred on SS 6236 9874)[Drawings 5, 5A]. Only the remains of two large buildings can now be recognised in this slightly sunken area: one indubitably a hothouse, and in its present form, the other only a greenhouse. These area the last structures to occupy an area where as many as 5 E-W aligned houses or frames of the same length at one time stood. The more northerly and larger of them (F) the hothouse, is 15 m long by 5.5 m probably now covers the site of the original pineapple house. But as its plan approximates to that on the earliest maps, it may incorporate much of the original materials. Attached to the NE corner and aligned with the E pine end of this building, is a substantial boiler house some 7 m by 2 m and     1 m deep.  It is approached down 7 or 8 steps from the W, the substantial remains of the cast boiler remaining in situ against the E wall. A broken heating pipe of  c 12 cm diam still passes through the wall into the greenhouse and there is an exit flue on the NW corner. The most obvious characteristic of this building, however, is the 9 brick-arched recesses surviving in the N wall, which itself still stands up to 1.75 m high. These recesses form part of a discrete division of the building, separated from the rest by a brick wall, and forming a raised platform probably up to 0.8 m above the height of the rest of the house. This could have carried underfloor heating. This entire division is only just over 1 m wide internally, and it was presumably intended to access the horticultural activity - ?pineapples - associated with the arched recesses. <br>It seems possible that this masy have been John Dillwyn Llewelyn's 'Melon Ground.. situated convenient to the forcing and plant houses,...completely shut in and sheltered by high hedges...and [containing] a good many pits and frames for bedding and other plants and plenty of open space for growing and plunging plants during the summer, and for storing hardy plants in winter. It s here that the seedling Rhododendrons, Azaleas, and coniferous plants are grown in boxes until they are sufficiently large to be bedded out in nursery lines'..(Morris unpubl. p 30).<br>The second structure (G) which in its final form may have been a mid-20th Century free-standing commercial greenhouse, at 15 m by 5.7 m, currently covers a similar ground space to F. At present it lacks obvious signs of an independent boiler house so it is possible that both were heated from the same boiler. This building currently presents three elongated divisions, the outer two of which are to some degree sunken (see Drawing 5A). The diverse ways in which brick and cemented sills have been placed upon stone footings; perforated bricks have been set on the outer wall There is evidence of Its site probably incorporates those of the three E-W aligned frames (H,I,J), depicted on the 1875 and 1905 OS maps, which perhaps were all originally heated from this same boiler. <br>Only excavation will now elucidate the history of this complex area.  <br>Glasshouses and frames K,L,M (now destroyed). In 1875 there was a further E-W aligned frame or free-standing greenhouse (K) set alone in a small trapezoidal enclosure appended on the south to the frame yard. It could still be traced in outline in 1999. Later, two further structures were built which are visible on the 1905 OS plan. By far the smaller glasshouse (L) ran N-S along what was to become the boundary between garden and field; bricks building upon the site of the second structure, a much more substantial E-W-aligned feature (M) was still visible in outline or in the outlines of a structure or structures which succeeded it,  when CSB visited the site with CRK in summer 1999. By January 2000, however, the contractors had encroached well into this enclosure, apparently bulldozing away most of the remaining footings. It is, however, still possible that the footings of (L) frame do survive under the dense undergrowth which now overruns the area or which has been piled up there by the contractors. <br>T is the garden's central pond. This survives, because one of us stumbled into it and almost broke an ankle. However, it was not possible to measure the feature because it was too overgrown.<br>N is the gardener's house (SS 6238 9870)[Drawing 6]. This was approached from the west by a footpath running to the south of a well-coursed sandstone slab wall, now in poor condition. The presence of iron lead-set footings in the stretch surviving west of the house demonstrates that at one stage it had carried iron railings, possibly decorative in nature. Beyond the house and to the east, both wall and path have disappeared. According to Edge this was the head gardener's house, immediately to the west of  which during the 1920s were artichoke and rhubarb patches immediately inside what had by then become the main entrance yard. At some stage, probably since about 1950, the enclosure around this house has been diminished. This is particularly the case on the E side, where indications of a low stone railing-capped wall remain, and the large stones from an entrance gate have been re-arranged close to the SE end corner of the house. At one time the front door on this elevation must have looked out towards the parkland over what is now a field, but which was originally a fruit garden. <br>It is difficult to unravel the structural history of the building. The place is unroofed, though remnants of a collapsed pine A-frame can be recognised, probably having carried an E-W pitching across the S part of the building. Originally, this may have been a single-pile dwelling aligned E-W, and c 7 by 4 m internally. This may have been extended from the NW corner by adding 3 m N-S by 5 m width, to create an angular plan. At a much later point in its history, a privy/coal store (1.7 m by 1.0m)  and entrance scullery (3 m by 1.7m) were added on the W. The main pile is built on a plinth and externally it is faced in a cement render. This is worked and incised in imitation of the sort of  rustication normally achieved by Coade stone keystones and quoins around the fenestration and door. Trouble has been taken to add a sense of quality to the rendering which also includes a complex moulding near the back door. The window cillstones are heavily tooled in a geometric decorative style with edges cut at right angles infilled by squarish hammered alignments.<br>Downstairs, from the indications of partitions, fireplaces and fenestration, four main rooms can be postulated. On the N, a kitchen with a stairwell and hall, and on the longer alignment a living room and parlour. It is possible to make out the site of the upstairs bathroom and toilet on the western wall; the others were presumably all bedrooms.<br>O is a lean-to glasshouse on the outside of the extreme south garden wall. This is c 14 m    m long and about 4.5 m wide. Its outline in length is traceable only with great difficulty and any remnant of an outer wall, which might give an exact width is now obscured under mud and vegetation. According to Ede this was a Late Vinery..heated by flue to chimney. His sketch seems to suggest that the flue was (perhaps unusually) on the outer SE corner.  <br>Almost immediately adjacent to the E of this glasshouse there was originally an out-turned gate,  only about 2.2 m wide which at the time of investigation was completely blocked up with vegetation.<br>Beyond this gate in an easterly direction and entirely covered in ivy, are the remains of peach frame, still attached to and overhanging from the outer wall. Interestingly, Ede records peach trees in this locality during the 1920s, though he does not recall the existence of any covering structure and it is mentioned in the description of 1886..  <br>P is a gothic cottage (at SS 6245 9875)[Drawing 7]. Aligned roughly N-S with the outside SE corner of the eastern garden wall, from which it is appended with an overlap of about 2-3 m, almost as if as an afterthought. It overlooks the valley garden. This was probably a gardener's cottage, and could have been the first such residence on the site. According to Ede, by the 'twenties it was a vegetable store. The interior is 9 m by 2.6 m. Its roof leaned against the wall at a point where it is 3.8 m high, and its inside wall was punctuated by a now-blocked Gothick arched window, while beyond the inner wall, where the garden wall appears to have taken up a slightly different alignment from the original, there was a Gothick doorway, also now blocked up. The place seems to have been heated by a fire in the NW corner, where the wall carries some indications of a flue. Traces of 3 pine joists survive about 1.8 m apart, at about 2.3 m above floor level. Some 8-10 m to the south, also on the outside of the wall, is a simple L-shaped privy.  <br>Q is the site of a kennels which is now completely lost. This is shown as a ruin on the 1959 25 inch plan, so was probably built of wood or stone. At that time there was still one upstanding unit on the NW corner.<br>Photographs<br>by GAW (already on card)<br>1-6. 2000/0063, 08-14 all of Bothy-storage shed area overlooking frames yard.<br>by CSB<br>990146:00A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Tunnel Brick from frames yard area.<br>990146:0A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: End finial from ? post-War asbestos garage roof ridge.<br>990146:XA and 1A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden Ceramic flue brick from structure G.<br> <br>990146:2A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: upstanding foundations of domestic or commercial glasshouse (Structure M)<br> <br>990146:3A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Arched brick recesses in pineapple house (Structure F) looking from South, with sunken feature, part of structure (Structure G) in foreground.<br>990146:4A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Similar view to 3A, but giving clearer impression of how well-coursed stone has been laid on a rubble base (Structure F).<br>990146:5A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Lead base for steel fence set into sandstone capping along boundary wall between S frames yard and Gardener's enclosure.<br>990146:6A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Looking into corner of S frames yard with Bothy E to right with boundary wall of Gardener's enclosure to left.<br> <br>990146:7A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: Looking E along S internal access path.<br>990146:8A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: most S wall showing brick over coursed stone and mortar element with cement capping. <br>990146:9A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: E-W edging related to internal arrangements of ?2nd phase greenhouse.<br>990146:10A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: S wall looking W and showing upper coursing with flag-like stone resting on lower part (?earlier phase).<br>990146:11A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: looking W along central part building with brick alcoves on L.<br>990146:12A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: looking into boiler room from E.<br>990146:13A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: Looking S along inner wall, showing multiple collapses.<br>990146:14A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: looking N at fireplace in central room on E of building. <br>990146:15A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: showing nature of fragmenting wall mortar in central wall. <br>990146:16A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: looking S into fireplace in NW outer room and showing some plaster on lower part of wall. <br>990146:17A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. The more S of the doorways through W kitchen garden wall.<br>990146:18A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking W through collapse in extreme E end of rear ancillary structure. <br>990146:19A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: more easterly boiler complex looking E.<br>990146:20A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C:  the wall of the structure  housing the most westerly boiler showing straight joint on E end.  <br>990146:21A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C:  looking into most westerly boiler house giving an impression of depth and indicating footings of wall which capped the span.<br>990146:22A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking into most westerly boiler house towards infilled doorway seen from other side in 27A. <br>990146:23A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: collapse in N garden wall from inside showing Rhododendron sp intruding through gap<br>990146:24A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: looking into boiler house of structure A with flag flue cover behind. Taken prior to pre-survey vegetation clearance.<br>990146:25A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: site of unidentified collapse in outer wall.<br>990146:26A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: blue ceramic capping tile from<br>S wall of Structure B overlooking pond garden. 15 cm length pen for scale.<br>990146:27A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: W end of structure C, showing quoined glasshouse spine wall to R with blocked entrance to boiler room to L. <br>990146:28A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: looking from SW garden entrance towards"}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': {'16': {'value': {'11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '<p>This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Glamorgan XIV, sheet 11 (1898). Its main elements on that map include lake, observatory, kennels, woodland with vista paths, woodland, walled garden, well, summerhouse (possible), sunday school, parkland, pheasantry, lodges, greenhouses, possible formal garden, conservatory, carriage drive, relict hedgelines and orchard. C.H. Nicholas, RCAHMW, 17th August 2006.</p>\n\n<p>Preliminary Notes to accompany a survey of the kitchen garden at Penllergare*, Swansea<br />\nSurvey undertaken by C.S.Briggs and G.A.Ward for RCAHMW January-August 2000<br />\nIntroduction<br />\nPenllergare is one of the most extensive and distinguished Parkland and garden sites in Wales dating from the earlier nineteenth century. It was at one time the home of the Dillwyn Llewelyns, where photographic work was undertaken. More importantly for the present study, its garden environs were where scientific work developed on orchids and rhododendrons. The family residence and ancillary buildings were demolished in the 1950s and the M4 now runs alongside the N boundary of the site. They included an important conservatory and the garden to an Observatory, the structure of which has survived. The house site, with important trees and some buildings including the observatory, now accommodates council offices.<br />\n*Whereas the name Penllergaer now attaches to the local village, this account employs the earlier form of the name, still associated with the historic estate.<br />\n<br />\nThe parkland (not dealt with here) demands a much fuller examination than has been afforded hitherto, in order to record any remaining neglected features before these become subsumed in recreational facilities for nearby residential housing. It seems particularly important to note the palaeoecological potential of the lake sediments for detecting lost exotic floral species. Beyond the kitchen garden complex, original parkland and ancillary farms was slowly overtaken during the earlier twentieth century, and more recently was heavily built over 1990-1998. In spite of this, many original paths, steps, seats, a summer house site and some specimen trees have survived. In 1991 the WHGT commissioned a report on the parkland, and the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (H Owen-John) made important representations to the local authority in order to secure proper investigation and preservation of parkland features. Current attempts to set up a Friends&#39; group (by M Norman) have resulted in the publication of a book, and seem to have stimulated ongoing interest with the new Swansea Authority.<br />\nEven in its present derelict state, Penllergare&#39;s kitchen garden complex is one of the most extensive surviving in Wales outside a major stately home. Its original range of forcing houses must still rate it amongst the most comprehensive group ever built in Britain. Of particular interest to the history of botany, is the orchid house, a building at the leading edge of orchid cultivation from c 1835 and continuing during the middle decades of the nineteenth century.<br />\nEde&#39;s Plan<br />\nA key aid to the interpretation of the garden&#39;s remaining structures is the sketch plan drawn from memory and recently provided to Mr Richard Morris by Mr William Ede, who ran a market garden there during the 1920s. Although schematic in places, the sketch offers some solutions which would otherwise not be available. There are one or two key areas where memory did not serve Ede well. First, he was unsure of the relationship between the W wall and the outside N-S track. However, this does not affect his recall that peach, nectarines and large black berries occupied the inside of that W wall. Neither does it matter that he effectively depicts 2 S walls. Most crucial to an appreciation of frame and glasshouse development is his inclusion of 2 otherwise unknown frames outside the more Tropical of the two major glasshouses (Structure C). The area of greatest potential interpretational difficulty relates to the frames and glasshouses F-M, where, the Pineapple House aside, it is unclear how far his &#39;bed of mint&#39; and three &#39;frames&#39; relate to the structure G recognised in this survey, or to the other now lost glass houses formerly within the frames yard (H,I,J) or (K.L,M) which were at one time within the curtilage of the gardener&#39;s cottage (N).<br />\nEde&#39;s plan does show what grew in the garden&#39;s four central quadrants, viz, NW Raspberries, SW gooseberries and espalier pears trained on iron railings with cooking cherries on the wall and a large black cherry tree, with some redcurrents; and the SE quadrant was similar with red and white currents on the N-facing S wall. The E wall carried &#39;Apples Trained&#39; throughout iuts entire length, and the N wall grew peaches.<br />\nEde also provided a useful plan of the orchid house.<br />\n<br />\nPenllergare on the Map<br />\nAlthough well depicted on OS (25-inch) plans until the 1920s, by the time of the 1959 survey several important components of the garden, including the central layout and its pond, the ornamental pond (B) and most of the frame yard had disappeared, presumably because they were overgrown and unsurveyable. At this time, a N-S aligned building - almost certainly an asbestos garage belonging to the (then presumably still inhabited) gardener&#39;s house - is depicted as upstanding. And in common with the Bothy (P) and the kennels, the ranges E and F had been reduced to &#39;Ruin&#39;. Such other significant structures as are then shown in outline must also have been ruined, though presumably to a greater degree of dereliction.<br />\n<br />\nThe Present Day<br />\nOver forty years on, despite its decrepitude, the importance of the surviving estate core to the history of botany and estate horticulture remains undiminished. Although said to be threatened by the theft of stone and by vandalism (M Norman in lit), visits 1998-2000 have offered little evidence of the former, and only limited evidence of the latter, except, that is, for the intrusion of a building contractor&#39;s yard into the original entrance area to the west of the gardener&#39;s cottage (between February and March 2000) and a great deal of tree damage in the park, from deliberate fire-setting and other physical intervention.<br />\nAn examination of estate records is currently under way by private researchers principally Richard Morris, whose results have kindly been made available to Commission staff to assist interpretation of the site.<br />\nThe Kitchen Garden Complex<br />\nFor convenience of reference, the structures are identified alphabetically A-Q, more or less working from North to South now marked on an OS 25-inch map extract included in the parish files. Other features are discussed with reference to these major structures,. Some of the features are only present on one of the several Editions of the OS 25-inch plan; some are present on all. Most attempts to explain their origins and uses here rely to considerable degree on information collected by Mr Richard Morris. According to an account of 1886, &#39;the ground is laid out in convenient quarters for cropping, which are divided by gravel walks. The borders on either side of the principal walks are planted with espalier and pyramidal fruit trees at suitable distances from the walk&#39;. There were &#39;trees on the walls and a &#39;good Peach wall ..with a projecting framework of glass under the coping&#39;. At this time &#39; the different quarters in the garden were cropped systematically, each being filled with vegetables of one kind, the dwarf and choicer sorts by themselves, and the cooler and stronger-growing kinds were treated in like manner, a quarter being devoted to Rhubarb, Artichokes, Asparagus, Peas, &amp;c. The smaller fruits -Gooseberries, Currants, Raspberries and Strawberries - were grown on the same principle&#39;(Morris unpubl, p 30).<br />\nA forged steel estate boundary fence of conventional type still encloses much of the interface of the original estate with that of the new houses to the west. Fragments survive running intermittently north from the kennels (Q). The line is broken to accommodate the contractor&#39;s site officers (at some time after February 2000). It then runs E by N, passing the stone buildings (E), before changing direction slightly and running slightly W of N to accommodate the walled pond garden, beyond which it joins the stone wall at the extreme NW corner of the compound. The pond garden area is enclosed by a typical nineteenth century steel forged estate fence punctuated by one gate.<br />\nThe garden wall (S) itself stands up to 4.5 m high. This is seen in section in drawings 5A and 7. It is built of slab-cleaved Carboniferous sandstone and mortared in a strong lime cement, with a matrix coloured by occasional lumps of coke up to 5mm diam. The enclosure is trapezoidal, the N wall 41.45 m long; the E wall 79 m; the S wall 85 m and the W wall 96 m long. The wall is capped by large local Carboniferous flagstones average about 1-1.3 m long, about 50 cm wide and 10 cm thick. Two open doorways still punctuate the W wall (both about 1.1m wide). There is also a gateway c 2 m wide accommodating ingress from the Pond Garden path. There are doorways in the NE corner, one on the N wall, the other, now blocked, on the outer, E wall. A further one is situated about 3/5ths the distance S along this wall (1.9m wide), and finally, there is a gateway midway along the S wall 2.2 m wide.<br />\nThe area outside the wall, and even inside it in places, is overrun by rogue trees: mainly sycamore and ash, though with some oak; ivy covers much of the garden wall; brambles in season, rose bay willow herb. In the north eastern corner beyond the major glasshouse are two distinct varieties of tropical grass, some laurel and former cultivars of rhododendron. There is occasional over-mature wysteria. A significant healthy stand of holly and yew runs along the outer side of the West wall. Few exotic plants are in evidence, though around the orchid house site, three camelias survive, two of red, the other pink.<br />\nStructure A is a free standing hothouse (at SS 6236 9884)[drawings 2, 2a]. Aligned roughly NNW-SSE, it sits alongside a well-made path, about 2 m wide runs to which access is gained from the main N-S track through ascension up two well-made single-piece steps. This is an unusual kind of garden structure of brick and stones 14.7 m long, about 3.7 m wide and 1.7 m high with under-floor heating. In the 1920s it was known as the Heath House (inf W. Edge per R Morris), presumably because it was used to force ericaceous or alpine plants. There is some indication that it originally had a doorway roughly halfway along the north-facing wall, where there is now an in-filled round-headed arch over.<br />\nFundamental to this structure are 10 contiguous flue compartments capped about 0.9 m from the floor; each is separated from the next by brick pillars with slate flags over. These pieces are all well-cut 0.5 m wide and between 0.9 and 1.4 m long. The flue carries hot air from its boiler house dug in on the E end and only about 2 m wide. It is reached down (perhaps) 5 steps (woodland litter and rubble obscure any more accurate account). The air was carried through the flagged flue to the W end of the building, then piped across the brick wall (now very loose) and skywards through a brick-built chimney at the extreme NW corner. The latter is now almost demolished by ivy. A stone bench about 0.7 m wide runs the length of the N wall, about 0.7 m from the ground.<br />\nThe building was apparently a lean-to affair, though with a relatively shallow fall. This is indicated by the rebate made for roofing timbers in the W end wall. The N wall is capped with flags.<br />\nB is an enclosed garden centred upon a brick-lined pond [Drawing 2]. In the 1920s this was known as the Rock Garden. The whole garden enclosure has been raised to a level platform above the arterial N-S track, from which it is revetted by a well coursed sandstone slab wall 0.7 m high. This garden is bounded on the S by a substantial upstanding stone wall 3 m high and about 26m long, the upper part, from about 2 m high in brick. The whole is capped in a reduced dark-blue ceramic tile ridge, the tiles 0.52m by 0.17 by about 0.12m thick. Along the inside base of this wall a random collection of stones is piled which suggests a rockery site. This rockery bed is less than 2 m wide. It fronts onto a path about 1.5 m wide running parallel with the wall. Less than halfway from its W end it a right angled junction takes the path around a central brick-lined shallow pond 7 m diam, no more than 0.5 m deep, in the centre of which was an island 2.3 m diam. entirely of brick. This pond had an edge-set stone berm, possibly for seating aquatic plants. The garden is now overgrown with laurel as well as sycamore and ash, and although the entire is very much overgrown, in its NW corner there is further evidence of a rockery, more of which may survive beneath the heavy woodland litter. The structural integrity of this entire complex is now threatened by extensive root damage. According to Ede the Rock garden was overlooked from the E side of the W garden wall by a Large Fig Tree&#39;.<br />\nC was 2 lean-to greenhouses and service buildings (at SS 6239 9883)[Drawings 3,3A]. This complex housed 2 massive glasshouses 31 m long, 5.5 m wide, leaning against the South side of a wall 4 m high (and 5.25 m + on the N side). The more westerly of these glasshouses (C1), which housed a number of unusual internal features, was 16 m long. It enclosed four distinct compartments. Against the wall were two benches, the more westerly 6 m long, separated by a gap of about 1 m from a similar bench just over 7 m long. These benches were made of upstanding stone-set steel brackets, 0.75 m high, which located edge-set custom-made dark-blue-black ceramic tiles c 0.9 m by 0.7 m by 5 cm thick. Offset slightly from these, respectively, is a slightly raised area about 6.5 by 1.5 m, and a sunken area 6.5 by 2.5 m (see section drawing 3A). In the 1920s this was said to have been a stove house producing bananas etc on the western end, and peaches and nectarines at the east end. It is certainly possible to see the two outer parts of the floorspace as areas which might have accommodated this kind of produce.<br />\nThe second greenhouse (C2), is 15 m long, with a narrow contiguous wall-set bed and a discernible access path running alongside. This house was originally given over to Burgundy grapes. By the &#39;twenties it grew &#39;yellow muscat grapes&#39; and &#39;black grapes&#39;.<br />\nAccording to Ede, during the &#39;twenties there were frames to the S and in front of these glasshouses, of which no trace now remains.<br />\nThese glasshouses were served by an important group of six associated ground-based and underground structures, mainly given over to heating and storage (C3-6). All are placed on the N outside and against a spinal wall rising to 4 m on the S and over 5 m where the buildings are excavated down on the N. Aside from occasional punctuations for cast heating pipes, the glasshouses lack interconnection from these ancillary buildings.<br />\nDescribing the ancillary buildings from E-W: C3 is a double-fenestrated room 4.5 m long and 2.6 m wide internally entered through the W wall, apparently without any heating, described by Ede as a potting shed in the &#39;20s. Adjacent, in a subterranean position, is a boiler room 2.5 m wide internally and about 3 m wide. The remains of a substantial cast iron boiler are built up against the spinal wall, its cast iron outlet passing into the wall above en route for Burgundy grape house. This room is open on the outside (N), presumably for the importation of coal.<br />\nC4 is a room lit by one window and about 5.4m by 2.5 m internally. This was presumably also for storage; it could have served for fuel, root or dried produce. It is connected to a further room (to the W), this one about 5.6 m long, but which was probably lit by a smaller window. It was also heated by a fire placed close to its juncture with the main wall.<br />\nC5 is the massive cellared boilerhouse which at one time served the western glasshouse. The heating apparatus are reached down 7 steps about 3 m long which lead beneath a brick span arch of 2.5 m width and 1.25 m height beneath C6. This final W room is irregularly shaped and about 6-7 m long and well-fenestrated (2 openings). It was described as a store in the 1920s.<br />\nD is the orchid house (at SS 6242 9881). An orchid house was first built on this site in 1835, but it was later re-designed in 1843 to accommodate a more natural habitat. It was planned and dug at one stage by Richard Morris and is described in a survey by Michael Powell of Rees Richards and Partners. Its measurements were 9 m long, plus 2.25 m for the boiler and pit room, 8.4 m wide and 4 m by 2.25 m. Water was brought to the house from a pond on the Home Farm at a higher level than the house and cascaded into the central basin down a built-up arrangement of firmly-bedded flat cut rocks from the Penllergare quarry, some 3.45m high. The pipe carrying the water to the top of the cascade passed with a single coil through the boiler, so that the resulting spray was warm, and therefore did not lower the house temperature. There were also hot pipes running round the inside perimeter of the house (Yearsley and Morris, 24-5). Apparently the house was later used for raising Camelias (idem p 26) which would explain why 3 Camelia trees still survive there.<br />\nE is a stone-built bothy range (at SS 6234 9874)[Drawings 4,4A], probably originally intended for housing junior gardeners, but most likely used in tandem as a storage facility. This fronts the N-S arterial trackway just inside the W estate boundary, only shaded from the fields and a view of the Home Farm by a single stand of hardwoods - principally sycamore. Beyond this range and immediately to the NW of the track, standing outside the SW entrance was a ty-bach. Beneath the trees inbetween, there are semi-contiguous heaps of broken glass, mainly greenhouse glass but also with broken bottles.<br />\nBecause its form is now so badly truncated, it is unclear exactly what form this building originally took (P 2000/0063/ 08,13). However, the ground floor plan is clear. On the E side, fronting into the frames yard were four rooms (E1-4), from N-S the 1st,2nd, and 4th opened into the yard (P 2000/0063/08). The first of these, a trapezoidal affair 4 m (3 m on the shorter side) by 3 m which is set against the extreme SW garden wall (E1)(P 2000/0063/12-13). This has a small brick-lined fireplace set in the N crotch against the garden wall. The third (E3), also accessed throughout the spinal wall, also possesses a neat brick-lined fireplace (P 2000/0063/09). According to Ede E1-3 were &#39;Bothy for young gardeners&#39;.. As his plan is schematic, it is possible that he also intended room E5 as gardeners&#39; accommodation.<br />\n<br />\nThe western, outer group of rooms comprised a more northernly, squarish, room 4 m by 3 m internally with a fireplace and circular brick-lined ?bread oven (E5) (P 2000/0063/10). This was certainly a bakehouse and may also have served as a kitchen, though that function may be better attributed to the room immediately adjacent and accessed through the spinal wall (room E2). This is also accessible from E3. The fourth room (E4) is 5 m by 3 m internally, and also had a fireplace, though this one was set in the extreme SW. Ede suggests that by the &#39;twenties this was a &#39;Fruit Room&#39;. However, so close to the original garden entrance, it is possible this could have been intended as an office. A clear understanding of its function and history may be dependent upon the degree of access it enjoyed into the &#39;Main Entrance Yard&#39;.<br />\nThe final outer (W) full room (E6) was 7.3 m long by 2 m wide. It was fenestrated by 2 narrow slits 0.6 m wide and was accessed externally through an open, pillared structure (E7) over 5 m long by 2 m wide. The two pillars are 70 cm by 45 cm. It was probably a barrow or cart-shelter (P 2000/0063/14). According to Ede, it was a &#39;Store Shop&#39;.<br />\nF-J is the frame yard and pineapple house (centred on SS 6236 9874)[Drawings 5, 5A]. Only the remains of two large buildings can now be recognised in this slightly sunken area: one indubitably a hothouse, and in its present form, the other only a greenhouse. These area the last structures to occupy an area where as many as 5 E-W aligned houses or frames of the same length at one time stood. The more northerly and larger of them (F) the hothouse, is 15 m long by 5.5 m probably now covers the site of the original pineapple house. But as its plan approximates to that on the earliest maps, it may incorporate much of the original materials. Attached to the NE corner and aligned with the E pine end of this building, is a substantial boiler house some 7 m by 2 m and 1 m deep. It is approached down 7 or 8 steps from the W, the substantial remains of the cast boiler remaining in situ against the E wall. A broken heating pipe of c 12 cm diam still passes through the wall into the greenhouse and there is an exit flue on the NW corner. The most obvious characteristic of this building, however, is the 9 brick-arched recesses surviving in the N wall, which itself still stands up to 1.75 m high. These recesses form part of a discrete division of the building, separated from the rest by a brick wall, and forming a raised platform probably up to 0.8 m above the height of the rest of the house. This could have carried underfloor heating. This entire division is only just over 1 m wide internally, and it was presumably intended to access the horticultural activity - ?pineapples - associated with the arched recesses.<br />\nIt seems possible that this masy have been John Dillwyn Llewelyn&#39;s &#39;Melon Ground.. situated convenient to the forcing and plant houses,...completely shut in and sheltered by high hedges...and [containing] a good many pits and frames for bedding and other plants and plenty of open space for growing and plunging plants during the summer, and for storing hardy plants in winter. It s here that the seedling Rhododendrons, Azaleas, and coniferous plants are grown in boxes until they are sufficiently large to be bedded out in nursery lines&#39;..(Morris unpubl. p 30).<br />\nThe second structure (G) which in its final form may have been a mid-20th Century free-standing commercial greenhouse, at 15 m by 5.7 m, currently covers a similar ground space to F. At present it lacks obvious signs of an independent boiler house so it is possible that both were heated from the same boiler. This building currently presents three elongated divisions, the outer two of which are to some degree sunken (see Drawing 5A). The diverse ways in which brick and cemented sills have been placed upon stone footings; perforated bricks have been set on the outer wall There is evidence of Its site probably incorporates those of the three E-W aligned frames (H,I,J), depicted on the 1875 and 1905 OS maps, which perhaps were all originally heated from this same boiler.<br />\nOnly excavation will now elucidate the history of this complex area.<br />\nGlasshouses and frames K,L,M (now destroyed). In 1875 there was a further E-W aligned frame or free-standing greenhouse (K) set alone in a small trapezoidal enclosure appended on the south to the frame yard. It could still be traced in outline in 1999. Later, two further structures were built which are visible on the 1905 OS plan. By far the smaller glasshouse (L) ran N-S along what was to become the boundary between garden and field; bricks building upon the site of the second structure, a much more substantial E-W-aligned feature (M) was still visible in outline or in the outlines of a structure or structures which succeeded it, when CSB visited the site with CRK in summer 1999. By January 2000, however, the contractors had encroached well into this enclosure, apparently bulldozing away most of the remaining footings. It is, however, still possible that the footings of (L) frame do survive under the dense undergrowth which now overruns the area or which has been piled up there by the contractors.<br />\nT is the garden&#39;s central pond. This survives, because one of us stumbled into it and almost broke an ankle. However, it was not possible to measure the feature because it was too overgrown.<br />\nN is the gardener&#39;s house (SS 6238 9870)[Drawing 6]. This was approached from the west by a footpath running to the south of a well-coursed sandstone slab wall, now in poor condition. The presence of iron lead-set footings in the stretch surviving west of the house demonstrates that at one stage it had carried iron railings, possibly decorative in nature. Beyond the house and to the east, both wall and path have disappeared. According to Edge this was the head gardener&#39;s house, immediately to the west of which during the 1920s were artichoke and rhubarb patches immediately inside what had by then become the main entrance yard. At some stage, probably since about 1950, the enclosure around this house has been diminished. This is particularly the case on the E side, where indications of a low stone railing-capped wall remain, and the large stones from an entrance gate have been re-arranged close to the SE end corner of the house. At one time the front door on this elevation must have looked out towards the parkland over what is now a field, but which was originally a fruit garden.<br />\nIt is difficult to unravel the structural history of the building. The place is unroofed, though remnants of a collapsed pine A-frame can be recognised, probably having carried an E-W pitching across the S part of the building. Originally, this may have been a single-pile dwelling aligned E-W, and c 7 by 4 m internally. This may have been extended from the NW corner by adding 3 m N-S by 5 m width, to create an angular plan. At a much later point in its history, a privy/coal store (1.7 m by 1.0m) and entrance scullery (3 m by 1.7m) were added on the W. The main pile is built on a plinth and externally it is faced in a cement render. This is worked and incised in imitation of the sort of rustication normally achieved by Coade stone keystones and quoins around the fenestration and door. Trouble has been taken to add a sense of quality to the rendering which also includes a complex moulding near the back door. The window cillstones are heavily tooled in a geometric decorative style with edges cut at right angles infilled by squarish hammered alignments.<br />\nDownstairs, from the indications of partitions, fireplaces and fenestration, four main rooms can be postulated. On the N, a kitchen with a stairwell and hall, and on the longer alignment a living room and parlour. It is possible to make out the site of the upstairs bathroom and toilet on the western wall; the others were presumably all bedrooms.<br />\nO is a lean-to glasshouse on the outside of the extreme south garden wall. This is c 14 m m long and about 4.5 m wide. Its outline in length is traceable only with great difficulty and any remnant of an outer wall, which might give an exact width is now obscured under mud and vegetation. According to Ede this was a Late Vinery..heated by flue to chimney. His sketch seems to suggest that the flue was (perhaps unusually) on the outer SE corner.<br />\nAlmost immediately adjacent to the E of this glasshouse there was originally an out-turned gate, only about 2.2 m wide which at the time of investigation was completely blocked up with vegetation.<br />\nBeyond this gate in an easterly direction and entirely covered in ivy, are the remains of peach frame, still attached to and overhanging from the outer wall. Interestingly, Ede records peach trees in this locality during the 1920s, though he does not recall the existence of any covering structure and it is mentioned in the description of 1886..<br />\nP is a gothic cottage (at SS 6245 9875)[Drawing 7]. Aligned roughly N-S with the outside SE corner of the eastern garden wall, from which it is appended with an overlap of about 2-3 m, almost as if as an afterthought. It overlooks the valley garden. This was probably a gardener&#39;s cottage, and could have been the first such residence on the site. According to Ede, by the &#39;twenties it was a vegetable store. The interior is 9 m by 2.6 m. Its roof leaned against the wall at a point where it is 3.8 m high, and its inside wall was punctuated by a now-blocked Gothick arched window, while beyond the inner wall, where the garden wall appears to have taken up a slightly different alignment from the original, there was a Gothick doorway, also now blocked up. The place seems to have been heated by a fire in the NW corner, where the wall carries some indications of a flue. Traces of 3 pine joists survive about 1.8 m apart, at about 2.3 m above floor level. Some 8-10 m to the south, also on the outside of the wall, is a simple L-shaped privy.<br />\nQ is the site of a kennels which is now completely lost. This is shown as a ruin on the 1959 25 inch plan, so was probably built of wood or stone. At that time there was still one upstanding unit on the NW corner.<br />\nPhotographs<br />\nby GAW (already on card)<br />\n1-6. 2000/0063, 08-14 all of Bothy-storage shed area overlooking frames yard.<br />\nby CSB<br />\n990146:00A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Tunnel Brick from frames yard area.<br />\n990146:0A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: End finial from ? post-War asbestos garage roof ridge.<br />\n990146:XA and 1A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden Ceramic flue brick from structure G.<br />\n<br />\n990146:2A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: upstanding foundations of domestic or commercial glasshouse (Structure M)<br />\n<br />\n990146:3A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Arched brick recesses in pineapple house (Structure F) looking from South, with sunken feature, part of structure (Structure G) in foreground.<br />\n990146:4A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Similar view to 3A, but giving clearer impression of how well-coursed stone has been laid on a rubble base (Structure F).<br />\n990146:5A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Lead base for steel fence set into sandstone capping along boundary wall between S frames yard and Gardener&#39;s enclosure.<br />\n990146:6A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Looking into corner of S frames yard with Bothy E to right with boundary wall of Gardener&#39;s enclosure to left.<br />\n<br />\n990146:7A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: Looking E along S internal access path.<br />\n990146:8A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: most S wall showing brick over coursed stone and mortar element with cement capping.<br />\n990146:9A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: E-W edging related to internal arrangements of ?2nd phase greenhouse.<br />\n990146:10A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: S wall looking W and showing upper coursing with flag-like stone resting on lower part (?earlier phase).<br />\n990146:11A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: looking W along central part building with brick alcoves on L.<br />\n990146:12A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: looking into boiler room from E.<br />\n990146:13A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: Looking S along inner wall, showing multiple collapses.<br />\n990146:14A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: looking N at fireplace in central room on E of building.<br />\n990146:15A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: showing nature of fragmenting wall mortar in central wall.<br />\n990146:16A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: looking S into fireplace in NW outer room and showing some plaster on lower part of wall.<br />\n990146:17A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. The more S of the doorways through W kitchen garden wall.<br />\n990146:18A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking W through collapse in extreme E end of rear ancillary structure.<br />\n990146:19A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: more easterly boiler complex looking E.<br />\n990146:20A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: the wall of the structure housing the most westerly boiler showing straight joint on E end.<br />\n990146:21A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking into most westerly boiler house giving an impression of depth and indicating footings of wall which capped the span.<br />\n990146:22A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking into most westerly boiler house towards infilled doorway seen from other side in 27A.<br />\n990146:23A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: collapse in N garden wall from inside showing Rhododendron sp intruding through gap<br />\n990146:24A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: looking into boiler house of structure A with flag flue cover behind. Taken prior to pre-survey vegetation clearance.<br />\n990146:25A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: site of unidentified collapse in outer wall.<br />\n990146:26A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: blue ceramic capping tile from<br />\nS wall of Structure B overlooking pond garden. 15 cm length pen for scale.<br />\n990146:27A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: W end of structure C, showing quoined glasshouse spine wall to R with blocked entrance to boiler room to L.<br />\n990146:28A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: looking from SW garden entrance towards</p>\n'}, 'action': 'update', 'status': 'review', 'reviewer': None, 'timestamp': '2022-07-25T09:32:16.894109Z', 'reviewtimestamp': None}}}, {'tileid': '04fa4f49-6fa7-4ed1-806b-7ddd1ccc1c72', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '7e820b1d-61ed-4ac0-8e69-ae5ea7e6d39e', '92cc3b9c-a005-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '54496ff2-4693-4138-ba03-277eff940f11', 'af6d3fd4-a006-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '078fdac7-cb0c-4ab6-b252-0db193d97a9c'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f58-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': '4dcf6c9f-5220-4140-ab05-b22fc4e274d2', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {'60120f5e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': [{'resourceId': '799bd292-9bfa-4262-b9d9-6ccfb64a4d4d', 'ontologyProperty': '', 'resourceXresourceId': 'c407c01e-ab02-4093-985e-e1cf0a086898', 'inverseOntologyProperty': ''}], '60120f75-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'd0444dc5-bec8-4279-bdc6-3e5521e97ee9'}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f5e-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 0, 'provisionaledits': None}, {'tileid': '3060f7b5-e6bf-42dd-95cb-4862e36de194', 'resourceinstance_id': UUID('dfab0055-80c2-4fcd-a6ad-186bfd84e5b0'), 'parenttile_id': None, 'data': {}, 'nodegroup_id': UUID('60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'), 'sortorder': 1, 'provisionaledits': {'16': {'value': {'60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': 'Penllergare Picturesque Landscape, Swansea', '60120f6f-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704': '30307785-4739-466f-9941-f85256fc9919'}, 'action': 'create', 'status': 'review', 'reviewer': None, 'timestamp': '2022-07-25T09:31:20.230401Z', 'reviewtimestamp': None}}}], 'permissions': {'users_without_read_perm': [], 'users_without_edit_perm': [], 'users_without_delete_perm': [], 'users_with_no_access': []}, 'strings': [{'string': 'Penllergaer', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f3d-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Glamorgan', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f61-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Swansea', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f46-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Penllergaer Park, Swansea', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f4c-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Parklands & Gardens of Wales No:PGW.`                            <br>\n1. PENLLERGAER (PENLLERGARE)  Although the house has gone, this site preserves a sizeable amount of a very important  large-scale picturesque and romantic landscape of formerly wooded park and grounds, including lakes and an artificial waterfall of the mid nineteenth century. It was created by John Dillwyn Llewelyn, a nationally important figure in horticulture. The site is unusual in that there are numerous contemporary photographs of it, taken by Llewelyn, who was also a pioneer of photography. Although much of his exotic planting has gone the structure of his landscaping remains, as do the ruins of his pioneering orchideous house in the walled kitchen garden.  The large walled kitchen garden complex, originally hosting a range of up to a dozen hot-houses, includes the ruins of a pioneering orchid house (1835). Otherwise the  main phases of construction were 1833-1882.<br>\nCSB<br>\n<br>\n2. This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Glamorgan XIV, sheet  11 (1898). Its main elements on that map include lake, observatory, kennels, woodland with vista paths, woodland, walled garden, well, summerhouse (possible), sunday school, parkland, pheasantry, lodges, greenhouses, possible formal garden, conservatory, carriage drive, relict hedgelines and orchard.  C.H. Nicholas, RCAHMW, 17th August 2006.<br>\n<br>\nAnd see NPRN 19645', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'English Description', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f43-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'SS6225399045', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f67-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704'}, {'string': 'SS6299', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'SS69NW', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Grid reference taken at centre of site', 'nodegroup_id': '688529e4-9ff5-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'UNK', 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'LM', 'nodegroup_id': '0b5446f8-a00c-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'GARDEN', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No Preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'GARDEN', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'No Preference', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f49-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '10G', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f55-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f55-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'PGW(GM)054(SWA)', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'PGW2', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Cadw', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f52-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Not Applicable', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': 'Earthwork', 'nodegroup_id': '60120f5b-9ff2-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': "Preliminary Notes to accompany a survey of the kitchen garden at Penllergare*, Swansea<br>Survey undertaken by C.S.Briggs and G.A.Ward  for RCAHMW January-August 2000<br>Introduction<br>Penllergare is one of the most extensive and distinguished Parkland and garden sites in Wales dating from the earlier nineteenth century. It was at one time the home of the Dillwyn Llewelyns, where photographic work was undertaken. More importantly for the present study, its garden environs were where scientific work developed on orchids and rhododendrons. The family residence and ancillary buildings were demolished in the 1950s and the M4 now runs alongside the N boundary of the site. They included an important conservatory and the garden to an Observatory, the structure of which has survived. The house site, with important trees and some buildings including the observatory, now accommodates council offices. <br>*Whereas the name Penllergaer now attaches to the local village, this account employs the earlier form of the name, still associated with the historic estate.<br> <br>The parkland (not dealt with here) demands a much fuller examination than has been afforded hitherto, in order to record any remaining neglected features before these become subsumed in recreational facilities for nearby residential housing. It seems particularly important to note the palaeoecological potential of the lake sediments for detecting lost exotic floral species. Beyond the kitchen garden complex, original parkland and ancillary farms was slowly overtaken during the earlier twentieth century, and more recently was heavily built over 1990-1998. In spite of this, many original paths, steps, seats, a summer house site and some specimen trees have survived. In 1991 the WHGT commissioned a report on the parkland, and the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (H Owen-John) made important representations to the local authority in order to secure proper investigation and preservation of parkland features. Current attempts to set up a Friends' group (by M Norman) have resulted in the publication of a book, and seem to have stimulated ongoing interest with the new Swansea Authority.<br>Even in its present derelict state, Penllergare's kitchen garden complex is one of the most extensive surviving in Wales outside a major stately home. Its original range of forcing houses must still rate it amongst the most comprehensive group ever built in Britain. Of particular interest to the history of botany, is the orchid house, a building at the leading edge of orchid cultivation from c 1835 and continuing during the middle decades of the nineteenth century.<br>Ede's Plan<br>A key aid to the interpretation of the garden's remaining structures is the sketch plan drawn from memory and recently provided to Mr Richard Morris by Mr William Ede, who ran a market garden there during the 1920s. Although schematic in places, the sketch offers some solutions which would otherwise not be available. There are one or two key areas where memory did not serve Ede well. First, he was unsure of the relationship between the W wall and the outside N-S track. However, this does not affect his recall that peach, nectarines and large black berries occupied the inside of that W wall. Neither does it matter that he effectively depicts 2 S walls. Most crucial to an appreciation of frame and glasshouse development is his inclusion of 2 otherwise unknown frames outside the more Tropical of the two major glasshouses (Structure C). The area of greatest potential interpretational difficulty relates to the frames and glasshouses F-M, where, the Pineapple House aside,  it is unclear how far his 'bed of mint' and three 'frames' relate to the structure G recognised in this survey, or to the other now lost glass houses formerly within the frames yard (H,I,J) or (K.L,M) which were at one time within the curtilage of the gardener's cottage (N).<br>Ede's plan does show what grew in the garden's four central quadrants, viz, NW Raspberries, SW gooseberries  and espalier pears trained on iron railings with cooking cherries on the wall and a large black cherry tree, with some redcurrents; and the SE quadrant was similar with red and white currents on the N-facing S wall. The E wall carried 'Apples Trained' throughout iuts entire length, and the N wall grew peaches.<br>Ede also provided a useful plan of the orchid house.<br> <br>Penllergare on the Map<br>Although well depicted on OS (25-inch) plans until the 1920s, by the time of the 1959     survey several important components of the garden, including the central layout and its pond, the ornamental pond (B) and most of the frame yard had disappeared, presumably because they were overgrown and unsurveyable. At this time, a N-S aligned building - almost certainly an asbestos garage belonging to the (then presumably still inhabited) gardener's house - is depicted as upstanding. And in common with the Bothy (P) and the kennels, the ranges E and F had been reduced to 'Ruin'. Such other significant structures as are then shown in outline must also have been ruined, though presumably to a greater degree of dereliction.<br> <br>The Present Day<br>Over forty years on, despite its decrepitude, the importance of the surviving estate core to the history of botany and estate horticulture remains undiminished. Although said to be threatened by the theft of stone and by vandalism (M Norman in lit), visits 1998-2000 have offered little evidence of the former, and only limited evidence of the latter, except, that is, for the intrusion of a building contractor's yard into the original entrance area to the west of the gardener's cottage (between February and March 2000) and a great deal of tree damage in the park, from deliberate fire-setting and other physical intervention. <br>An examination of estate records is currently under way by private researchers principally Richard Morris, whose results have kindly been made available to Commission staff  to assist interpretation of the site.<br>The Kitchen Garden Complex<br>For convenience of reference, the structures are identified alphabetically A-Q, more or less working from North to South now marked on an OS 25-inch map extract included in the parish files. Other features are discussed with reference to these major structures,. Some of the features are only present on one of the several Editions of the OS 25-inch plan; some are present on all. Most attempts to explain their origins and uses here rely to considerable degree on information collected by Mr Richard Morris. According to an account of 1886, 'the ground is laid out in convenient quarters for cropping, which are divided by gravel walks. The borders on either side of the principal walks are planted with espalier and pyramidal fruit trees at suitable distances from the walk'. There were 'trees on the walls and a 'good Peach wall ..with a projecting framework of glass under the coping'. At this time ' the different quarters in the garden were cropped systematically, each being filled with vegetables of one kind, the dwarf and choicer sorts by themselves, and the cooler and stronger-growing kinds were treated in like manner, a quarter being devoted to Rhubarb, Artichokes, Asparagus, Peas, &c. The smaller fruits -Gooseberries, Currants, Raspberries and Strawberries - were grown on the same principle'(Morris unpubl, p 30).   <br>A forged steel estate boundary fence of conventional type still encloses much of the interface of the original estate with that of the new houses to the west. Fragments survive running intermittently north from the kennels (Q). The line is broken to accommodate the contractor's site officers (at some time after February 2000). It then runs E by N, passing the stone buildings (E), before changing direction slightly and running slightly W of N to accommodate the walled pond garden, beyond which it joins the stone wall at the extreme NW corner of the compound.  The pond garden area is enclosed by a typical nineteenth century steel forged estate fence punctuated by one gate.<br>The garden wall (S) itself stands up to  4.5 m high. This is seen in section in  drawings 5A and 7. It is built of slab-cleaved Carboniferous sandstone and mortared in a strong lime cement, with a matrix coloured by occasional lumps of coke up to 5mm diam. The enclosure is trapezoidal, the N wall 41.45 m long; the E wall 79 m; the S wall 85 m and the W wall 96 m long. The wall is capped by large local Carboniferous flagstones average about 1-1.3 m long, about 50 cm wide and 10 cm thick.  Two open doorways still punctuate the W wall (both about 1.1m wide). There is also a gateway c 2 m wide accommodating ingress from the Pond Garden path. There are doorways in the NE corner, one on the N wall, the other, now blocked, on the outer, E wall. A further one is situated about 3/5ths the distance S along this wall (1.9m wide), and finally, there is a gateway midway along the S wall 2.2  m wide.<br>The area outside the wall, and even inside it in places, is overrun by rogue trees: mainly sycamore and ash, though with some oak; ivy covers much of the garden wall; brambles in season, rose bay willow herb. In the north eastern corner beyond the major glasshouse are two distinct varieties of tropical grass, some laurel and former cultivars of rhododendron. There is occasional over-mature wysteria. A significant healthy stand of holly and yew runs along the outer side of the West wall. Few exotic plants are in evidence, though around the orchid house site, three camelias survive, two of red, the other pink.<br>Structure A is a free standing hothouse (at SS 6236 9884)[drawings 2, 2a]. Aligned roughly NNW-SSE, it sits alongside a well-made path, about 2 m wide runs to which access is gained from the main N-S track through ascension up two well-made single-piece steps. This is an unusual kind of garden structure of brick and stones 14.7 m long, about 3.7 m wide and  1.7 m high with under-floor heating. In the 1920s it was known as the Heath House (inf W. Edge per R Morris), presumably because it was used to force ericaceous or alpine plants. There is some indication that it originally had a doorway roughly halfway along the north-facing wall, where there is now an in-filled  round-headed arch over. <br>Fundamental to this structure are 10 contiguous flue compartments capped about 0.9 m from the floor; each is separated from the next by brick pillars with slate flags over. These pieces are all well-cut 0.5 m wide and between 0.9 and 1.4 m long. The flue carries hot air from its boiler house dug in on the E end and only about 2 m wide. It is reached down (perhaps) 5 steps (woodland litter and rubble obscure any more accurate account). The air was carried through the flagged flue to the W end of the building, then piped across the brick wall (now very loose) and skywards through a brick-built chimney at the extreme NW corner. The latter is now almost demolished by ivy. A stone bench about 0.7 m wide runs the length of the N wall, about 0.7 m from the ground.   <br>The building was apparently a lean-to affair, though with a relatively shallow fall. This is indicated by the rebate made for roofing timbers in the W end wall. The N wall is capped with flags. <br>B is an enclosed garden centred upon a brick-lined pond [Drawing 2]. In the 1920s this was known as the Rock Garden. The whole garden enclosure has been raised to a level platform above the arterial N-S track, from which it is revetted by a well coursed sandstone slab wall  0.7 m high.   This garden is bounded on the S by a substantial upstanding stone wall 3 m high and about 26m long, the upper part, from  about 2 m high in brick. The whole is capped in a reduced dark-blue ceramic tile ridge, the tiles 0.52m by 0.17 by about 0.12m thick.  Along the inside base of this wall a random collection of stones is piled which suggests a rockery site. This rockery bed is less than 2 m wide. It fronts onto a path about 1.5 m wide running parallel with the wall. Less than halfway from its W end it a right angled junction takes the path around a central brick-lined shallow pond 7 m diam, no more than 0.5 m deep,  in the centre of which was an island  2.3 m diam. entirely of brick. This pond had an edge-set stone berm, possibly for seating aquatic plants. The garden is now overgrown with laurel as well as sycamore and ash, and although the entire is very much overgrown, in its NW corner there is further evidence of a rockery, more of which may survive beneath the heavy woodland litter. The structural integrity of this entire complex is now threatened by extensive root damage. According to Ede the Rock garden was overlooked from the E side of the W garden wall by a Large Fig Tree'.<br>C was 2 lean-to greenhouses and service buildings (at SS 6239 9883)[Drawings 3,3A]. This complex housed 2 massive glasshouses 31 m long, 5.5 m wide, leaning against the South side of a wall 4  m high (and 5.25 m + on the N side). The more westerly of these glasshouses (C1), which housed a number of unusual internal features, was 16 m long. It enclosed four distinct compartments. Against the wall were two benches, the more westerly 6 m long, separated by a gap of about 1 m from a similar bench just over 7 m long. These benches were made of upstanding stone-set steel brackets, 0.75 m high, which located edge-set custom-made dark-blue-black ceramic tiles c 0.9 m by  0.7 m by  5 cm thick.  Offset slightly from these, respectively, is a slightly raised area about 6.5 by 1.5 m, and a sunken area 6.5 by 2.5 m (see section drawing 3A). In the 1920s this was said to have been a stove house producing bananas etc on the western end, and peaches and nectarines at the east end. It is certainly possible to see the two outer parts of the floorspace as areas which might have accommodated this kind of produce.<br>The second greenhouse (C2), is 15 m long, with a narrow contiguous wall-set bed and a discernible access path running alongside. This house was originally given over to Burgundy grapes. By the 'twenties it grew 'yellow muscat grapes' and 'black grapes'. <br>According to Ede, during the 'twenties there were frames to the S and in front of these glasshouses, of which no trace now remains.<br>These glasshouses were served by an important group of six associated ground-based and underground structures, mainly given over to heating and storage (C3-6). All are placed on the N outside and against a spinal wall rising to 4 m on the S and over 5 m where the buildings are excavated down on the N. Aside from occasional punctuations for cast heating pipes, the glasshouses lack interconnection from these ancillary buildings. <br>Describing the ancillary buildings from E-W: C3 is a double-fenestrated room 4.5 m long and 2.6 m wide internally entered through the W wall, apparently without any heating, described by Ede as a potting shed in the '20s. Adjacent, in a subterranean position, is a boiler room 2.5 m wide internally and about 3 m wide. The remains of a substantial cast iron boiler are built up against the spinal wall, its cast iron outlet passing into the wall above en route for Burgundy grape house.  This room is open on the outside (N), presumably for the importation of coal. <br>C4 is a room lit by one window and about 5.4m by 2.5 m internally. This was presumably also for storage; it could have served for fuel, root or dried produce. It is connected to a further room (to the W), this one about 5.6 m long, but which was probably lit by a smaller window. It was also heated by a fire placed close to its juncture with the main wall.<br>C5 is the massive cellared boilerhouse which at one time served the western glasshouse. The heating apparatus are reached down 7 steps about 3 m long which lead beneath a brick span arch of 2.5 m width and 1.25 m height beneath C6. This final W room is irregularly shaped and about 6-7 m long and well-fenestrated (2 openings). It was described as a store in the 1920s.       <br>D is the orchid house (at SS 6242 9881). An orchid house was first built on this site in 1835, but it was later re-designed in 1843 to accommodate a more natural habitat. It was planned and dug at one stage by Richard Morris and is described in a survey by Michael Powell of Rees Richards and Partners. Its measurements were 9 m long, plus 2.25 m for the boiler and pit room, 8.4 m wide and 4 m by 2.25 m. Water was brought to the house from a pond on the Home Farm at a higher level than the house and cascaded into the central basin down a built-up arrangement of firmly-bedded flat cut rocks from the Penllergare quarry, some 3.45m high. The pipe carrying the water to the top of the cascade passed with a single coil through the boiler, so that the resulting spray was warm, and therefore did not lower the house temperature. There were also hot pipes running round the inside perimeter of the house (Yearsley and Morris, 24-5). Apparently the house was later used for raising Camelias (idem p 26) which would explain why 3 Camelia trees still survive there.  <br>E is a stone-built bothy range (at SS 6234 9874)[Drawings 4,4A], probably originally intended for housing junior gardeners, but most likely used in tandem as a storage facility. This fronts the N-S arterial trackway just inside the W estate boundary, only shaded from the fields and a view of the Home Farm by a single stand of hardwoods - principally sycamore. Beyond this range and immediately to the NW of the track, standing outside the SW entrance was a ty-bach.  Beneath the trees inbetween, there are semi-contiguous heaps of broken glass, mainly greenhouse glass but also with broken bottles. <br>Because its form is now so badly truncated, it is unclear exactly what form this building originally took (P 2000/0063/ 08,13). However, the ground floor plan is clear. On the E side, fronting into the frames yard were four rooms (E1-4), from N-S the 1st,2nd, and 4th opened into the yard (P 2000/0063/08). The first of these, a trapezoidal affair 4 m (3 m on the shorter side) by 3 m which is set against the extreme SW garden wall (E1)(P 2000/0063/12-13). This has a small brick-lined fireplace set in the N crotch against the garden wall. The third (E3), also accessed throughout the spinal wall, also possesses a neat brick-lined fireplace (P 2000/0063/09). According to Ede E1-3 were 'Bothy for young gardeners'.. As his plan is schematic, it is possible that he also intended room E5 as gardeners' accommodation. <br> <br>The western, outer group of rooms comprised a more northernly, squarish, room 4 m by 3 m internally with a fireplace and circular brick-lined ?bread oven (E5) (P 2000/0063/10). This was certainly a bakehouse and may also have served as a kitchen, though that function may be better attributed to the room immediately adjacent and accessed through the spinal wall (room E2). This is also accessible from E3. The fourth room (E4) is 5 m by 3 m internally, and also had a fireplace, though this one was set in the extreme SW. Ede suggests that by the 'twenties this was a 'Fruit Room'. However, so close to the original garden entrance, it is possible this could have been intended as an office. A clear understanding of its function and history may be dependent upon the degree of access it enjoyed into the 'Main Entrance Yard'. <br>The final outer (W) full room (E6) was 7.3 m long by 2 m wide. It was fenestrated by 2 narrow slits 0.6 m wide and was accessed externally through an open, pillared structure (E7) over 5 m long by 2 m wide. The two pillars are 70 cm by 45 cm. It was  probably a barrow or cart-shelter (P 2000/0063/14). According to Ede, it was a 'Store Shop'. <br>F-J is the frame yard and pineapple house (centred on SS 6236 9874)[Drawings 5, 5A]. Only the remains of two large buildings can now be recognised in this slightly sunken area: one indubitably a hothouse, and in its present form, the other only a greenhouse. These area the last structures to occupy an area where as many as 5 E-W aligned houses or frames of the same length at one time stood. The more northerly and larger of them (F) the hothouse, is 15 m long by 5.5 m probably now covers the site of the original pineapple house. But as its plan approximates to that on the earliest maps, it may incorporate much of the original materials. Attached to the NE corner and aligned with the E pine end of this building, is a substantial boiler house some 7 m by 2 m and     1 m deep.  It is approached down 7 or 8 steps from the W, the substantial remains of the cast boiler remaining in situ against the E wall. A broken heating pipe of  c 12 cm diam still passes through the wall into the greenhouse and there is an exit flue on the NW corner. The most obvious characteristic of this building, however, is the 9 brick-arched recesses surviving in the N wall, which itself still stands up to 1.75 m high. These recesses form part of a discrete division of the building, separated from the rest by a brick wall, and forming a raised platform probably up to 0.8 m above the height of the rest of the house. This could have carried underfloor heating. This entire division is only just over 1 m wide internally, and it was presumably intended to access the horticultural activity - ?pineapples - associated with the arched recesses. <br>It seems possible that this masy have been John Dillwyn Llewelyn's 'Melon Ground.. situated convenient to the forcing and plant houses,...completely shut in and sheltered by high hedges...and [containing] a good many pits and frames for bedding and other plants and plenty of open space for growing and plunging plants during the summer, and for storing hardy plants in winter. It s here that the seedling Rhododendrons, Azaleas, and coniferous plants are grown in boxes until they are sufficiently large to be bedded out in nursery lines'..(Morris unpubl. p 30).<br>The second structure (G) which in its final form may have been a mid-20th Century free-standing commercial greenhouse, at 15 m by 5.7 m, currently covers a similar ground space to F. At present it lacks obvious signs of an independent boiler house so it is possible that both were heated from the same boiler. This building currently presents three elongated divisions, the outer two of which are to some degree sunken (see Drawing 5A). The diverse ways in which brick and cemented sills have been placed upon stone footings; perforated bricks have been set on the outer wall There is evidence of Its site probably incorporates those of the three E-W aligned frames (H,I,J), depicted on the 1875 and 1905 OS maps, which perhaps were all originally heated from this same boiler. <br>Only excavation will now elucidate the history of this complex area.  <br>Glasshouses and frames K,L,M (now destroyed). In 1875 there was a further E-W aligned frame or free-standing greenhouse (K) set alone in a small trapezoidal enclosure appended on the south to the frame yard. It could still be traced in outline in 1999. Later, two further structures were built which are visible on the 1905 OS plan. By far the smaller glasshouse (L) ran N-S along what was to become the boundary between garden and field; bricks building upon the site of the second structure, a much more substantial E-W-aligned feature (M) was still visible in outline or in the outlines of a structure or structures which succeeded it,  when CSB visited the site with CRK in summer 1999. By January 2000, however, the contractors had encroached well into this enclosure, apparently bulldozing away most of the remaining footings. It is, however, still possible that the footings of (L) frame do survive under the dense undergrowth which now overruns the area or which has been piled up there by the contractors. <br>T is the garden's central pond. This survives, because one of us stumbled into it and almost broke an ankle. However, it was not possible to measure the feature because it was too overgrown.<br>N is the gardener's house (SS 6238 9870)[Drawing 6]. This was approached from the west by a footpath running to the south of a well-coursed sandstone slab wall, now in poor condition. The presence of iron lead-set footings in the stretch surviving west of the house demonstrates that at one stage it had carried iron railings, possibly decorative in nature. Beyond the house and to the east, both wall and path have disappeared. According to Edge this was the head gardener's house, immediately to the west of  which during the 1920s were artichoke and rhubarb patches immediately inside what had by then become the main entrance yard. At some stage, probably since about 1950, the enclosure around this house has been diminished. This is particularly the case on the E side, where indications of a low stone railing-capped wall remain, and the large stones from an entrance gate have been re-arranged close to the SE end corner of the house. At one time the front door on this elevation must have looked out towards the parkland over what is now a field, but which was originally a fruit garden. <br>It is difficult to unravel the structural history of the building. The place is unroofed, though remnants of a collapsed pine A-frame can be recognised, probably having carried an E-W pitching across the S part of the building. Originally, this may have been a single-pile dwelling aligned E-W, and c 7 by 4 m internally. This may have been extended from the NW corner by adding 3 m N-S by 5 m width, to create an angular plan. At a much later point in its history, a privy/coal store (1.7 m by 1.0m)  and entrance scullery (3 m by 1.7m) were added on the W. The main pile is built on a plinth and externally it is faced in a cement render. This is worked and incised in imitation of the sort of  rustication normally achieved by Coade stone keystones and quoins around the fenestration and door. Trouble has been taken to add a sense of quality to the rendering which also includes a complex moulding near the back door. The window cillstones are heavily tooled in a geometric decorative style with edges cut at right angles infilled by squarish hammered alignments.<br>Downstairs, from the indications of partitions, fireplaces and fenestration, four main rooms can be postulated. On the N, a kitchen with a stairwell and hall, and on the longer alignment a living room and parlour. It is possible to make out the site of the upstairs bathroom and toilet on the western wall; the others were presumably all bedrooms.<br>O is a lean-to glasshouse on the outside of the extreme south garden wall. This is c 14 m    m long and about 4.5 m wide. Its outline in length is traceable only with great difficulty and any remnant of an outer wall, which might give an exact width is now obscured under mud and vegetation. According to Ede this was a Late Vinery..heated by flue to chimney. His sketch seems to suggest that the flue was (perhaps unusually) on the outer SE corner.  <br>Almost immediately adjacent to the E of this glasshouse there was originally an out-turned gate,  only about 2.2 m wide which at the time of investigation was completely blocked up with vegetation.<br>Beyond this gate in an easterly direction and entirely covered in ivy, are the remains of peach frame, still attached to and overhanging from the outer wall. Interestingly, Ede records peach trees in this locality during the 1920s, though he does not recall the existence of any covering structure and it is mentioned in the description of 1886..  <br>P is a gothic cottage (at SS 6245 9875)[Drawing 7]. Aligned roughly N-S with the outside SE corner of the eastern garden wall, from which it is appended with an overlap of about 2-3 m, almost as if as an afterthought. It overlooks the valley garden. This was probably a gardener's cottage, and could have been the first such residence on the site. According to Ede, by the 'twenties it was a vegetable store. The interior is 9 m by 2.6 m. Its roof leaned against the wall at a point where it is 3.8 m high, and its inside wall was punctuated by a now-blocked Gothick arched window, while beyond the inner wall, where the garden wall appears to have taken up a slightly different alignment from the original, there was a Gothick doorway, also now blocked up. The place seems to have been heated by a fire in the NW corner, where the wall carries some indications of a flue. Traces of 3 pine joists survive about 1.8 m apart, at about 2.3 m above floor level. Some 8-10 m to the south, also on the outside of the wall, is a simple L-shaped privy.  <br>Q is the site of a kennels which is now completely lost. This is shown as a ruin on the 1959 25 inch plan, so was probably built of wood or stone. At that time there was still one upstanding unit on the NW corner.<br>Photographs<br>by GAW (already on card)<br>1-6. 2000/0063, 08-14 all of Bothy-storage shed area overlooking frames yard.<br>by CSB<br>990146:00A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Tunnel Brick from frames yard area.<br>990146:0A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: End finial from ? post-War asbestos garage roof ridge.<br>990146:XA and 1A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden Ceramic flue brick from structure G.<br> <br>990146:2A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: upstanding foundations of domestic or commercial glasshouse (Structure M)<br> <br>990146:3A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Arched brick recesses in pineapple house (Structure F) looking from South, with sunken feature, part of structure (Structure G) in foreground.<br>990146:4A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Similar view to 3A, but giving clearer impression of how well-coursed stone has been laid on a rubble base (Structure F).<br>990146:5A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Lead base for steel fence set into sandstone capping along boundary wall between S frames yard and Gardener's enclosure.<br>990146:6A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Looking into corner of S frames yard with Bothy E to right with boundary wall of Gardener's enclosure to left.<br> <br>990146:7A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: Looking E along S internal access path.<br>990146:8A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: most S wall showing brick over coursed stone and mortar element with cement capping. <br>990146:9A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: E-W edging related to internal arrangements of ?2nd phase greenhouse.<br>990146:10A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: S wall looking W and showing upper coursing with flag-like stone resting on lower part (?earlier phase).<br>990146:11A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: looking W along central part building with brick alcoves on L.<br>990146:12A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: looking into boiler room from E.<br>990146:13A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: Looking S along inner wall, showing multiple collapses.<br>990146:14A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: looking N at fireplace in central room on E of building. <br>990146:15A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: showing nature of fragmenting wall mortar in central wall. <br>990146:16A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: looking S into fireplace in NW outer room and showing some plaster on lower part of wall. <br>990146:17A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. The more S of the doorways through W kitchen garden wall.<br>990146:18A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking W through collapse in extreme E end of rear ancillary structure. <br>990146:19A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: more easterly boiler complex looking E.<br>990146:20A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C:  the wall of the structure  housing the most westerly boiler showing straight joint on E end.  <br>990146:21A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C:  looking into most westerly boiler house giving an impression of depth and indicating footings of wall which capped the span.<br>990146:22A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking into most westerly boiler house towards infilled doorway seen from other side in 27A. <br>990146:23A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: collapse in N garden wall from inside showing Rhododendron sp intruding through gap<br>990146:24A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: looking into boiler house of structure A with flag flue cover behind. Taken prior to pre-survey vegetation clearance.<br>990146:25A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: site of unidentified collapse in outer wall.<br>990146:26A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: blue ceramic capping tile from<br>S wall of Structure B overlooking pond garden. 15 cm length pen for scale.<br>990146:27A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: W end of structure C, showing quoined glasshouse spine wall to R with blocked entrance to boiler room to L. <br>990146:28A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: looking from SW garden entrance towards", 'nodegroup_id': '11f027e3-a00d-11ea-a530-000d3a86d704', 'provisional': False}, {'string': '<p>This garden is depicted on the Second Edition Ordnance Survey 25-inch map of Glamorgan XIV, sheet 11 (1898). Its main elements on that map include lake, observatory, kennels, woodland with vista paths, woodland, walled garden, well, summerhouse (possible), sunday school, parkland, pheasantry, lodges, greenhouses, possible formal garden, conservatory, carriage drive, relict hedgelines and orchard. C.H. Nicholas, RCAHMW, 17th August 2006.</p>\n\n<p>Preliminary Notes to accompany a survey of the kitchen garden at Penllergare*, Swansea<br />\nSurvey undertaken by C.S.Briggs and G.A.Ward for RCAHMW January-August 2000<br />\nIntroduction<br />\nPenllergare is one of the most extensive and distinguished Parkland and garden sites in Wales dating from the earlier nineteenth century. It was at one time the home of the Dillwyn Llewelyns, where photographic work was undertaken. More importantly for the present study, its garden environs were where scientific work developed on orchids and rhododendrons. The family residence and ancillary buildings were demolished in the 1950s and the M4 now runs alongside the N boundary of the site. They included an important conservatory and the garden to an Observatory, the structure of which has survived. The house site, with important trees and some buildings including the observatory, now accommodates council offices.<br />\n*Whereas the name Penllergaer now attaches to the local village, this account employs the earlier form of the name, still associated with the historic estate.<br />\n<br />\nThe parkland (not dealt with here) demands a much fuller examination than has been afforded hitherto, in order to record any remaining neglected features before these become subsumed in recreational facilities for nearby residential housing. It seems particularly important to note the palaeoecological potential of the lake sediments for detecting lost exotic floral species. Beyond the kitchen garden complex, original parkland and ancillary farms was slowly overtaken during the earlier twentieth century, and more recently was heavily built over 1990-1998. In spite of this, many original paths, steps, seats, a summer house site and some specimen trees have survived. In 1991 the WHGT commissioned a report on the parkland, and the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (H Owen-John) made important representations to the local authority in order to secure proper investigation and preservation of parkland features. Current attempts to set up a Friends&#39; group (by M Norman) have resulted in the publication of a book, and seem to have stimulated ongoing interest with the new Swansea Authority.<br />\nEven in its present derelict state, Penllergare&#39;s kitchen garden complex is one of the most extensive surviving in Wales outside a major stately home. Its original range of forcing houses must still rate it amongst the most comprehensive group ever built in Britain. Of particular interest to the history of botany, is the orchid house, a building at the leading edge of orchid cultivation from c 1835 and continuing during the middle decades of the nineteenth century.<br />\nEde&#39;s Plan<br />\nA key aid to the interpretation of the garden&#39;s remaining structures is the sketch plan drawn from memory and recently provided to Mr Richard Morris by Mr William Ede, who ran a market garden there during the 1920s. Although schematic in places, the sketch offers some solutions which would otherwise not be available. There are one or two key areas where memory did not serve Ede well. First, he was unsure of the relationship between the W wall and the outside N-S track. However, this does not affect his recall that peach, nectarines and large black berries occupied the inside of that W wall. Neither does it matter that he effectively depicts 2 S walls. Most crucial to an appreciation of frame and glasshouse development is his inclusion of 2 otherwise unknown frames outside the more Tropical of the two major glasshouses (Structure C). The area of greatest potential interpretational difficulty relates to the frames and glasshouses F-M, where, the Pineapple House aside, it is unclear how far his &#39;bed of mint&#39; and three &#39;frames&#39; relate to the structure G recognised in this survey, or to the other now lost glass houses formerly within the frames yard (H,I,J) or (K.L,M) which were at one time within the curtilage of the gardener&#39;s cottage (N).<br />\nEde&#39;s plan does show what grew in the garden&#39;s four central quadrants, viz, NW Raspberries, SW gooseberries and espalier pears trained on iron railings with cooking cherries on the wall and a large black cherry tree, with some redcurrents; and the SE quadrant was similar with red and white currents on the N-facing S wall. The E wall carried &#39;Apples Trained&#39; throughout iuts entire length, and the N wall grew peaches.<br />\nEde also provided a useful plan of the orchid house.<br />\n<br />\nPenllergare on the Map<br />\nAlthough well depicted on OS (25-inch) plans until the 1920s, by the time of the 1959 survey several important components of the garden, including the central layout and its pond, the ornamental pond (B) and most of the frame yard had disappeared, presumably because they were overgrown and unsurveyable. At this time, a N-S aligned building - almost certainly an asbestos garage belonging to the (then presumably still inhabited) gardener&#39;s house - is depicted as upstanding. And in common with the Bothy (P) and the kennels, the ranges E and F had been reduced to &#39;Ruin&#39;. Such other significant structures as are then shown in outline must also have been ruined, though presumably to a greater degree of dereliction.<br />\n<br />\nThe Present Day<br />\nOver forty years on, despite its decrepitude, the importance of the surviving estate core to the history of botany and estate horticulture remains undiminished. Although said to be threatened by the theft of stone and by vandalism (M Norman in lit), visits 1998-2000 have offered little evidence of the former, and only limited evidence of the latter, except, that is, for the intrusion of a building contractor&#39;s yard into the original entrance area to the west of the gardener&#39;s cottage (between February and March 2000) and a great deal of tree damage in the park, from deliberate fire-setting and other physical intervention.<br />\nAn examination of estate records is currently under way by private researchers principally Richard Morris, whose results have kindly been made available to Commission staff to assist interpretation of the site.<br />\nThe Kitchen Garden Complex<br />\nFor convenience of reference, the structures are identified alphabetically A-Q, more or less working from North to South now marked on an OS 25-inch map extract included in the parish files. Other features are discussed with reference to these major structures,. Some of the features are only present on one of the several Editions of the OS 25-inch plan; some are present on all. Most attempts to explain their origins and uses here rely to considerable degree on information collected by Mr Richard Morris. According to an account of 1886, &#39;the ground is laid out in convenient quarters for cropping, which are divided by gravel walks. The borders on either side of the principal walks are planted with espalier and pyramidal fruit trees at suitable distances from the walk&#39;. There were &#39;trees on the walls and a &#39;good Peach wall ..with a projecting framework of glass under the coping&#39;. At this time &#39; the different quarters in the garden were cropped systematically, each being filled with vegetables of one kind, the dwarf and choicer sorts by themselves, and the cooler and stronger-growing kinds were treated in like manner, a quarter being devoted to Rhubarb, Artichokes, Asparagus, Peas, &amp;c. The smaller fruits -Gooseberries, Currants, Raspberries and Strawberries - were grown on the same principle&#39;(Morris unpubl, p 30).<br />\nA forged steel estate boundary fence of conventional type still encloses much of the interface of the original estate with that of the new houses to the west. Fragments survive running intermittently north from the kennels (Q). The line is broken to accommodate the contractor&#39;s site officers (at some time after February 2000). It then runs E by N, passing the stone buildings (E), before changing direction slightly and running slightly W of N to accommodate the walled pond garden, beyond which it joins the stone wall at the extreme NW corner of the compound. The pond garden area is enclosed by a typical nineteenth century steel forged estate fence punctuated by one gate.<br />\nThe garden wall (S) itself stands up to 4.5 m high. This is seen in section in drawings 5A and 7. It is built of slab-cleaved Carboniferous sandstone and mortared in a strong lime cement, with a matrix coloured by occasional lumps of coke up to 5mm diam. The enclosure is trapezoidal, the N wall 41.45 m long; the E wall 79 m; the S wall 85 m and the W wall 96 m long. The wall is capped by large local Carboniferous flagstones average about 1-1.3 m long, about 50 cm wide and 10 cm thick. Two open doorways still punctuate the W wall (both about 1.1m wide). There is also a gateway c 2 m wide accommodating ingress from the Pond Garden path. There are doorways in the NE corner, one on the N wall, the other, now blocked, on the outer, E wall. A further one is situated about 3/5ths the distance S along this wall (1.9m wide), and finally, there is a gateway midway along the S wall 2.2 m wide.<br />\nThe area outside the wall, and even inside it in places, is overrun by rogue trees: mainly sycamore and ash, though with some oak; ivy covers much of the garden wall; brambles in season, rose bay willow herb. In the north eastern corner beyond the major glasshouse are two distinct varieties of tropical grass, some laurel and former cultivars of rhododendron. There is occasional over-mature wysteria. A significant healthy stand of holly and yew runs along the outer side of the West wall. Few exotic plants are in evidence, though around the orchid house site, three camelias survive, two of red, the other pink.<br />\nStructure A is a free standing hothouse (at SS 6236 9884)[drawings 2, 2a]. Aligned roughly NNW-SSE, it sits alongside a well-made path, about 2 m wide runs to which access is gained from the main N-S track through ascension up two well-made single-piece steps. This is an unusual kind of garden structure of brick and stones 14.7 m long, about 3.7 m wide and 1.7 m high with under-floor heating. In the 1920s it was known as the Heath House (inf W. Edge per R Morris), presumably because it was used to force ericaceous or alpine plants. There is some indication that it originally had a doorway roughly halfway along the north-facing wall, where there is now an in-filled round-headed arch over.<br />\nFundamental to this structure are 10 contiguous flue compartments capped about 0.9 m from the floor; each is separated from the next by brick pillars with slate flags over. These pieces are all well-cut 0.5 m wide and between 0.9 and 1.4 m long. The flue carries hot air from its boiler house dug in on the E end and only about 2 m wide. It is reached down (perhaps) 5 steps (woodland litter and rubble obscure any more accurate account). The air was carried through the flagged flue to the W end of the building, then piped across the brick wall (now very loose) and skywards through a brick-built chimney at the extreme NW corner. The latter is now almost demolished by ivy. A stone bench about 0.7 m wide runs the length of the N wall, about 0.7 m from the ground.<br />\nThe building was apparently a lean-to affair, though with a relatively shallow fall. This is indicated by the rebate made for roofing timbers in the W end wall. The N wall is capped with flags.<br />\nB is an enclosed garden centred upon a brick-lined pond [Drawing 2]. In the 1920s this was known as the Rock Garden. The whole garden enclosure has been raised to a level platform above the arterial N-S track, from which it is revetted by a well coursed sandstone slab wall 0.7 m high. This garden is bounded on the S by a substantial upstanding stone wall 3 m high and about 26m long, the upper part, from about 2 m high in brick. The whole is capped in a reduced dark-blue ceramic tile ridge, the tiles 0.52m by 0.17 by about 0.12m thick. Along the inside base of this wall a random collection of stones is piled which suggests a rockery site. This rockery bed is less than 2 m wide. It fronts onto a path about 1.5 m wide running parallel with the wall. Less than halfway from its W end it a right angled junction takes the path around a central brick-lined shallow pond 7 m diam, no more than 0.5 m deep, in the centre of which was an island 2.3 m diam. entirely of brick. This pond had an edge-set stone berm, possibly for seating aquatic plants. The garden is now overgrown with laurel as well as sycamore and ash, and although the entire is very much overgrown, in its NW corner there is further evidence of a rockery, more of which may survive beneath the heavy woodland litter. The structural integrity of this entire complex is now threatened by extensive root damage. According to Ede the Rock garden was overlooked from the E side of the W garden wall by a Large Fig Tree&#39;.<br />\nC was 2 lean-to greenhouses and service buildings (at SS 6239 9883)[Drawings 3,3A]. This complex housed 2 massive glasshouses 31 m long, 5.5 m wide, leaning against the South side of a wall 4 m high (and 5.25 m + on the N side). The more westerly of these glasshouses (C1), which housed a number of unusual internal features, was 16 m long. It enclosed four distinct compartments. Against the wall were two benches, the more westerly 6 m long, separated by a gap of about 1 m from a similar bench just over 7 m long. These benches were made of upstanding stone-set steel brackets, 0.75 m high, which located edge-set custom-made dark-blue-black ceramic tiles c 0.9 m by 0.7 m by 5 cm thick. Offset slightly from these, respectively, is a slightly raised area about 6.5 by 1.5 m, and a sunken area 6.5 by 2.5 m (see section drawing 3A). In the 1920s this was said to have been a stove house producing bananas etc on the western end, and peaches and nectarines at the east end. It is certainly possible to see the two outer parts of the floorspace as areas which might have accommodated this kind of produce.<br />\nThe second greenhouse (C2), is 15 m long, with a narrow contiguous wall-set bed and a discernible access path running alongside. This house was originally given over to Burgundy grapes. By the &#39;twenties it grew &#39;yellow muscat grapes&#39; and &#39;black grapes&#39;.<br />\nAccording to Ede, during the &#39;twenties there were frames to the S and in front of these glasshouses, of which no trace now remains.<br />\nThese glasshouses were served by an important group of six associated ground-based and underground structures, mainly given over to heating and storage (C3-6). All are placed on the N outside and against a spinal wall rising to 4 m on the S and over 5 m where the buildings are excavated down on the N. Aside from occasional punctuations for cast heating pipes, the glasshouses lack interconnection from these ancillary buildings.<br />\nDescribing the ancillary buildings from E-W: C3 is a double-fenestrated room 4.5 m long and 2.6 m wide internally entered through the W wall, apparently without any heating, described by Ede as a potting shed in the &#39;20s. Adjacent, in a subterranean position, is a boiler room 2.5 m wide internally and about 3 m wide. The remains of a substantial cast iron boiler are built up against the spinal wall, its cast iron outlet passing into the wall above en route for Burgundy grape house. This room is open on the outside (N), presumably for the importation of coal.<br />\nC4 is a room lit by one window and about 5.4m by 2.5 m internally. This was presumably also for storage; it could have served for fuel, root or dried produce. It is connected to a further room (to the W), this one about 5.6 m long, but which was probably lit by a smaller window. It was also heated by a fire placed close to its juncture with the main wall.<br />\nC5 is the massive cellared boilerhouse which at one time served the western glasshouse. The heating apparatus are reached down 7 steps about 3 m long which lead beneath a brick span arch of 2.5 m width and 1.25 m height beneath C6. This final W room is irregularly shaped and about 6-7 m long and well-fenestrated (2 openings). It was described as a store in the 1920s.<br />\nD is the orchid house (at SS 6242 9881). An orchid house was first built on this site in 1835, but it was later re-designed in 1843 to accommodate a more natural habitat. It was planned and dug at one stage by Richard Morris and is described in a survey by Michael Powell of Rees Richards and Partners. Its measurements were 9 m long, plus 2.25 m for the boiler and pit room, 8.4 m wide and 4 m by 2.25 m. Water was brought to the house from a pond on the Home Farm at a higher level than the house and cascaded into the central basin down a built-up arrangement of firmly-bedded flat cut rocks from the Penllergare quarry, some 3.45m high. The pipe carrying the water to the top of the cascade passed with a single coil through the boiler, so that the resulting spray was warm, and therefore did not lower the house temperature. There were also hot pipes running round the inside perimeter of the house (Yearsley and Morris, 24-5). Apparently the house was later used for raising Camelias (idem p 26) which would explain why 3 Camelia trees still survive there.<br />\nE is a stone-built bothy range (at SS 6234 9874)[Drawings 4,4A], probably originally intended for housing junior gardeners, but most likely used in tandem as a storage facility. This fronts the N-S arterial trackway just inside the W estate boundary, only shaded from the fields and a view of the Home Farm by a single stand of hardwoods - principally sycamore. Beyond this range and immediately to the NW of the track, standing outside the SW entrance was a ty-bach. Beneath the trees inbetween, there are semi-contiguous heaps of broken glass, mainly greenhouse glass but also with broken bottles.<br />\nBecause its form is now so badly truncated, it is unclear exactly what form this building originally took (P 2000/0063/ 08,13). However, the ground floor plan is clear. On the E side, fronting into the frames yard were four rooms (E1-4), from N-S the 1st,2nd, and 4th opened into the yard (P 2000/0063/08). The first of these, a trapezoidal affair 4 m (3 m on the shorter side) by 3 m which is set against the extreme SW garden wall (E1)(P 2000/0063/12-13). This has a small brick-lined fireplace set in the N crotch against the garden wall. The third (E3), also accessed throughout the spinal wall, also possesses a neat brick-lined fireplace (P 2000/0063/09). According to Ede E1-3 were &#39;Bothy for young gardeners&#39;.. As his plan is schematic, it is possible that he also intended room E5 as gardeners&#39; accommodation.<br />\n<br />\nThe western, outer group of rooms comprised a more northernly, squarish, room 4 m by 3 m internally with a fireplace and circular brick-lined ?bread oven (E5) (P 2000/0063/10). This was certainly a bakehouse and may also have served as a kitchen, though that function may be better attributed to the room immediately adjacent and accessed through the spinal wall (room E2). This is also accessible from E3. The fourth room (E4) is 5 m by 3 m internally, and also had a fireplace, though this one was set in the extreme SW. Ede suggests that by the &#39;twenties this was a &#39;Fruit Room&#39;. However, so close to the original garden entrance, it is possible this could have been intended as an office. A clear understanding of its function and history may be dependent upon the degree of access it enjoyed into the &#39;Main Entrance Yard&#39;.<br />\nThe final outer (W) full room (E6) was 7.3 m long by 2 m wide. It was fenestrated by 2 narrow slits 0.6 m wide and was accessed externally through an open, pillared structure (E7) over 5 m long by 2 m wide. The two pillars are 70 cm by 45 cm. It was probably a barrow or cart-shelter (P 2000/0063/14). According to Ede, it was a &#39;Store Shop&#39;.<br />\nF-J is the frame yard and pineapple house (centred on SS 6236 9874)[Drawings 5, 5A]. Only the remains of two large buildings can now be recognised in this slightly sunken area: one indubitably a hothouse, and in its present form, the other only a greenhouse. These area the last structures to occupy an area where as many as 5 E-W aligned houses or frames of the same length at one time stood. The more northerly and larger of them (F) the hothouse, is 15 m long by 5.5 m probably now covers the site of the original pineapple house. But as its plan approximates to that on the earliest maps, it may incorporate much of the original materials. Attached to the NE corner and aligned with the E pine end of this building, is a substantial boiler house some 7 m by 2 m and 1 m deep. It is approached down 7 or 8 steps from the W, the substantial remains of the cast boiler remaining in situ against the E wall. A broken heating pipe of c 12 cm diam still passes through the wall into the greenhouse and there is an exit flue on the NW corner. The most obvious characteristic of this building, however, is the 9 brick-arched recesses surviving in the N wall, which itself still stands up to 1.75 m high. These recesses form part of a discrete division of the building, separated from the rest by a brick wall, and forming a raised platform probably up to 0.8 m above the height of the rest of the house. This could have carried underfloor heating. This entire division is only just over 1 m wide internally, and it was presumably intended to access the horticultural activity - ?pineapples - associated with the arched recesses.<br />\nIt seems possible that this masy have been John Dillwyn Llewelyn&#39;s &#39;Melon Ground.. situated convenient to the forcing and plant houses,...completely shut in and sheltered by high hedges...and [containing] a good many pits and frames for bedding and other plants and plenty of open space for growing and plunging plants during the summer, and for storing hardy plants in winter. It s here that the seedling Rhododendrons, Azaleas, and coniferous plants are grown in boxes until they are sufficiently large to be bedded out in nursery lines&#39;..(Morris unpubl. p 30).<br />\nThe second structure (G) which in its final form may have been a mid-20th Century free-standing commercial greenhouse, at 15 m by 5.7 m, currently covers a similar ground space to F. At present it lacks obvious signs of an independent boiler house so it is possible that both were heated from the same boiler. This building currently presents three elongated divisions, the outer two of which are to some degree sunken (see Drawing 5A). The diverse ways in which brick and cemented sills have been placed upon stone footings; perforated bricks have been set on the outer wall There is evidence of Its site probably incorporates those of the three E-W aligned frames (H,I,J), depicted on the 1875 and 1905 OS maps, which perhaps were all originally heated from this same boiler.<br />\nOnly excavation will now elucidate the history of this complex area.<br />\nGlasshouses and frames K,L,M (now destroyed). In 1875 there was a further E-W aligned frame or free-standing greenhouse (K) set alone in a small trapezoidal enclosure appended on the south to the frame yard. It could still be traced in outline in 1999. Later, two further structures were built which are visible on the 1905 OS plan. By far the smaller glasshouse (L) ran N-S along what was to become the boundary between garden and field; bricks building upon the site of the second structure, a much more substantial E-W-aligned feature (M) was still visible in outline or in the outlines of a structure or structures which succeeded it, when CSB visited the site with CRK in summer 1999. By January 2000, however, the contractors had encroached well into this enclosure, apparently bulldozing away most of the remaining footings. It is, however, still possible that the footings of (L) frame do survive under the dense undergrowth which now overruns the area or which has been piled up there by the contractors.<br />\nT is the garden&#39;s central pond. This survives, because one of us stumbled into it and almost broke an ankle. However, it was not possible to measure the feature because it was too overgrown.<br />\nN is the gardener&#39;s house (SS 6238 9870)[Drawing 6]. This was approached from the west by a footpath running to the south of a well-coursed sandstone slab wall, now in poor condition. The presence of iron lead-set footings in the stretch surviving west of the house demonstrates that at one stage it had carried iron railings, possibly decorative in nature. Beyond the house and to the east, both wall and path have disappeared. According to Edge this was the head gardener&#39;s house, immediately to the west of which during the 1920s were artichoke and rhubarb patches immediately inside what had by then become the main entrance yard. At some stage, probably since about 1950, the enclosure around this house has been diminished. This is particularly the case on the E side, where indications of a low stone railing-capped wall remain, and the large stones from an entrance gate have been re-arranged close to the SE end corner of the house. At one time the front door on this elevation must have looked out towards the parkland over what is now a field, but which was originally a fruit garden.<br />\nIt is difficult to unravel the structural history of the building. The place is unroofed, though remnants of a collapsed pine A-frame can be recognised, probably having carried an E-W pitching across the S part of the building. Originally, this may have been a single-pile dwelling aligned E-W, and c 7 by 4 m internally. This may have been extended from the NW corner by adding 3 m N-S by 5 m width, to create an angular plan. At a much later point in its history, a privy/coal store (1.7 m by 1.0m) and entrance scullery (3 m by 1.7m) were added on the W. The main pile is built on a plinth and externally it is faced in a cement render. This is worked and incised in imitation of the sort of rustication normally achieved by Coade stone keystones and quoins around the fenestration and door. Trouble has been taken to add a sense of quality to the rendering which also includes a complex moulding near the back door. The window cillstones are heavily tooled in a geometric decorative style with edges cut at right angles infilled by squarish hammered alignments.<br />\nDownstairs, from the indications of partitions, fireplaces and fenestration, four main rooms can be postulated. On the N, a kitchen with a stairwell and hall, and on the longer alignment a living room and parlour. It is possible to make out the site of the upstairs bathroom and toilet on the western wall; the others were presumably all bedrooms.<br />\nO is a lean-to glasshouse on the outside of the extreme south garden wall. This is c 14 m m long and about 4.5 m wide. Its outline in length is traceable only with great difficulty and any remnant of an outer wall, which might give an exact width is now obscured under mud and vegetation. According to Ede this was a Late Vinery..heated by flue to chimney. His sketch seems to suggest that the flue was (perhaps unusually) on the outer SE corner.<br />\nAlmost immediately adjacent to the E of this glasshouse there was originally an out-turned gate, only about 2.2 m wide which at the time of investigation was completely blocked up with vegetation.<br />\nBeyond this gate in an easterly direction and entirely covered in ivy, are the remains of peach frame, still attached to and overhanging from the outer wall. Interestingly, Ede records peach trees in this locality during the 1920s, though he does not recall the existence of any covering structure and it is mentioned in the description of 1886..<br />\nP is a gothic cottage (at SS 6245 9875)[Drawing 7]. Aligned roughly N-S with the outside SE corner of the eastern garden wall, from which it is appended with an overlap of about 2-3 m, almost as if as an afterthought. It overlooks the valley garden. This was probably a gardener&#39;s cottage, and could have been the first such residence on the site. According to Ede, by the &#39;twenties it was a vegetable store. The interior is 9 m by 2.6 m. Its roof leaned against the wall at a point where it is 3.8 m high, and its inside wall was punctuated by a now-blocked Gothick arched window, while beyond the inner wall, where the garden wall appears to have taken up a slightly different alignment from the original, there was a Gothick doorway, also now blocked up. The place seems to have been heated by a fire in the NW corner, where the wall carries some indications of a flue. Traces of 3 pine joists survive about 1.8 m apart, at about 2.3 m above floor level. Some 8-10 m to the south, also on the outside of the wall, is a simple L-shaped privy.<br />\nQ is the site of a kennels which is now completely lost. This is shown as a ruin on the 1959 25 inch plan, so was probably built of wood or stone. At that time there was still one upstanding unit on the NW corner.<br />\nPhotographs<br />\nby GAW (already on card)<br />\n1-6. 2000/0063, 08-14 all of Bothy-storage shed area overlooking frames yard.<br />\nby CSB<br />\n990146:00A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Tunnel Brick from frames yard area.<br />\n990146:0A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: End finial from ? post-War asbestos garage roof ridge.<br />\n990146:XA and 1A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden Ceramic flue brick from structure G.<br />\n<br />\n990146:2A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: upstanding foundations of domestic or commercial glasshouse (Structure M)<br />\n<br />\n990146:3A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Arched brick recesses in pineapple house (Structure F) looking from South, with sunken feature, part of structure (Structure G) in foreground.<br />\n990146:4A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Similar view to 3A, but giving clearer impression of how well-coursed stone has been laid on a rubble base (Structure F).<br />\n990146:5A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Lead base for steel fence set into sandstone capping along boundary wall between S frames yard and Gardener&#39;s enclosure.<br />\n990146:6A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Looking into corner of S frames yard with Bothy E to right with boundary wall of Gardener&#39;s enclosure to left.<br />\n<br />\n990146:7A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: Looking E along S internal access path.<br />\n990146:8A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: most S wall showing brick over coursed stone and mortar element with cement capping.<br />\n990146:9A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure G: E-W edging related to internal arrangements of ?2nd phase greenhouse.<br />\n990146:10A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: S wall looking W and showing upper coursing with flag-like stone resting on lower part (?earlier phase).<br />\n990146:11A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: looking W along central part building with brick alcoves on L.<br />\n990146:12A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure F: looking into boiler room from E.<br />\n990146:13A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: Looking S along inner wall, showing multiple collapses.<br />\n990146:14A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: looking N at fireplace in central room on E of building.<br />\n990146:15A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: showing nature of fragmenting wall mortar in central wall.<br />\n990146:16A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: Structure E: looking S into fireplace in NW outer room and showing some plaster on lower part of wall.<br />\n990146:17A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. The more S of the doorways through W kitchen garden wall.<br />\n990146:18A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking W through collapse in extreme E end of rear ancillary structure.<br />\n990146:19A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: more easterly boiler complex looking E.<br />\n990146:20A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: the wall of the structure housing the most westerly boiler showing straight joint on E end.<br />\n990146:21A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking into most westerly boiler house giving an impression of depth and indicating footings of wall which capped the span.<br />\n990146:22A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden. Structure C: looking into most westerly boiler house towards infilled doorway seen from other side in 27A.<br />\n990146:23A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: collapse in N garden wall from inside showing Rhododendron sp intruding through gap<br />\n990146:24A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: looking into boiler house of structure A with flag flue cover behind. Taken prior to pre-survey vegetation clearance.<br />\n990146:25A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: site of unidentified collapse in outer wall.<br />\n990146:26A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: blue ceramic capping tile from<br />\nS wall of Structure B overlooking pond garden. 15 cm length pen for scale.<br />\n990146:27A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: W end of structure C, showing quoined glasshouse spine wall to R with blocked entrance to boiler room to L.<br />\n990146:28A. Penllergare Kitchen Garden: looking from SW garden entrance towards</p>\n', 'n